\par \par \uc2 PHOTO: Dan Page\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill is back in court. On Friday the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals ordered the Food and Drug Administration to pause a 2023 regulation allowing the distribution of mifepristone by mail without the need for a doctor's visit. \uc2 Danco Laboratories\uc2  and GenBioPro, which make mifepristone, petitioned the Supreme Court on Saturday to lift the stay. (Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  on Monday put it on hold for a week while the justices consider the drug companies' request.)\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit's order has a sound basis. Mifepristone\u8212\'20\'14not to be confused with the Plan B morning-after pill, which is available over the counter\u8212\'20\'14poses clinical risks and is a potential tool of coercion and abuse. Its distribution without safeguards heightens these dangers.\par \par \uc2 Deregulation of mifepristone began in 2016, when the agency allowed nonphysicians such as nurse practitioners to prescribe it and reduced the number of required in-person medical visits from three to one. In 2021 the \uc2 FDA\uc2  eliminated the requirement for in-person dispensing entirely, a temporary Covid move that was made permanent in 2023. The issue reached the Supreme Court in \uc2 FDA\uc2  v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine (2024), but the justices dismissed that case on grounds that the plaintiffs, pro-life doctors and medical associations, lacked standing to sue. In May 2025, Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  announced that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  would conduct a "complete review" of mifepristone's safety.\par \par \uc2 The medical risk is real. An in-person doctor's visit is the only reliable way to diagnose an ectopic pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14a life-threatening condition in which an embryo implants outside the uterus. Because the symptoms of a chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (abdominal pain and uterine bleeding) can be similar to those of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, a woman who doesn't get an ultrasound or physical exam may mistake an emergency for a normal drug side effect.\par \par \uc2 Another risk arises when a woman doesn't want an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The lack of an in-person dispensing requirement makes it easier for a father who doesn't want his child, or another interested party, to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail and administer them without the mother's knowledge or consent, such as by putting them into her food or drink.\par \par \uc2 Claims of such abuse have appeared in courts across the country. Justin Anthony Banta was indicted by a Tarrant County, Texas, grand jury in February on charges of attempted capital murder. The indictment alleges that in October 2024 Mr. Banta gave cookies containing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing medication to his then-girlfriend with the intent of killing her unborn baby. Investigators say the girlfriend told them that Mr. Banta had previously suggested ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication online, to which she had replied that she wanted to keep the baby. Mr. Banta's lawyers have said he maintains his innocence.\par \par \uc2 Emerson Evans of Illinois faces intentional-homicide charges for allegedly administering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs to his girlfriend without her knowledge or consent last August. She told police she was approximately seven weeks pregnant, and an officer observed what he suspected to be blood and "a human fetus" in the toilet. According to investigators, Mr. Evans bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from a third party. Mr. Evans has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Beyond outright deception, the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's framework can embolden coercive partners. Surgery resident Hassan-James Abbas was indicted in December by a Lucas County, Ohio, grand jury, which accused him of unlawful distribution of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drug and five other felony counts. A formal notice from Ohio's State Medical Board, which suspended Dr. Abbas's medical license, alleges that he used his estranged wife's identity to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs from an out-of-state telemedical provider, then held his pregnant girlfriend down while forcing crushed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills into her mouth. The baby died, according to a civil complaint filed by the former girlfriend. Dr. Abbas has pleaded not guilty and has until next week to respond to the civil complaint. His lawyers said he denies that he administered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills by force. They said there is no evidence the girlfriend was ever pregnant, and they declined to comment on the allegation that Dr. Abbas used his estranged wife's identity.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs in the Fifth Circuit lawsuit are the state of Louisiana and Rosalie Markezich. She alleges that in 2023 her unnamed boyfriend ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills by mail from an out-of-state prescriber and pressured her to take them while they sat in a car, telling her she would "ruin his life" if she refused. Fearing he might physically harm her, she complied. "I felt despair," she said in an interview. She added that she has been seeing a psychiatrist to help her recover from the trauma of losing the baby.\par \par \uc2 Research from the Charlotte Lozier Institute suggests that coercion is common. Sixty percent of surveyed women who had abortions reported facing intense pressure to do so, and 1 in 4 said the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unwanted or coerced. By making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs available without a face-to-face consultation, the FDA handed a potent tool to those who wish to override a woman's will.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the FDA is considering possible downstream effects such as coercion and abuse alongside medical data in its safety review of mifepristone, press secretary Emily Hilliard dodged the question. "The FDA will describe the mifepristone risk factors analyzed through the study after it is complete," she said in an email. The issue is on Commissioner Marty Makary's radar. "I know personally of OB doctors who prefer to insist\u8212\'20\'14even though they have the option to prescribe otherwise\u8212\'20\'14but they choose to insist that mifepristone be taken when necessary in their office as they observe the person taking it," Dr. Makary said during a Senate hearing last year. "I think their concern is that if this drug is in the wrong hands, it could be used for coercion."\par \par \uc2 Pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups say they champion women's rights, yet they promote a policy that endangers women. It is possible to hold divergent views on the legality and morality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while still demanding a return to basic medical standards.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit's order is a necessary corrective to the FDA's failures. The agency should use this pause as an opportunity to reinstate permanently in-person dispensing and physician-led follow-up visits, which are necessary to protect women's health and freedom. In its effort to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more accessible and convenient, the FDA has sacrificed the safety of those it should protect.\par \par \uc2 Ms. McClain is an assistant editorial features editor at the Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020260504em54005pl\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
