\par\par \uc2 Main Street\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Pro-Lifers Push Back on the President; What did Trump mean when he called for 'flexibility' on the Hyde Amendment?\b0\par\par\uc2 By William McGurn \par \uc2 857 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2026\par 22:18 GMT\par \uc2 wsj.com\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 "You have to be a little flexible on Hyde."\par \par \uc2 With those words President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  roiled the Republican Party. He spoke last Tuesday, in the thick of an effort by Republican and Democratic lawmakers to bang out a compromise that would extend ObamaCare subsidies. The president says Republicans need to compromise on the Hyde Amendment to make possible a deal.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump, however, overlooks one fact: Hyde is itself a compromise. First passed by Congress in September 1976, less than four years after Roe v. Wade, the amendment, named after Rep. Henry Hyde of Illinois (1924-2007). didn't challenge the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it did target taxpayer funding. On that uneasy accommodation federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has rested ever since.\par \par \uc2 The original Hyde vote in June 1976 says something about how politics have changed. The House voted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/94-1976/h952"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 199-165}}}\uc2  in favor. Of the 199 aye votes, Democrats supplied 107 and Republicans 92. This was largely a Democratic victory\u8212\'20\'14a point worth recalling amid all the talk about how extreme the Republicans have become on the issue.\par \par \uc2 A half century later, the compromise still stands. Pro-lifers get a ban on their taxpayer dollars being used but not a federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself. Hyde shows that even on ostensibly irreconcilable issues, political compromise doesn't always require surrendering one's principles.\par \par \uc2 For pro-choicers it works the other way. Hyde doesn't touch the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it says those who want one can't demand their fellow citizens who consider it murder pay for it.\par \par \uc2 Both sides cite polls bolstering their position.\par \par \uc2 A few years back, those who wanted Hyde repealed pointed to a poll from Hart Research showing that 62% of voters thought Medicaid should cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In January 2025, a new Marist Poll posed the question more directly: "Do you strongly support, support, oppose, or strongly oppose using tax dollars to pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" It found 57% oppose or strongly oppose such funding. Against this, only 41% of those polled supported or strongly supported using tax dollars.\par \par \uc2 That helps explain why the president's remarks provoked such a pro-life backlash. On Thursday the House passed a bill to extend the ObamaCare subsidies for three years, minus Hyde protections. Pro-life Republicans are fighting to make sure the Senate doesn't follow suit. If Mr. Trump were to sign an extension that didn't include the Hyde protections, he would go into November's midterms\u8212\'20\'14in which he needs every Republican vote\u8212\'20\'14having alienated a key Republican constituency.\par \par \uc2 The Hyde compromise has survived because it roughly reflects the division on public thinking, which is neither fully pro-life or pro-choice. Meanwhile, GOP pro-lifers say Hyde is nonnegotiable.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, that looked to be the White House position too. Last January it put out a press release boasting that it was "enforcing the Hyde amendment." Last week White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt, in response to pro-life concerns, stated categorically that "the president did not change the administration's policy."\par \par \uc2 We'll see. Some fear that Mr. Trump won't be able to resist if a Republican Congress puts a bill on his table that extends ObamaCare subsidies without any Hyde language. Or maybe there will be an accounting gimmick such as the one President Obama used in 2010 to get around his assurance that "no federal dollars will be used to fund abortions."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump occupies a singular spot on this issue. He declares himself "the most pro-life president" in American history, and he has a good argument. In 2016 he campaigned hard on the issue\u8212\'20\'14famously putting \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  on the ropes in their final presidential debate by focusing on her support for late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . His appointees accounted for three of the five Supreme Court votes in the 2022 decision that overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 For pro-lifers and pro-choicers alike, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a conviction issue. For pro-lifers, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nothing less than the deliberate taking of an innocent life.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans' problem today isn't that their position on Hyde is unpopular. It is that it hasn't been tethered to anything higher. How much better off the American people would be today if the Republican Party had been the immovable object in the way of ObamaCare. Meanwhile, Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, warns that Mr. Trump's calls to be "flexible" on Hyde may come back to haunt him in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 "The president and vice president are happy enough to trade Hyde for healthcare votes," Ms. Dannenfelser says. "What Trump and Vance miss is that the trade is a net voter loss in battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 "The power of Hyde is that it is a GOP bedrock and a bipartisan consensus," she says. "Any marginal gain Trump-Vance win by ditching the Hyde amendment will be overshadowed by a net loss of enthusiasm, door-to-door ground game and votes."\par \par \uc2 Write to mcgurn@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020260112em1c006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
