\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Will the Supreme Court Overturn Gay Marriage? Hillary Clinton says yes. Barrett's book tour and much more suggest no.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 591 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2025\par 21:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kim Davis, a former Kentucky clerk who refused to issue same-sex marriage licenses in 2015, recently filed a petition to the Supreme Court, asking to end the constitutional right to gay unions that was handed down in Obergefell v. Hodges. The Justices could decline to hear her appeal, since they get roughly 8,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 Yet Hillary Clinton's advice to gay couples? Tie the knot while you can. "It took 50 years to overturn Roe v. Wade," she told a podcast. "The Supreme Court will hear a case about gay marriage. My prediction is, they will do to gay marriage what they did to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They will send it back to the states." So get hitched now, "because I don't think they'll undo existing marriages, but I fear that they will undo the national right."\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , a cautious swing vote, doesn't sound raring to do it. In an interview on her new book, \uc2 CBS\uc2  quoted her as writing that the Court has held "the rights to marry, engage in sexual intimacy, use birth control, and raise children are fundamental, but the rights to do business, commit suicide and obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not." Justice Barrett said she is only restating doctrine, but asked about Mrs. Clinton's remarks, she sounded dismissive: "I think people who criticize the court or who are outside say a lot of different things."\par \par \uc2 The way that the 5-4 majority in Obergefell (2015) tried to settle a cultural debate, by reading the Constitution to require state recognition of same-sex unions, bears a resemblance to what happened in Roe, the 1973 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling. Yet there are also differences. One is reliance interests. The Justices are hesitant to overturn an erroneous precedent if it has become a load-bearing wall for many Americans.\par \par \uc2 The Court explained this at length in Dobbs (2022), when it changed course and correctly said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't in the Constitution. "Conventional, concrete reliance interests are not present here," Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  said. He quoted the 1992 ruling \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  v. Casey, which upheld the essence of Roe, as saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is generally "unplanned activity," and that people would be able to "take virtually immediate account" of a reversal.\par \par \uc2 As an example of concrete reliance interests, Justice Alito cited "those that develop in 'cases involving property and contract rights.'" Well, the Census Bureau now says "nearly 1.5 million adults lived with a spouse of the same sex in 2022." They own property, share insurance, and have other arrangements that would be hard for courts to unwind, which is why even the partisan Mrs. Clinton can't imagine it.\par \par \uc2 Dobbs shouldn't be read "to cast doubt on" other precedents, Justice Alito wrote, calling that an "unfounded fear." Why? "Rights regarding contraception and same-sex relationships are inherently different," he said, because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "uniquely involves what Roe and Casey termed 'potential life.'"\par \par \uc2 Another difference with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though it isn't a legal point, is that public opinion on gay marriage was moving before Obergefell, and the trend continues. As of last year, 67% of Americans said they supported it, according to surveys by the Public Religion Research Institute. That includes at least 50% in every state and 50% of Republicans. Across the 32 states where gay marriage would be barred without Obergefell? The public supports it 63% to 34%.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250911el9b0076d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
