\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 The Men Suing Over Their Partners' Abortions; The antiabortion movement is encouraging partners and ex-partners who say they were harmed by abortions of their unborn children\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas and Mariah Timms \par \uc2 947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2025\par 14:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The antiabortion movement has found an unlikely new ally to target \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers: husbands, boyfriends and exes.\par \par \uc2 Men have filed several recent lawsuits stemming from abortions obtained by their partners or ex-partners. The strategy is part of an effort by antiabortion lawyers to gain ground in their uphill battle to target mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and interstate travel for the procedure. But it carries risks\u8212\'20\'14putting dysfunctional relationships on display to advance a divisive claim that men are harmed when their intimate partners end pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 "They're finding people who might have an ax to grind against their ex to try to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. These are not savory plaintiffs," said Marc Hearron, a lawyer with the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  who represented a Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activist in one case where a man sued several people over his then-wife's self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The cases, Hearron said, are based on "a wackadoo theory."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents say men suffer harm when their unborn children are aborted and have legitimate legal claims, including for wrongful death, against doctors and others who helped the mother do it.\par \par \uc2 "We're starting to have fathers who feel that they can speak out and speak up for their rights and for the rights of the child," said \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , president of Students for Life of America, a national antiabortion group.\par \par \uc2 The cases principally flow from Texas, which allows a parent to sue for the wrongful death of an unborn child. Many of the suits have been spearheaded by Jonathan F. Mitchell, the former Texas solicitor general who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/behind-texas-abortion-law-an-attorneys-unusual-enforcement-idea-11630762683"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the architect of a Texas law}}}\uc2 , known as SB 8, which allows private litigants to bring civil lawsuits to enforce a prohibition on performing or aiding abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 One man in the Houston area last month filed two lawsuits alleging his girlfriend's estranged husband and her mother forced her to take pills prescribed by a California-based doctor to end two pregnancies. The plaintiff, Jerry Rodriguez, says that his girlfriend was happy to be pregnant by him both times but that her ex-husband ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills for her from an online provider that ships \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs to states with bans, and pressured her to take them.\par \par \uc2 Rodriguez says in the lawsuit that his girlfriend is pregnant again. He is seeking to sue on behalf of "current and future fathers of unborn children" and asking a judge to block the doctor from sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the mail. He is also seeking at least $1 million from the girlfriend's family members. The girlfriend didn't respond to requests for comment. The defendants have yet to respond in court. One declined to comment, and the others didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Many of the cases are still in the early stages, so courts haven't yet tested the plaintiffs' legal or factual claims.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents hope to use the lawsuits to pierce shield laws passed by blue states that protect providers and others within their borders who work with out-of-state patients residing in areas with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Texas Right to Life, an antiabortion organization, has been tapping into its network of crisis pregnancy centers, support groups and conservative circles to find potential plaintiffs, male or female, said John Seago, the group's president. "We have been trying to sound the alarm and recruit people to this fight," he said.\par \par \uc2 Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  has embraced similar tactics. In one case against a New York doctor, the Republican official alleges a 20-year-old woman in northern Texas became pregnant in May 2024 and didn't tell the biological father about the pregnancy or that she had ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from Dr. Margaret Daley Carpenter. When the woman began to hemorrhage, the father took her to the hospital and later discovered the pills, according to the filing.\par \par \uc2 Paxton asked a court to block Carpenter, the co-founder of the organization \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition for Telemedicine, from sending pills to Texas. A Texas judge entered a $100,000 default judgment against her, which officials in New York have refused to enforce, citing the state's shield laws. Carpenter couldn't be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 The cases have been jarring for defendants. Aracely Garcia, a Houston-based \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights organizer who was represented by Hearron in a case brought two years ago, said being named in a lawsuit was confusing and scary.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should partners or ex-partners be able to prevent a woman's decision to end a pregnancy? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "I kept hearing, 'this is the first of its kind,' so there was nothing in the past to look at to see what could be argued in my defense. It was overwhelming," Garcia said. "We didn't go to trial, so I never even got to see the man in person. Part of me wanted to look him in the eye and somehow make him feel ashamed."\par \par \uc2 After a countersuit by some of the defendants, both sides dropped their claims.\par \par \uc2 State antiabortion groups and lawmakers are eyeing other ways to bring more cases. Texas lawmakers are considering a bill that would allow private citizens with no connections to the abortions to bring lawsuits seeking damages starting at $100,000 against providers sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to their state.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com}}}\uc2  and Mariah Timms at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:mariah.timms@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mariah.timms@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250826el8q002ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
