\par\par \uc2 The Saturday Essay\par \uc2 Life\par \b \uc2 What Is Planned Parenthood Fighting For? Facing government pressure and massive budget shortfalls, the group is increasingly torn between its healthcare mission and progressive advocacy.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pamela Paul \par \uc2 2,568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2025\par 15:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Laura Edwards-Leeper co-founded the nation's first pediatric gender clinic. An earlier version of this article incorrectly stated it was the first gender clinic. (Corrected on July 19)\par \par \uc2 To American feminists, the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  brand symbolizes liberation and empowerment. To Medicaid recipients and rural women, it means access to affordable contraceptives, testing and treatment for sexually transmitted infections, and cancer screenings. To conservatives and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it means the devaluation of human life and dissolution of the family.\par \par \uc2 But to many young people encountering \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  today, the organization, founded in 1916 as a grassroots movement to provide family planning to poor women, means something else entirely. When a 3- to 5-year-old asks, "Is that a boy or a girl?" \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , currently the country's leading provider of sex education, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kids-ask.org/scripts/new?script%5Bage_range_id%5D=3&script%5Bscript_type%5D=question&script%5Btopic_id%5D=3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggests}}}\uc2  replying, "Only an individual can define their gender identity. Gender identity is separate from what body parts a person has." (\uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is also now the country's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/uploads/filer_public/db/88/db881467-f6e5-47a3-a6a3-2c7d3ea545e0/gac2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 second largest provider}}}\uc2  of cross-sex hormones for transgender treatments.)\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , where young people are most likely to seek information, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  offers decidedly liberationist advice, including graphic descriptions of sexual techniques. Posts celebrate Pansexual Pride Day and declare that "virginity is a social construct." In keeping with the organization's racial justice agenda, which includes support for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/blog/defunding-the-police-what-it-means-and-why-planned-parenthood-supports-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #DefundthePolice}}}\uc2 , its TikTok account displays a video of a Black woman seemingly fleeing and then laughing, with the tag, "Running from the police, but then they say, suspect is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights baddie."\par \par \uc2 All of these aspects of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  are now under threat, and from all three branches of the federal government. In March, Trump withheld Title X grants, which fund contraceptive, reproductive and sexual health services for poor people, from at least nine \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliates while the administration investigates their compliance with its policies on D.E.I. In June, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-ruling-makes-it-easier-for-states-to-boot-planned-parenthood-clinics-from-medicaid-2d8a54a6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court ruled}}}\uc2  that patients do not have the right to sue states for denying state Medicaid funding to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics, making it easier for more states to withdraw funding. And earlier this month, Congress passed Trump's megabill, which effectively ends federal Medicaid funding for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for the next year. (Trump has pledged not to cut off what he sees as deserving Medicaid recipients but to focus on waste, fraud and abuse.)\par \par \uc2 "We are in a fight for survival," said Rebecca Gibron, CEO of Planned Parenthood of the Greater Northwest, which includes Hawaii, Alaska, Washington, Kentucky, Indiana and Idaho, and is the country's largest affiliate. She said the organization may face a deficit as high as $800 million. "This is a catastrophic situation. So we are doing an analysis of the impact, looking at where we may need to consolidate. It's a moving target."\par \par \uc2 As it faces this potentially dire future, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is also contending with an identity crisis that has been brewing for decades. The trouble stems from its dual and often dueling roles as both a national advocacy organization and a local healthcare provider, one inherently political and the other necessarily nonpartisan. While its roughly 600 clinics offer patient care, the national organization operates as an advocacy group, raising money to support positions that place it firmly on the progressive left in America's culture wars.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  joins a long list of institutions targeted by the administration, including law firms, universities, technology companies and media organizations. Most have responded with appeasement or outright capitulation. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has taken a more combative stance.\par \par \uc2 On July 3, \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , the CEO and president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and its political-action committee, \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 , since 2020, announced that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/uploads/filer_public/11/57/1157c2e7-7500-4f7a-beb1-d2f16d3760ff/1-complaint_07072025.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it would sue}}}\uc2  the Trump administration over the withdrawal of Medicaid funding. "They know we fight for freedom, and they hate us for it," McGill Johnson declared in a video posted on social media. "But that has never stopped us before, and it won't stop us now. President Trump, we'll see you in court."\par \par \uc2 It's a bold approach for an organization that has suffered a recent series of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/planned-parenthood-abortion-trump-supreme-court-a0c54920"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal and political setbacks}}}\uc2 , most notably the Supreme Court's 2022 Dobbs decision overturning Roe v. Wade. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has been in financial trouble for years. According to one former executive, a little over a decade ago, it hired McKinsey to assess its healthcare operations; McKinsey concluded they were unsustainable. Many of its clinics are poorly maintained, understaffed and short on funding.\par \par \uc2 Though the Dobbs ruling led to a surge in fundraising, donations have dropped 15% from a decade high of $428 million in 2022, and most of those funds go toward advocacy rather than patient care. Despite receiving a record $792.2 million in government reimbursements and grants in 2023-24, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has closed several clinics, including its Manhattan clinic, which was losing more than $30 million a year. Its Greater New York affiliate stopped providing abortions after 20 weeks due to a budget shortfall.\par \par \uc2 Trump's policies will only intensify these problems. According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a healthcare policy and advocacy organization, the administration's cuts mean that 834,000 people will lose access to Title X-funded care, a large portion of which would have been delivered by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  estimates that 200 clinics could close, affecting 1.1 million patients, due to the Medicaid cuts alone.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has frequently had to evolve in response to political, medical and legal changes: the arrival of the birth-control pill in 1962, the legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, the passage of the Hyde amendment in 1976 (which restricted the use of federal money for abortions), the murders and bombings of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the '90s, the passage of the Affordable Care Act (which gave many patients alternatives to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 ), the development of medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  alternatives and now the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's mission? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 In 1993, when Pamela Maraldo, a nurse with a Ph.D., succeeded Faye Wattleton, a legendary figure and the first Black woman to lead the organization, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  had just been elected president. With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights considered safe, donations to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  plummeted. Maraldo saw a public health opportunity\u8212\'20\'14the chance to provide more primary healthcare services and treat patients holistically.\par \par \uc2 At the time, less than half of the organization's clinics performed abortions. "Like Clinton, I wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be safe, legal and rare," Maraldo said. "But people at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  didn't like that\u8212\'20\'14they felt it was apologetic. I used to say, 'Why wave red flags in front of a bull? Let's try to raise prevention.'" She says her efforts to adapt and bring more people along were met with strong internal resistance. Only two years into the job, Maraldo resigned after a vote of no confidence from the board.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , which declined requests for an interview, responded by email to Maraldo's comments with the following statement: "\uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is proud to be the nation's leading provider of high-quality, affordable sexual and reproductive healthcare for all people, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as the nation's largest provider of sex education."\par \par \uc2 Maraldo's struggles reflect the challenge of managing \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's federated structure, with 48 independently incorporated affiliates operating under the national organization's umbrella.\par \par \uc2 Gloria Feldt was president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  from 1996 to 2005 during a time of intense violence against clinics, including murders and firebombings, and the organization was deep in red ink. But Feldt says she left because she was "exhausted" by managing an organization in which affiliates were often deeply resentful of the national organization's financial advantage and control.\par \par \uc2 "I could have fought the external politics all day," she said. "But the internal politics were very difficult. The structure makes it very hard to lead a movement, especially in times of political turmoil."\par \par \uc2 What benefits the national organization doesn't always translate into improvements for affiliates. According to a former senior executive at the national office, outrage tends to fuel donations; the more restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the more it benefits the national office. (In its emailed response, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  called this "a gross mischaracterization.") Some former staff members describe the national office as bloated, with too many employees making too much money in undefined roles. (\uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  did not respond to a request for comment.) McGill Johnson received more than $900,000 in compensation, according to the organization's most recent publicly available tax returns.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, many clinics are underfunded and operate at a deficit. Some former executives said the national office's outspoken progressive agenda can make it harder for affiliates to gain the support of local and state governments. Over the past two decades, clinics have closed or left the network. (\uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  would not provide specific numbers.) \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal9600
