\par \par \uc2 During her brief tenure as president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in 2018-19, \uc2 Leana Wen\uc2  tried to focus less on the political battles around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . PHOTO: Rosem Morton for WSJ\par \par \uc2 Under Cecile Richards, president between 2006 and 2018, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights moved front and center; new bylaws established that any clinic that did not want to provide abortions had to leave the network. When \uc2 Leana Wen\uc2 , the former health commissioner of Baltimore, was briefly president following Richards's departure, her efforts to focus less on the political battles around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to broaden services into primary-care issues affecting poor women, such as asthma, were characterized by her critics as "mission creep." Wen declined to comment, but in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@Dr.LeanaWen/open-letter-to-the-planned-parenthood-family-9560d7c35af7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Medium pos}}}\uc2 t at the time, she cited "philosophical differences about the direction and future of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 In the years since Wen was forced out, a different kind of mission creep set in, with the organization tethering itself to causes like democracy reform (including support for expanding the Supreme Court and ending the filibuster in the Senate) and gun control\u8212\'20\'14actions that have alienated some donors, according to former employees. These moves reflect the political motivations of its workforce, increasingly populated by what some employees refer to as social justice warriors\u8212\'20\'14young people who come to the organization for its progressive values more than for its provision of healthcare.\par \par \uc2 A self-described "champion for social and racial justice," McGill Johnson shares this vision. In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/17/opinion/planned-parenthood-margaret-sanger.html?utm_source=twitter&utm_medium=tweet&utm_campaign=alexistw&utm_content=nyt-april21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2021 op-ed}}}\uc2 , she accused \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  of focusing too much on "women's health" and "privileging whiteness." As she wrote, "What we don't want to be, as an organization, is a Karen. You know Karen: She escalates small confrontations because of her own racial anxiety. She calls the manager. She calls the police. She stands with other white parents to maintain school segregation."\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  wants to be the head of the anti-Trump resistance in all its forms, according to one former senior executive at the national office. The question, she said, is who are they alienating in the process?\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  no longer positions itself as the leading healthcare provider for women and has largely stopped referring to women on its website and in policy statements. The only mention of "women" among its promotional items are T-shirts emblazoned with "Stand with Black Women." In testimony before Congress, Dr. Bhavik Kumar, then a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  medical director and now chief medical officer at the Greater Ohio affiliate, said that "men can have pregnancies, especially transmen."\par \par \uc2 The organization's pervasive language around "pregnant people" is intended to be inclusive of transgender people, a cause that the organization connects to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights under the umbrella of "bodily autonomy." As \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  put it on Threads, "trans and nonbinary people are essential to the movement for sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rights\u8212\'20\'14the fight for trans rights is our fight."\par \par \uc2 Not everyone agrees this is the best approach for a movement founded to empower women. "I don't understand the national office's thinking in not allowing anyone to talk about women's health anymore," said Maraldo. "These really, really left-wing ideological postures are to me just as off-putting as they are on the right when they're counter to basic Americans' common sense."\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has also rapidly expanded its services into one of the most contested and politicized areas of healthcare, gender transitions. Its national office does not reveal numbers on these services, instead grouping them into an "other services" category in its annual report. In 2019, that category included 17,791 cases. It rose to 77,858 in 2023. With trans-identified minors, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  follows an "informed consent" model, which, according to its patient guidelines, enables patients to get a same-day prescription for cross-sex hormones after a 30-minute in-person or remote consultation with a staff member. No professional diagnosis is required.\par \par \uc2 More moderate voices on gender medicine like Laura Edwards-Leeper, who co-founded the nation's first pediatric gender clinic and helped develop the transgender advocacy group WPATH's standards of care, believes that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is operating "beyond their scope of practice." Her young adult patients have told her about how quickly they were able to get hormone treatments from \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . "It really seems that their protocol is substandard and lacks the rigor necessary for a life-altering intervention," Edwards-Leeper said.\par \par \uc2 According to an analysis of insurance claim information by the Manhattan Institute, a conservative think tank, at least 40,000 patients went to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for gender medicine in 2023. About 40% of them were 18- to 22-year-olds. Between 2017 and 2023, it also treated 12,000 kids aged 12 to 17 for gender dysphoria. (These figures do not include patients who paid out of pocket, patients at VA facilities or those covered by Kaiser.)\par \par \uc2 In response to emailed questions about the specifics of its gender protocols, a spokesperson wrote, "Many \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  health centers are proud to provide gender-affirming care to eligible patients, as part of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's mission to ensure all people have access to the care they need to exercise their right to control their own bodies and lives."\par \par \uc2 Though its commitment to these principles remains strong, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  itself is in a kind of existential limbo. On July 8, U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani issued a temporary restraining order on Trump's Medicaid measure. The administration challenged the order, but Talwani issued a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fingfx.thomsonreuters.com/gfx/legaldocs/akvexaembpr/07112025planned.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 temporary restraining order}}}\uc2  on July 11. Arguments on the case will begin on July 18.\par \par \uc2 Like any independent healthcare provider, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is plagued by the same pressures facing all of American medicine: a shortage of primary care, industry consolidation, hospital closures and a relentlessly profit-driven system. According to several public health experts who specialize in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in many parts of the country, especially rural areas, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is the sole provider of primary and family-planning care for poor people, which they may not be able to access elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 "This is where you get that ripple effect," said Amy Friedrich-Karnik, director of federal policy at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . "It not only affects \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , it affects all people in a given community. Healthcare is a complicated ecosystem. In theory, these actions by the federal government are targeted, but in reality, they affect many, many more people. It's hard to overstate how devastating this will be."\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250718el7i003xp\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
