\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Planned Parenthood Gets Cut Off; The defunding of the  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  giant is the most beautiful thing in the GOP megabill.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Hennessey \par \uc2 876 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2025\par 21:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats know how to put lipstick on a pig. In 2022 they tarted up the Inflation Reduction Act, calling it a necessary makeover for the pandemic-scarred economy. Only after jamming the bill through Congress did they let the mask drop. President Biden never said another word about what it would do for inflation. He touted it instead as "the most significant climate-change law ever."\par \par \uc2 The One Big Beautiful Bill Act needs no cosmetics. The Medicaid work requirements and border funding are naturally gorgeous, and the extension of the 2017 tax cuts is a perfect 10. But beneath the surface is where the true beauty lies: It's the most significant pro-life law ever.\par \par \uc2 The bill, which President Trump signed on July 4, cuts off Medicaid funding to nonprofit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics no matter what other health-related services they offer\u8212\'20\'14or claim to. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  will of course be hardest hit. The nation's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider warns that 200 of its 600 "health centers" will be forced to close when the federal spigot runs dry. That is one of the most beautiful things I've ever heard.\par \par \uc2 Federal law already disallows the use of taxpayer money to pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But any kid with an allowance knows that money is fungible. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  pockets the Medicaid funding it gets for the cancer screenings and mental-health counseling it supposedly does. It then uses the money to pay for abortions. A third of the organization's funding comes from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Social conservatives have dreamed for decades about bankrupting \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Principal credit for this aspect of the big, beautiful victory belongs not to Mr. Trump but to Speaker Mike Johnson and Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2 . Congressional Republicans, as a whole, are to the right of the president on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But give Mr. Trump a large gold star for simply letting it happen, despite his liberal inclinations on such matters.\par \par \uc2 You can imagine a scenario in which the pro-life provisions of a bill like this got watered down or dropped during complex negotiations. That didn't happen here, and pro-lifers who are put off by Mr. Trump's style ought to tip their caps, even if doing so complexifies their understanding of the moral dimensions of his presidency. The Trump administrations have been good for social conservatives.\par \par \uc2 For all the beauty of the one big bill, the reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -funding provision is bound to get ugly. It's the nature of the beast. The president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  Affiliates of California called it "nothing short of cruelty for cruelty's sake." \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  president and CEO \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2  is claiming in interviews that the new law will be "devastating" for patients who live in what she calls "maternity healthcare deserts."\par \par \uc2 None of that adds up. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's defenders insist that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only a small part of what the organization does. They claim its core business is providing poor women in underserved areas with access to cancer screenings, mental-health counseling and birth control. If that's the case, and so much vital healthcare provision is at stake, why get so worked up about the abortions? Why not let the controversial aspect of the business go and keep doing the Lord's work?\par \par \uc2 Because the vital healthcare claim is hogwash, and everyone knows it. Killing babies is what \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  does, to the tune of 400,000 a year. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14not pap tests or mental health\u8212\'20\'14is the reason for its existence. Take that away and \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is nothing more than a glorified school nurse's office.\par \par \uc2 Think about what goes on in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. I don't want to ruin your day, but spend a few minutes thinking about what an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  really is\u8212\'20\'14how it's performed and what it means. Uncle Sam has been indirectly subsidizing this butchery for decades. So, yes, the end of the relationship between taxpayers and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry is a beautiful thing. It's OK to say it out loud.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is a decentralized, affiliate organization, more like a franchise business than a nationwide chain. Headquarters doesn't directly run local clinics. This structure makes it likely that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  won't go away anytime soon, despite all the wailing. Remember Acorn, the progressive network of community organizers that got caught fraudulently registering voters during the Obama administration? A Republican Congress defunded it, leading the national umbrella organization to dissolve itself, but local affiliates carried on under different names. Expect the same from \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The fight continues. Rust never sleeps. The important thing for pro-lifers is to stay engaged. With victories at the Supreme Court and in Congress, social conservatives are getting what they always wanted. This is a disorienting feeling for a movement that conceives of itself as a silent majority and had begun to believe that the horrors of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a permanent feature of American life.\par \par \uc2 In an ugly world, beauty has found a way.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hennessey is the Journal's deputy editorial features editor.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250707el770050l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
