\par \par \uc2 Alana Tedmon and her husband moved to Philadelphia last summer from Texas. PHOTO: Rachel Wisniewski for WSJ\par \par \uc2 Alana Tedmon and her husband moved to the outskirts of Dallas in June 2022, attracted by the lower cost of living and proximity to family. That same month,{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade}}}\uc2  and Texas followed by banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through all nine months of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 "It seemed like people were always trying to change the legislation around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  every single year but I never thought it would really happen legitimately," she said.\par \par \uc2 The 37-year-old freelance illustrator and her husband moved back to Philadelphia last summer, largely because of the ban. Then Tedmon got pregnant unexpectedly. She was initially excited, but anxiety about the couple's financial security ultimately led her to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14something she was grateful was feasible in the state.\par \par \uc2 "If we have a child, I want it to be because we're ready, and not because 'oops, it just happened,'" she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banned or heavily restricted}}}\uc2  in about one-third of U.S. states, and some women of childbearing age say that has introduced a new calculus about where to live and work. Though migration patterns are complicated, early data show that the states with the most restrictive laws are seeing some residents leave.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/papers/w33328"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent paper published}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2  estimated that 13 states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans collectively saw about 146,000 residents leave due to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the year after the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to the procedure. The paper found that while those states\u8212\'20\'14mostly in the South\u8212\'20\'14had been gaining population at a significantly faster rate than other parts of the country, that advantage essentially vanished afterward. The authors looked at patterns in Postal Service change-of-address data after the Supreme Court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 Over a five-year period, those states' populations could be about 1% smaller than if they hadn't passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, the paper estimated. The researchers received some funding from the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Society of Family Planning, two groups that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The extent to which women are making decisions about where to live based on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans has been more pronounced than many economists who study this issue anticipated.\par \par \uc2 "A single policy change is unlikely to be the marginal factor in deciding on a move. And yet here we have this really strong new evidence that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy really is impacting migration," said Caitlin Myers, an economist at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. The overturning of Roe, Myers said, was "a moment of understanding the extent to which state policies can become very, very salient."\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edworkingpapers.com/sites/default/files/ai25-1126.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Another recent study}}}\uc2  found a decline in the proportion of high-achieving women applying to universities in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans after Roe was overturned compared with a couple of years earlier. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aamc.org/about-us/mission-areas/health-care/post-dobbs-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Research has also shown}}}\uc2  a decline in applications to medical school residencies in states that have heavily restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Research in this field remains in a relatively early phase, and the impacts identified so far are small in the context of the larger U.S. economy. For the most part, big companies haven't made large public shifts in hiring as a result of state-level \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think will be the economic impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The out-migration trend has been most sustained for single people, reflecting the fact that younger people are more likely to be more mobile and better able to move on principle, according to the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2  paper. That means that states with bans stand to lose out on workers in some of their prime career-building years.\par \par \uc2 In interviews, some women who are factoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws into their life decisions cited worries about suffering complications during a planned pregnancy and being unable to get care or having to travel out of state for an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which can cost in the tens of thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Kayla Smith had lived in Idaho for more than a decade. But she decided to move after the state's ban prevented her from obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in her home state for her unborn son who was suffering from a fatal fetal heart condition.\par \par \uc2 She and her husband took out a $16,000 personal loan because they weren't sure if their health-insurance provider would cover her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Washington state. That was in addition to travel expenses. (Nine months later her insurance company reimbursed her for the procedure.)\par \par \uc2 When Smith got pregnant again, the couple left Idaho for good, even though that meant moving to a corner of Washington with limited obstetric care. "That to us was safer than staying in the state of Idaho," Smith said.\par \par \uc2 Some women say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have curtailed their earnings and career advancement, forcing them to forgo conferences or other work travel while pregnant, concerned they wouldn't be able to obtain emergency medical care. All bans allow doctors to terminate a pregnancy to save the life of the mother, but those exceptions don't encompass all emergency situations and in practice doctors also have sometimes found them difficult to apply.\par \par \uc2 For Emilie Aries, who regularly traveled about 40 times a year as a consultant and keynote speaker, the fear of being unable to get emergency care was so great that she decided during her second pregnancy to stop traveling to states with bans. She had suffered a series of miscarriages before that. She said she lost tens of thousands of dollars in income because she wouldn't travel to states such as Texas.\par \par \uc2 "No amount of money is worth putting my life at risk. It's a terrible position to be put in, quite frankly," Aries said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com}}}\uc2  and Harriet Torry at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:harriet.torry@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 harriet.torry@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250706el76000jh\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
