\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Wisconsin's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Settlement; A Badger State Supreme Court ruling sustains the state's political compromise on the issue.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 356 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2025\par 21:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's Dobbs decision in 2022 reopened partisan warfare over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it is settling down to a rough political compromise in most states. A case in point is Wisconsin, where a state Supreme Court decision this week reveals the settlement terms.\par \par \uc2 In a 4-3 decision in Kaul v. Urmanski, the court's liberal majority finally overturned an 1849 ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was still on the books even though it hasn't been enforced. Justice \uc2 Rebecca Dallet\uc2  wrote that "comprehensive legislation enacted over the last 50 years regulating in detail the 'who, what, where, when, and how' of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so thoroughly covers the entire subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it was meant as a substitute for the 19th century near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The court split along ideological lines, with a majority vote provided by new progressive Justice Janet Protasiewicz, who campaigned on the issue. But it's notable that the court stopped short of finding a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution, which 10 other states including California, Michigan, Vermont and Colorado have done. The Justices dismissed as moot a second companion case, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  v. Urmanski, that presented the opportunity to rule on the state constitutional issue.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the motivation for this act of judicial restraint, it is welcome. A majority of voters in Wisconsin, as in most of the country, favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access with some restrictions. The Wisconsin court's decision means that the state's current \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations remain in effect. These include a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks, a parental-consent requirement for minors, and a 24-hour waiting period before an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is performed.\par \par \uc2 The political battles over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't go away because basic principles of morality and human liberty are at stake. But Wisconsin seems to have come out in a place most voters can accept, which is a benefit of letting states and their voters decide rather than pre-empt them as Roe v. Wade did.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250702el72006k5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
