\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Makes It Easier for States to Boot Planned Parenthood From Medicaid; Justices find that patients don't have a right to sue states that disqualify the  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  provider from the healthcare program\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariah Timms \par \uc2 670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Thursday made it easier for conservative states to kick \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics out of their Medicaid programs, a financial blow to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.          \par \par \uc2 The court, in a 6-3 decision, ruled that patients don't have a right to sue states that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/planned-parenthood-abortion-trump-supreme-court-a0c54920"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disqualify Planned Parenthood}}}\uc2  based on their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The court's three liberal justices dissented.          \par \par \uc2 "Private enforcement does not always benefit the public, not least because it requires states to divert money and attention away from social services and toward litigation," Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  wrote for the majority. "And balancing those costs and benefits poses a question of public policy that, under our system of government, only Congress may answer."          \par \par \uc2 The case came from South Carolina, where Gov. \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  in 2018 issued an executive order that led to the removal of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  as an eligible healthcare provider under Medicaid, the joint federal-state program for limited-income Americans.           \par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics offer a range of healthcare services to women aside from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and treating Medicaid patients has provided a significant source of revenue for the organization. Although Medicaid funds can't be used to pay for abortions, South Carolina says any income \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  receives frees up other money the organization could use to help women end pregnancies.          \par \par \uc2 One Planned Parenthood patient sued the state, arguing she had a right under Medicaid law to seek care from any qualified healthcare provider. Lower courts allowed her claim to proceed and agreed with her arguments, saying \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's status as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider wasn't a valid basis to exclude the group from the state's Medicaid network.                    \par \par \uc2 States control their lists of Medicaid-eligible providers, usually governed by requirements around licensing and other medical qualifications. Lawyers for South Carolina argued the state has broad power to define eligibility, including based on what procedures a provider offers. South Carolina said it has an interest in cutting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers' access to state funding after residents elected officials with antiabortion platforms.           \par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and its allies tried to turn Medicaid into a weapon to force their agenda on our State. The Court just told them: No," South Carolina Attorney General Alan Wilson said Thursday.           \par \par \uc2 The court's dissenters said Thursday's decision "is likely to result in tangible harm to real people."            \par \par \uc2 "It will deprive Medicaid recipients in South Carolina of their only meaningful way of enforcing a right that Congress has expressly granted to them. And, more concretely, it will strip those South Carolinians\u8212\'20\'14and countless other Medicaid recipients around the country\u8212\'20\'14of a deeply personal freedom: the ability to decide who treats us at our most vulnerable," Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  wrote.           \par \par \uc2 Allowing states to cut off Medicaid funds could prompt a significant shift in the sprawling organization's funding stream and offerings.          \par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's national office serves as a fundraising, lobbying and communications juggernaut. A network of about 50 local affiliates operates some 600 clinics providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contraception, breast-cancer screenings and other healthcare. In recent years, the organization {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/planned-parenthood-abortion-trump-supreme-court-a0c54920"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has faced financial and political stress}}}\uc2 , including other federal-funding cuts, drops in contributions, dozens of staff-member layoffs and clinic closures across the country.          \par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is fending off a range of additional financial threats, including a recent decision by the Trump administration to freeze its Title X contraception funding and efforts by congressional Republicans to cut off its Medicaid funding entirely.           \par \par \uc2 Democrats and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates say Thursday's decision and these additional threats could cripple the organization and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision nationwide.           \par \par \uc2 "Today's decision is a grave injustice that strikes at the very bedrock of American freedom and promises to send South Carolina deeper into a health care crisis," said \uc2 Paige Johnson\uc2 , president and CEO of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic.           \par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250626el6q0053d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
