\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Republican's Life-Threatening Pregnancy Collided With Florida's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Politics; Due in August with her first child, Rep. Kat Cammack says doctors last year hesitated to treat her ectopic pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katy Stech Ferek \par \uc2 1,483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2025\par 18:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rep. Kat Cammack arrived at the emergency room in May 2024 terrified by what she had just learned: Her pregnancy could kill her at any moment.\par \par \uc2 It would only get worse. The Florida Republican needed a shot of methotrexate to help expel her ectopic pregnancy, in which there is no way for the embryo to survive. Her state's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/gov-ron-desantis-signs-florida-six-week-abortion-ban-b18029cc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  had just taken effect. She said doctors and nurses who saw her said they were worried about losing their licenses or going to jail if they gave her drugs to end her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 She began arguing her case. The staff resisted, she said, even though doctors earlier that day estimated she was just five weeks pregnant. There was no heartbeat, and her life was at risk. She pulled up the state law on her phone for hospital workers to read. She said she called the governor's office late at night for help, but no one picked up.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, doctors finally agreed to give her the methotrexate, Cammack said.\par \par \uc2 The Florida ban, which took effect May 1, 2024, made abortions illegal after six weeks with limited exceptions. It didn't ban procedures for ectopic pregnancies, but concern about the law's wording made doctors hesitant, said patients and physicians. Months later, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ahca.myflorida.com/content/download/25109/file/9.19.24_Notice_to_Providers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida regulators gave guidance}}}\uc2  to address what they called misinformation, making clear that doctors should intervene in cases such as Cammack's.\par \par \uc2 Pregnant again and due this summer, Cammack said the politics of the incident linger with her. While Cammack opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and co-chairs the House Pro-Life Caucus, she supports exceptions for reported cases of rape and incest in the first trimester and in instances in which the mother's life is at risk. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich427\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6405
