\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Josh Hawley Smears a Pro-Life Lawyer; I had to quit the \uc2 FDA\uc2  after he falsely accused me of supporting  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 By Hilary Perkins \par \uc2 770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2025\par 21:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I wanted to serve in President Trump's new administration. I voted for him and admire his resilience in the face of adversity from politicized prosecutions to assassination attempts. So when Marty Makary asked me to join his team as chief counsel for the Food and Drug Administration, I jumped at the chance. For years I had defended the \uc2 FDA\uc2  as a career Justice Department attorney.\par \par \uc2 A few days after I started the job, Sen. \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  (R., Mo.) tweeted an Axios article about me and commented: "This can't be right. This report says Marty Makary is trying to sneak a Biden \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyer into a top leadership position at \uc2 FDA\uc2 ." It wasn't right, but Mr. Hawley escalated the accusation, later citing two cases of the many I supervised as evidence I had argued "for Joe Biden's pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and draconian vaccine mandates."\par \par \uc2 In most of the cases I handled involving the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone, I defended the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's restrictions against challenges by pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups. In the one case brought by pro-life groups, the Supreme Court ruled unanimously in the government's favor. In the vaccine-mandate case, the government prevailed for lack of jurisdiction. I didn't set these policies or argue for them. My job was to defend the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's legal position regardless of my personal views.\par \par \uc2 I tried to reach Mr. Hawley the day he first tweeted, but he refused to speak with me. I hoped the issue would blow over and I would have a chance to prove myself to him and the rest of the pro-life community. Instead, he issued an ultimatum: Unless I resigned, he would vote against Dr. Makary in committee.\par \par \uc2 It was a gut punch. But our nation needed Dr. Makary at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . So the next morning I resigned, and Mr. Hawley voted in favor of Dr. Makary.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how I became the target of a U.S. senator. But I am certain he made a terrible mistake. Instead of pushing out a "Biden \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyer," he forced out an experienced conservative pro-life attorney who supports the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 After law school, I served as a clerk on the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals. I worked in corporate litigation at \uc2 Jones Day\uc2 , where I first became interested in politics. Defending Gov. Bob McDonnell in his public corruption trial, I found myself agreeing with his views and appreciating the dangers of a weaponized justice system. That prosecution was led by Jack Smith, who years later would spearhead the politicized prosecutions of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 After seven years at \uc2 Jones Day\uc2 , I moved into public service at the Justice Department. Initially I investigated pill mills fueling the opioid epidemic, but soon began defending the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , including its in-person dispensing requirement for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone. The first Trump administration rewarded my success by promoting me to a management position, in which I led a team of attorneys defending the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As a career attorney, my job wasn't tied to a political party or administration. So although I voted for Mr. Trump in 2020, it never crossed my mind to quit after Joe Biden came into office. Sometimes my professional responsibilities conflicted with my personal views, such as when I had to defend the legality of the Biden administration's removal of the in-person dispensing requirement I'd previously argued to keep.\par \par \uc2 I've always considered myself pro-life, but losing a baby early in pregnancy led me to a deeper conviction that all life is precious and deserves protection. Those are my deeply held personal beliefs. My job as a career attorney was to represent the U.S. regardless of those beliefs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hawley misjudged me. I am a committed Christian and a common-sense pro-life conservative. I'm a mom of three beautiful girls, and my family's life centers on our church community and our girls' Catholic school. Nothing I did as a career attorney negates who I am or what I believe. I simply followed my oath to defend the legality of the government's actions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump needs competent and experienced attorneys who share the conservative values of his administration. I am one. All of us, including Mr. Hawley, owe it to the president to honor his staffing decisions so that we can focus on achieving his agenda.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Perkins is a former government lawyer.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250325el3p004v2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
