\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Illinois Cancels Henry Hyde; Progressives stripped the late GOP Congressman's name from a courthouse over  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 369 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2025\par 22:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If you think the end of the Biden Administration means the end of cancel culture, alas, no. This month board members in DuPage County in Illinois voted 10-5 to remove former Rep. Henry Hyde's name from the county courthouse.\par \par \uc2 Was the late Congressman a closet Confederate who supported slavery or fought against the union? Nope. The highly regarded Republican, who represented his suburban Chicago seat in Congress from 1975 to 2007, will have his legacy expunged because he opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In 1976 he led the fight for what became known as the Hyde Amendment, which banned federal funding for women seeking to terminate their pregnancies. The amendment doesn't ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It merely says taxpayer dollars shouldn't fund the procedure given the profound matter of conscience implicated in having or paying for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One hundred and seven Democrats voted for the Hyde Amendment when it came up for a standalone vote in 1976. And for years it enjoyed strong bipartisan support, including from the likes of Joe Biden until he flipped on the issue to run for President again in 2020.\par \par \uc2 If opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is grounds for cancellation, that rules out naming buildings for nearly every Republican lawmaker, since the party platform included a pro-life plank from 1976 until the 2024 election when Roe v. Wade had been overturned. It also could cancel pro-life Democrats who weren't rare in the party before support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became a progressive litmus test.\par \par \uc2 Ending federal funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has bipartisan support among voters. But if progressive de-namers intend to broaden the categories of unacceptable political positions, what about gay rights and LGBTQ? Barack Obama's name adorns dozens of schools and roads. Should his name be removed because until his second presidential term he opposed same-sex marriage?\par \par \uc2 Henry Hyde was the kind of thoughtful, principled Member who used to be far more common in Congress. It's a sign of the rotten times, and perhaps the rot in Illinois, that DuPage County Democrats want his name banished from public memory.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020250221el2l007sl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
