\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Lobby Endangers Pregnant Women; Medical authorities are reclassifying life-saving procedures to make a political statement.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rachel Roth Aldhizer \par \uc2 965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 December 2024\par 21:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I'm pregnant for the eighth time. After four live births in my 20s, I had three consecutive miscarriages last year. While I'm optimistic that our family will welcome a fifth child in the spring, I've become used to bad news. I know better than most what kinds of complications can arise in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of one such complication haunted me recently after I saw a Kamala Harris {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WGCVxgcxJRo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign ad}}}\uc2  that featured the heartbreaking story of a Texas woman named Ondrea. Ondrea's water broke at 16 weeks, threatening to send her into early labor with a baby far from the age of viability. This complication is known as periviable prelabor rupture of membranes, or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://urldefense.com/v3/__http:/unmfm.pbworks.com/w/file/fetch/140666496/Prelabor*20Rupture*20of*20Membranes_ACOG*20Practice*20Bulletin,*20Number*20217.pdf__;JSUlJSUlJQ!!F0Stn7g!Gpg8xPz7NVoT-tQXDthXJue0tnqJRum9Lt28eIcImZEJI4HjN_R4-62bNA8X5up05HGf4LoMdaH-Hs6whQ$"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 PPROM}}}\uc2 . Ondrea's child didn't survive, and Ondrea developed a septic infection that threatened her life and fertility. "Because I live in Texas," she explains, "I was denied the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care I needed."\par \par \uc2 But Ondrea didn't need an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; nor was she a victim of Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, which explicitly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legiscan.com/TX/text/HB3058/id/2820126"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protect}}}\uc2  treatment for PPROM. The standard of care for PPROM has always been either induced labor or expectant management, according to previous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://unmfm.pbworks.com/w/file/fetch/140666496/Prelabor Rupture of Membranes_ACOG Practice Bulletin, Number 217.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 practice guidelines}}}\uc2  from the American College of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Neither have ever been previously called abortions.\par \par \uc2 Yet this October the American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology released {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajog.org/article/S0002-9378(24)00759-2/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new clinical guidelines}}}\uc2  on the treatment of PPROM, produced by the \uc2 Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine\uc2 . Now induction of labor for PPROM is categorized under \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. "All patients," the paper states, should be "offered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and expectant management can also be offered." Although the journal is independent of \uc2 ACOG\uc2 , the college endorsed these recommended changes as new clinical guidance. The Harris campaign exploited this equivocation.\par \par \uc2 PPROM prior to 24 weeks gestation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajog.org/article/S0002-9378(09)00693-0/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 occurs}}}\uc2  in less than 0.37% of pregnancies. Treatment depends on gestational age, viability and potential infection risks to the mother. Leaking amniotic fluid heightens the danger of sepsis, which can lead to maternal death. PPROM prior to 21 weeks gestation results in higher risks to mothers and markedly increased fatality for perinates. Expectant management\u8212\'20\'14waiting to see if the baby reaches viability\u8212\'20\'14may be riskier for mothers earlier in pregnancy and results in higher rates of infection for both mother and child. Induction or augmentation of labor for midtrimester PPROM, regardless of fetal viability, may result in better maternal outcomes.\par \par \uc2 The intent when managing PPROM is to balance the needs of mother and baby, and the best-case scenario is to preserve both their lives. The death of a fetus from labor and delivery during management for PPROM is a tragic secondary effect of treatment. According to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , this isn't an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the CDC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/reproductive-health/data-statistics/abortion-surveillance-system.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defines}}}\uc2  as an intervention with the "intention to terminate a suspected or known ongoing intrauterine pregnancy and that does not result in a live birth."\par \par \uc2 Reclassifying induction of labor\u8212\'20\'14or, rarely, surgical resolution for PPROM\u8212\'20\'14as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care seems to threaten women's prenatal care nationwide. No \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation in any state restricts emergency procedures to protect the life or health of the mother. Yet this linguistic shift could mislead physicians in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions into believing that standard treatments for pregnancy complications may be illegal, or at least subject to a higher standard of physician judgment when determining a treatment course.\par \par \uc2 I live in North Carolina, which enacted a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncleg.gov/Sessions/2023/Bills/Senate/PDF/S20v6.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restriction}}}\uc2  in 2023. Although the law makes exceptions for medical emergencies, it's unclear how it defines treatments for complications like PPROM. After conversations with several people involved in developing the law, I wasn't able to gain any clarity on whether induction of labor for prenatal complications is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under North Carolina law. Practitioners and hospital systems are coming to their own conclusions.\par \par \uc2 My own physician told me he would consider an induction for mid-trimester PPROM an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and he wouldn't perform such a procedure unless the alternative was maternal death or physical impairment. But another North Carolina-based physician offers induction of labor for PPROM even before signs of infection appear as one management option, and she doesn't consider this act either to be an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulated by the law or a violation of her own staunch pro-life beliefs. A North Carolina-based maternal-fetal medicine specialist told me that his hospital system would require \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  documentation if he induced labor for PPROM but not if he augmented labor for PPROM.\par \par \uc2 Only the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobby and the politicians who support it benefit from these linguistic games. By their standard, many treatments for pregnancy complications are now considered abortions, from removing ectopic pregnancies and miscarriage management to induction of labor for PPROM. In other words, prenatal care is complete only with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the toolbox. But it is intent, not simply a procedure, that makes something an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To mislabel standard medical treatment for PPROM as "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care" to advance a political agenda is as disingenuous as it is dangerous, and it places undue moral weight on patients and physicians alike when evaluating treatment options.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life advocates aren't the ones threatening women's prenatal care, but to ensure high standards in prenatal care without compromising moral objections to elective abortions, states like North Carolina should amend their legislation to clarify what does or doesn't qualify as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Arming physicians with the information they need to provide quality prenatal care includes developing precise definitions that eliminate hesitation and confusion. Pregnant women deserve clarity that protects their health and well-being.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Aldhizer is a Robert Novak Fellow at the Fund for American Studies.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020241219ekcj005k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
