\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 My Mother and the Selzer Poll; Politeness about  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  creates an illusion of consensus among women.\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Taranto \par \uc2 683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2024\par 22:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If your mother says she loves you, check it out, an old journalistic adage has it. My sources confirm that she did, but my late mother once told me something that didn't check out\u8212\'20\'14and that turns out to be relevant to politics decades later.\par \par \uc2 It was the spring of 1992 and mom, then 60, was visiting me in New York. We got into a discussion of politics. It was an election year, the Supreme Court was considering whether to overturn Roe v. Wade, and the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  came up. I thought Roe was an affront to the Constitution and was (as I am now) ambivalent about legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mom's position on the latter question was clear, and she pressed it with an appeal to popular opinion: "I've never met a woman who was against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 I had no reason to doubt her sincerity, but I knew that her statement was false. The previous day she had met my landlady, a conservative pro-life Catholic writer. Even without the counterexample, it would have rung false. The women I knew were all over the map on the issue, and polls I had seen showed little difference between the sexes in the distribution of opinions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Mom wasn't particularly political, and I surmised that the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rarely came up in her conversations with acquaintances. It occurred to me that women who opposed or had misgivings about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably tend to keep it to themselves when speaking with women whose view they don't know. After all, many women have had abortions, and the opinion that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wrong implies a judgment about them and their actions. Thus politeness creates an illusion of consensus.\par \par \uc2 I thought of this over the weekend, when the Des Moines Register {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/iowa-poll/2024/11/02/iowa-poll-kamala-harris-leads-donald-trump-2024-presidential-race/75354033007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  that its highly regarded poll found \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  leading \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by 3 points in the Hawkeye State. Mr. Trump carried Iowa by 9 points in 2016 and 8 in 2020. "Age and gender are the two most dynamic factors that are explaining these numbers," the pollster, Ann Selzer, told the Register. Her survey found Ms. Harris ahead of Mr. Trump among suburban Iowans, 59% to 36%.\par \par \uc2 The prevailing hypothesis (assuming the poll wasn't an outlier) was that Iowa women, particularly older suburban women, had swung en masse toward the Democrats in reaction to a restrictive state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that took effect in July. When a friend emailed me on Election Day asking for my prediction, I wrote: "There may be a substantial female shy Trump vote." I suspected the delicacy of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue was compounding the familiar problem of social-acceptability bias.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  no doubt is a voting issue for many women (and men), and it seems plausible, even likely, that Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization gave Democrats a boost in 2022 and even 2024. But the idea that it was going to lead to a mass change in voting behavior when the parties have been polarized over the issue since 1980 never made sense.\par \par \uc2 The Selzer poll turned out to be an outlier. With most votes counted, Mr. Trump leads Ms. Harris in Iowa by close to 13 points. CNN's exit poll found that nationwide he outpolled her among suburban white women\u8212\'20\'14my mother's demographic\u8212\'20\'1453% to 47%.\par \par \uc2 As firm as mom was in her support for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she wasn't a single-issue voter. She told me how she voted in three elections\u8212\'20\'14for Rep. John Anderson (a liberal Republican running as an independent) in 1980, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in 1992 and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in 2000.\par \par \uc2 When she visited me in 1992, she was leaning toward \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2 , because she thought he would "shake things up." I have no idea what she would make of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I like to think she'd be ambivalent about him, taking after her son.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Taranto is the Journal's editorial features editor.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020241107ekb7007bx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
