\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Was a Bust for Democrats; The issue did little for their candidates despite broad public support.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2024\par 22:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 From \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  down through the House races, Democrats who bet that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would save them came up empty on Tuesday. They saw the issue as a political lifeline in a cycle dominated by inflation and the border. But even voters who oppose restrictions looked past their overheated rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris described \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a dividing line between the parties, claiming that President Trump and GOP majorities in Congress would enact a national ban. She hoped the threat would motivate progressives to turn out in key states and persuade independents and Republican women to cross over. Democrats spent $175 million on TV ads across the country to hammer the point\u8212\'20\'14more than any other issue.\par \par \uc2 Yet millions of Americans voted for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access while choosing Republicans on the same ballot. Voters more worried about the economy and immigration voted for change in Washington while preserving or expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access through state ballot measures. Among the 11% who said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was their top issue, 29% of them voted for Mr. Trump, according to the AP-Fox VoteCast.\par \par \uc2 Democratic attacks fell flat in part because Mr. Trump took an early stance against a national ban. He took credit in April for helping to overturn Roe v. Wade, but he said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy should be decided in each state, and he later said he'd veto a national ban. His position let pro-life candidates in state races promise that no ban would happen, even if they supported one personally.\par \par \uc2 That blunted the issue in federal races. Montanans approved a ballot measure adding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to their state constitution, and they also tossed out Democratic Sen. \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 , who ran on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Republican Tim Sheehy defeated the three-term incumbent, who welcomed the ballot measure in the hope that it would provide him an electoral tailwind.\par \par \uc2 The same split occurred in Florida. Democrats bet that their $118 million campaign on the issue would boost Senate candidate \uc2 Debbie Mucarsel-Powell\uc2 . Yet 56% of voters chose to re-elect Republican Sen. Rick Scott while 57% voted to repeal a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, though the ballot measure fell short of the 60% support it needed to pass.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats twisted their opponents' positions in an attempt to increase the stakes. Among the most egregious was New York's \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 , whose supporters ran ads suggesting that Rep. Mike Lawler would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with no exception for rape, and that he had voted to "put doctors in jail." Mr. Lawler corrected the record in his own ads and won the suburban swing district by seven points.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drove less enthusiasm this year than in the 2022 midterms in part because many states have made it more accessible since Roe was overturned that year. Through legislation, the ballot or the courts, 22 states have either expanded access or made it harder to restrict. And despite significant restrictions in 13 states, the number of abortions in the U.S. increased in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate is playing out much as we predicted before Roe fell. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would be decided by the people, not judges, though it would take several elections to resolve. The issue's political salience may fall further in the next two years if Republicans and Mr. Trump show voters they were honest about opposing a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020241107ekb7007hh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
