\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Live Results:  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  on the Ballot in 10 States; Voters decide on 10  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 -rights measures and one antiabortion measure\b0\par\par\uc2 By WSJ Staff \par \uc2 847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par 14:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Voters in 10 states are deciding on ballot measures in the 2024 election that would enshrine {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/these-republicans-are-pushing-for-abortion-rights-cadf5606"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 . That is the most in one election since the since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade}}}\uc2  two years ago. Going into the election, half of those states had banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy, or after six, 12 or 15 weeks. Nebraska has two competing ballot measures, one that would protect the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability and another that would ban it after the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 See in real time how all the measures are faring, or skip ahead to a particular state's section. States are sorted by if they have been called and then \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  status prior to the election. Bans may have limited exceptions, such as for medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 * Missouri (passed)\par \par \uc2 * South Dakota (failed)\par \par \uc2 * Florida (failed)\par \par \uc2 * Nebraska (passed and failed)\par \par \uc2 * New York (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Colorado (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Maryland (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Arizona (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Montana (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Nevada (passed)\par \par \uc2 Missouri (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned with few exceptions\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the citizen initiative would prevent the government from restricting a person's fundamental right to reproductive freedom, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability or to protect the health of the pregnant patient.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 50% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 South Dakota (failed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned with few exceptions\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the citizen initiative would amend the state constitution to establish a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first trimester but allow restrictions later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 50% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 Florida (failed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned after six weeks\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the citizen initiative would amend the state's constitution to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights until fetal viability and to protect the health of the patient.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 60% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 Nebraska (passed and failed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned after 12 weeks\par \par \uc2 Nebraska has two measures. To pass, a measure would need a majority of votes in support and at least 35% of the total ballots cast in Nebraska to be in its favor.\par \par \uc2 If both pass, the measure with the most votes in favor would win pending a decision from the governor on whether the two conflict enough to bar both from taking effect. Nebraska's secretary of state has said the two measures conflict with another and couldn't both be in effect simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Nebraska Gov. \uc2 Jim Pillen\uc2  declined to comment on "speculative hypotheticals" ahead of the election.\par \par \uc2 A for vote on citizen-led Initiative 434 would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the first trimester except in medical emergencies or if the pregnancy is the result of sexual assault or incest.\par \par \uc2 A for vote on citizen-led Initiative 439 would amend the state constitution to provide a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability or to protect the health of the patient.\par \par \uc2 Arizona (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned after 15 weeks\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the citizen initiative would amend the state constitution to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights until fetal viability, or roughly 23 weeks of pregnancy, and to protect the health of the pregnant patient.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 50% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 Montana (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned after fetal viability (typically around 23 weeks into pregnancy)\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the citizen initiative would amend the state's constitution to expressly provide a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability or to protect the health of the patient.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 50% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 New York (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned after fetal viability (typically around 23 weeks into pregnancy)\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the legislatively referred measure would establish broad equal-rights protections, including for pregnancy and reproductive healthcare, that supporters say would make laws restricting abortions unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 50% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 Nevada (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned after 24 weeks\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the citizen initiative would place the question on the ballot in 2026. Two votes approving the initiative in back-to-back general elections would amend the state constitution to provide the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a qualified healthcare practitioner until fetal viability or to protect the health of the patient.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 50% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 Colorado (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: no gestational limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the citizen initiative would amend the state constitution to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and repeal a section that limited health-insurance coverage for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 55% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 Maryland (passed)\par \par \uc2 * Pre-election: no gestational limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 A yes vote on the legislatively referred measure would guarantee a fundamental right to reproductive freedom, including the right to terminate one's own pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Threshold to pass: 50% or more of votes\par \par \uc2 Sources: KFF (measures, threshold to pass); \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  and staff reports (pre-election bans and gestational limits)\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Calfas, Kara Dapena, Laura Kusisto, Dov Friedman, Lakshmi Ketineni and Andrew Levinson contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020241105ekb5007y5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
