\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin Sen. \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  is seen as the most vulnerable of the Democrats in the 'blue wall' states. PHOTO: Liam James Doyle for WSJ\par \par \uc2 Republican \uc2 Eric Hovde\uc2 , a wealthy banker, was also trying to unseat Democrat Sen. \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  in Wisconsin\u8212\'20\'14one of three states that form Democrats' "blue wall" in both the presidential and Senate contests.\par \par \uc2 Baldwin was seen as the most vulnerable of the Democrats in those states, which also include Michigan and Pennsylvania. She worked to run up her margins in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/wisconsin-senator-tammy-baldwin-reelection-campaign-b8112332"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 traditionally red rural areas}}}\uc2 , making herself an expert on dairy and other agricultural products, but was hit by Republican advertisements.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Republicans swooped in to support Rogers, a former House lawmaker who was competing against Democratic Rep. \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  to fill an open seat.\par \par \uc2 Democrats' best hope for a pickup had been in Texas. Cruz, who was first elected to the Senate in 2012 as a conservative firebrand, recast himself as a moderate this election cycle, emphasizing his work across the aisle. Allred had tried to argue that Cruz was an extremist and criticized him for hiding in a closet when Trump supporters rioted at the U.S. Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021. Allred also tied Cruz to the loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Texas, while Cruz sought to link Allred to problems with inflation and border security under the Biden-Harris administration.\par \par \uc2 Scott Calvert and Christopher Otts contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lindsay Wise at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:lindsay.wise@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lindsay.wise@wsj.com}}}\uc2  and Siobhan Hughes at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Siobhan.hughes@wsj.com"}}{\fldr