\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Opinion: Election Night 2024; Writers for the Journal's opinion pages react as the polls close and the votes are counted.\b0\par\par\uc2 By WSJ Opinion Staff \par \uc2 4,676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par 23:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As millions of ballots were counted, our contributors offered real-time commentary on the state of play. Thanks for sticking with us as we watched election night 2024. Tallying votes is expected to go into the wee hours, but please come back to WSJ Opinion in the morning for more commentary on the final results as they arrive.\par \par \uc2 Barton Swaim\u8212\'20\'14 12:46 a.m.\par \par \uc2 A final thought: If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins, as looks increasingly likely, it will be proof that deciders and describers, to use \uc2 Nicholas Eberstadt\uc2 's term\u8212\'20\'14the mainstream media, the expert class, the entertainers and influencers\u8212\'20\'14don't have the power that they think they do. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had few if any significant accomplishments, had no retail political skills, and was tied to an administration responsible for a series of epic blunders: inflation as a result of overspending, disaster in Afghanistan, a war in Europe, chaos at the southern border. The deciders and describers have tried their best to convince Americans that Vice President Harris is somebody she isn't. But any candidate, even one facing an opponent as unpopular as Mr. Trump, would find it a near-impossible challenge to win under the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Swaim is a Journal editorial page writer.\par \par \uc2 Collin Levy\u8212\'20\'14 12:38 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are missing Scranton Joe. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is currently winning Lackawanna County with about 51% of the vote, more than two points lower than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's 53.6% in 2020. That margin makes a difference. The areas around Scranton and Wilkes-Barre in neighboring Luzerne County (where Ms. Harris is also underperforming Mr. Biden) are working class, former coal mining towns that have been trending more conservative. Mr. Biden may have been holding back that shift as a hometown favorite. Ms. Harris may have needed Pennsylvania Gov. \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  on the ticket more than she realized.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Levy is a member of the Journal's editorial board.\par \par \uc2 Allysia Finley\u8212\'20\'14 12:24 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Ohio Sen. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 's defeat is a loss for the economic populism embraced by much of the left\u8212\'20\'14and increasingly many on the right. Unions have had no stronger defender in the Senate than Mr. Brown, who has promoted Build America, Buy America mandates and increased tariffs. A handmaiden for the Biden administration's industrial policy, he lobbied for subsidies for semiconductor and solar-panel manufacturers in Ohio. Mr. Brown's defeat suggests caution about interpreting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's groundswell of support as a voter mandate for such policies.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14\u8212\'20\'14Ms. Finley is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Life Science" column.\par \par \uc2 Barton Swaim\u8212\'20\'14 12:09 a.m.\par \par \uc2 The outcome of this election is a consequence\u8212\'20\'14bear with me\u8212\'20\'14of the Supreme Court's 2015 ruling in Obergefell v. Hodges. At least in some substantial measure.\par \par \uc2 That decision declared same-sex marriage a constitutional right, taking the question out of the political sphere. Before Obergefell, a vast and well-funded army of nonprofits and activist groups was advocating same-sex marriage laws and amendments in states across the country. Then, suddenly, they hit the jackpot. Thanks to a 5-4 court majority, they won. There was nothing left to do.\par \par \uc2 That, not coincidentally, was the moment at which the cultural left shifted its emphasis to an issue very few Americans had ever considered: the rights of transgender people. (There wasn't then, and there still isn't, a workable noun, only an adjective, "transgender," adjoined to "people"\u8212\'20\'14a measure of the phenomenon's newness.)\par \par \uc2 In the absence of any need to defend marriage equality, as the same-sex marriage cause had been called, the left moved on to this new cause. But the consequent spread of transgenderism created unforeseen and unsolvable social problems, chief among them biological men competing in women's sports and transgender ideology spreading in elementary school classrooms.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  would put it, pounced. GOP candidates across the country\u8212\'20\'14among them \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Ohio Senator-elect \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2  (who has a gay son)\u8212\'20\'14challenged their Democratic opponents on the transgender question, and those Democrats offered weak responses almost to a man. At this point a critical mass of those Republicans appear to have prevailed. Democrats may have to decide if they want to hold on to a political cause that American parents do not like.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Swaim is a Journal editorial page writer.\par \par \uc2 Allysia Finley\u8212\'20\'14 11:37 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Democrats may have drawn false comfort from their success last year in holding the state Senate and flipping the House of Delegates. As the Journal's editorial pages noted at the time, Republicans performed better than expected in many legislative races, but they faced a barrage of Democratic ads on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the 2022 reversal of Roe v. Wade was still recent.\par \par \uc2 Another year later, and the closeness of the presidential race in Virginia suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue may be losing its political potency in the D.C. suburbs, especially relative to quality of life issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Finley is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Life Science" column.\par \par \uc2 Kim Strassel\u8212\'20\'14 11:33 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Ohio Sen. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  was perhaps the most nimble of the Democratic incumbents fighting for re-election, skillfully pitching himself as a populist for the working class. It didn't save him his job, as Fox News has now called the race for Republican challenger \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2 . Ohio was one of the GOP's top Senate pickup targets, given the state's steady shift to the right. That continued tonight. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the former swing-state by eight points in 2020. Tonight, with about 90% of the vote in, Mr. Trump is up by 12 points. Unless Nebraska Sen. \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2  loses her re-election, the Senate is now firmly in the hands of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Strassel is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Potomac Watch" column.\par \par \uc2 Allysia Finley\u8212\'20\'14 11:21 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Exit polls show \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  roughly splitting the union vote in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania. This may reflect the fact that members of building trades unions tend to be more conservative. There are also divisions within government employees, since police and firefighter unions are more likely to support Republicans, whereas teachers unions are Democratic stalwarts. Mr. Trump may have cleaned up among union members in the building trades and public safety, while losing among other government and service unions.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Finley is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Life Science" column.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Hennessey\u8212\'20\'14 11:20 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Your sense of what's happening tonight probably depends on which channel you're watching.\par \par \uc2 On MSNBC, the mood is trending grim. Near 11 p.m., the talking heads are reaching for rationalizations. \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  says that Ted Cruz's re-election victory can be explained, in part, by the fact that Texas is a "deeply voter suppressed" state. Rachel Maddow reminds viewers that "not a single swing state has been called yet." Nicole Wallace underscores that a "landslide victory" for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  "was always a fantasy."\par \par \uc2 On CNN, the anchors labor to project an air of analytical rigor. John King works his board. Dana Bash and Audie Cornish try to determine whether Latinos in Allentown, Pa., will punish \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for a comedian's comments about Puerto Rico during last week's rally at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . They nod their heads and agree it's hard to tell from the data we have so far.\par \par \uc2 On Fox, cautious optimism reigns. Brit Hume says that if Ms. Harris manages to win, it will be "an amazing come-from-behind victory." Kellyanne Conway says the Harris campaign will lose because it put all its hopes in the idea that "demography is destiny." Former Democratic Rep. \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2  says that no matter what happens Democrats will have to "rethink" how they speak to minority voters.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Hennessey is the Journal's deputy editorial features editor.\par \par \uc2 Barton Swaim\u8212\'20\'14 10:16 p.m.\par \par \uc2 A result that may portend a larger shift in the Hispanic vote: Miami-Dade County has plumped for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with 55% to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's 44%, per the latest data. That county hasn't gone for a Republican presidential candidate since 1988. Further evidence, if any were needed, that the GOP "autopsy" report after \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 's loss in 2012\u8212\'20\'14which claimed, in part, that Republicans had to move to the left on immigration in order to attract Hispanic votes\u8212\'20\'14was stupendously wrong.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Swaim is a Journal editorial page writer.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Hennessey\u8212\'20\'14 10:12 p.m.\par \par \uc2 In New York, a ballot proposition to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state constitution has passed. The amendment will also expand the language of the state constitution's Equal Protection Clause to include "sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy outcomes, and reproductive healthcare and autonomy." The result is bad news for social conservatives in the Empire State, where Democratic Attorney General Letitia James has sicced prosecutors on pro-life pregnancy centers and counties that have tried to keep boys out of girls' sports.\par \par \uc2 As the Journal's Nicole Ault wrote {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/new-yorks-dangerous-proposition-1-civil-liberties-religious-freedom-gender-abortion-e4f5dc48"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over the weekend}}}\uc2 , "It isn't hard to imagine other prosecutions under Proposal 1: Doctors who object to performing abortions or transgender surgeries, pharmacists who refuse to dispense puberty blockers, professors who would rather not use a student's 'preferred pronouns,' or domestic-violence shelters whose purpose is to keep female victims safe from men." Such efforts will now have a constitutional basis.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Hennessey is the Journal's deputy editorial features editor.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Hennessey\u8212\'20\'14 10:03 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Florida's Amendment Four, which would have created a state constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has failed. While more Floridians voted "Yes" than "No," with the figures right now at about 57% to 43%, the measure fell short of the 60% support necessary to become law. That means that the Sunshine State's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban stands.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  worked hard to defeat Amendment Four, and the result is a feather in his cap. It's a major loss for Florida Democrats, who hoped voters would be willing to "codify" the legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime of Roe v. Wade in state law.\par \par \uc2 On MSNBC tonight, a panel led by host Rachel Maddow seemed convinced that the amendment's failure will cripple Florida's economy. They suggested the state will become a no-go zone for global companies with female employees. Obviously nobody knows what the future holds, but the Florida growth machine under Mr. DeSantis has proved remarkably resilient. The state's economy has grown significantly faster than the national average over the past five years, and that includes since the Dobbs decision that overturned Roe. The state's low corporate income tax and lack of an individual income tax are attractive to companies and workers alike. Also the weather is pretty nice.\par \par \uc2 Unless any of that stuff changes, color me skeptical that Amendment Four will have any real effect on Florida's economic future. Even \uc2 Disney\uc2  ultimately decided it preferred to stay.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Hennessey is the Journal's deputy editorial features editor.\par \par \uc2 Kim Strassel\u8212\'20\'14 9:40 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has just called the most expensive Senate race in the nation, and it looks like Democrats blew another small fortune while failing to flip the Lone Star State. Texas Sen. Ted Cruz keeps his seat, defeating former \uc2 NFL\uc2  linebacker and Democratic Rep. \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 . Axios reported that more than $165 million was spent on the race by the two sides, enough to buy nearly 25 million Double Meat Whataburgers. Note that $100 million of that was spent on Mr. Allred's behalf, and it still didn't land Senate Democrats their best prospect for a pickup in this year's cycle.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Strassel is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Potomac Watch" column.\par \par \uc2 Allysia Finley\u8212\'20\'14 9:10 p.m.\par \par \uc2 NBC News's Georgia exit poll shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doing better than he did in 2020 across various demographic groups. He's winning 42% of Latinos, five points higher than last time, according to that year's NBC exit poll.\par \par \uc2 He's winning white voters by 43 percentage points, up from 39 points in 2020. He's also losing women by 7 points instead of 9 points. Also notable, Mr. Trump is running nearly even with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  among voters with bachelor's degrees, although he trails by 62% to 37% among those with advanced degrees. Some national polls have found that people with B.A.s vote more similarly to those without college degrees than those with graduate diplomas. If the exit polls reflect actual voting, it's hard to see Mr. Trump losing Georgia.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Finley is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Life Science" column.\par \par \uc2 Tunku Varadarajan\u8212\'20\'14 9:01 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could win the White House the same way he did in 2016: by carrying the Electoral College even while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  earns more votes nationwide. If that happens, another cry will go up: Get rid of the Electoral College!\par \par \uc2 I asked Harvey Mansfield for help in explaining why the Electoral College is an impeccable constitutional device. Mr. Mansfield is professor emeritus of government at Harvard, and he's virtually the only conservative in his department. Here's what he said:\par \par \uc2 "The fundamental principle is that there is a difference between what makes democracy more democratic and what makes democracy work well. The U.S. is a large country with much diversity that is recognized in our constitution with its provisions for federalism. Power to the states allows a majority to be composed that represents the American whole as it is divided into parts, rather than the whole as homogeneous."\par \par \uc2 "Without the electoral college the U.S. would be ruled by the populous cities. Elections would focus on them, divisions would be sharper. Separation of powers and federalism are the twin pillars of the 'auxiliary precautions' that The Federalist declared to be necessary to a large republic in addition to elections. There are people in the U.S. who want to get rid of the electoral college because they foolishly ignore the principle stated above."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mansfield added that the best recent defense of the Electoral College that he has read is by Allen Guelzo, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalaffairs.com/publications/detail/in-defense-of-the-electoral-college"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  in National Affairs that "the idea that a national popular vote would lead to clearer and more representative results ignores the nature of our constitutional republic and fails to contemplate the challenges that a truly national election in our vast country would involve."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Varadarajan, a Journal contributor, is a fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  and at New York University Law School's Classical Liberal Institute.\par \par \uc2 Kim Strassel\u8212\'20\'14 8:31 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Florida is a master vote counter (other states, please take note), meaning the Associated Press has already been able to call an important race for a Republican incumbent that Democrats had hoped to defeat with their "Red to Blue" program to retake the House.\par \par \uc2 Only last week, the chatter was that Democratic challenger Whitney Fox was tied or leading GOP Rep. Anna Paulina Luna in Florida's 13th congressional district. Ms. Fox also consistently outraised Ms. Luna. Yet AP has already called the race for the Republican. With 93% of the vote counted, she's beating Ms. Fox handily, 55% to 45%.\par \par \uc2 It's more evidence that the GOP is having another strong night in Florida, as Sen. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  also romped to re-election victory, and with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  putting up stronger numbers than in 2020.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Strassel is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Potomac Watch" column.\par \par \uc2 Tunku Varadarajan\u8212\'20\'14 8:02 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Definitive proof that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a New Yorker arrived this afternoon in the form of his latest ad on X (formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 In New York, people stay "on," and not "in," line. Will they get his drift in those swing states where they speak a different language?\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Varadarajan, a Journal contributor, is a fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  and at New York University Law School's Classical Liberal Institute.\par \par \uc2 Kim Strassel\u8212\'20\'14 7:58 p.m.\par \par \uc2 With networks calling the Senate race in West Virginia for Republican \uc2 Jim Justice\uc2  (a flip from Democrat Joe Manchin), Republicans are a step closer to retaking the chamber. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's position as majority leader now rests on either: a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  victory, a 50-50 Senate, and a tie-breaking vote from Vice President \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 ; or an unexpected Democratic Senate pickup in Florida, Texas or Nebraska. If Republicans win the Montana seat, even those scenarios might be out.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Strassel is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Potomac Watch" column.\par \par \uc2 Tunku Varadarajan\u8212\'20\'14 7:48 p.m.\par \par \uc2 The most eye-catching\u8212\'20\'14and constructive\u8212\'20\'14recent observations on the election come from Nigel Farage, a man often described glibly (and erroneously) as an English Trump. Mr. Farage, a member of Parliament and leader of the more-conservative-than-the-Tories Reform UK party, said in a Cincinnatus-like interview with the Daily Telegraph that if Mr. Trump loses tonight, he should move on gracefully and not make a ruckus.\par \par \uc2 "I have never gone along with the stolen election narrative," Mr. Farage said. "Let's hope and pray that is not an issue this time. If it [the outcome] was clear then Republicans have to accept the result. The whole point of voting is we don't need to fight. It is what we fought two world wars for. We settle our differences with the ballot box." Mr. Farage added that if Mr. Trump loses, instead of mounting a rebellion he should "go and play golf at Turnberry."\par \par \uc2 The English politician had some advice for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as well. "If she gets in on Tuesday I hope she pardons him," he said, referring to the criminal cases against Mr. Trump. "She could look magnanimous and it would dampen down potential tensions."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Farage has posted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sky.com/video/nigel-farage-heads-to-mar-a-lago-to-join-donald-trump-on-election-night-13249142"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a video of himself}}}\uc2 en route to Mar-a-Lago. One assumes he'll repeat his civic message to Mr. Trump in person.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Varadarajan, a Journal contributor, is a fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  and at New York University Law School's Classical Liberal Institute.\par \par \uc2 Allysia Finley\u8212\'20\'14 7:27 p.m.\par \par \uc2 NBC News's exit poll reports that 45% of voters said they "are financially worse off now than they were four years ago," higher than in any presidential race since at least 2008, when the figure was 42% after that year's financial panic. This ought to prompt some reflection among liberals who have tried to diminish voter concerns about inflation and an uneven job market. Their condescension and deflections, such as blaming inflation on corporate greed, have only added political insult to economic injury.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Finley is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Life Science" column.\par \par \uc2 Kim Strassel\u8212\'20\'14 7:16 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Fox News is reporting that its Georgia exit poll shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning 25% of black men, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  running about six points behind President Biden among black women. One big unknown is relative turnout (i.e., if women are turning out in greater numbers than men). Still, noteworthy numbers.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Strassel is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Potomac Watch" column.\par \par \uc2 William McGurn\u8212\'20\'14 7:10 p.m.\par \par \uc2 In ancient Rome they read entrails to try to divine the future. We do the same today, only we call them polls. Now that the election is upon us, though, the commentators are less categorical about what they suggest.\par \par \uc2 Already there are voices saying we won't know the presidential winner tonight. In the richest and most technologically advanced democracy in the world, this should be an embarrassment. It can be done. Florida went from a national joke in 2000\u8212\'20\'14remember hanging chads?\u8212\'20\'14to arguably the best vote-counting system in the nation. There is no good reason other states couldn't get it done, too.\par \par \uc2 My sympathies are with all the TV commentators, who have to fill the air time for a few hours before hard results start coming in.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. McGurn is a member of the Journal's editorial board. He writes the weekly "Main Street" column.\par \par \uc2 Allysia Finley\u8212\'20\'14 7:01 p.m.\par \par \uc2 One potential tailwind for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 : The inflation burden hasn't fallen equally across the U.S., and housing costs have gone up much more in battleground Sun Belt states. Over the past five years, housing prices have risen 72.7% in Arizona, 70.6% in Georgia, and 74.7% in North Carolina, according to the \uc2 Federal Housing Finance Agency\uc2 . Those increases are among the largest in the country and significantly greater than in states like Massachusetts (56.2%), Illinois (48.4%) and New York (59.4%). What links the housing trends in those two groups of states? The Sun Belt has experienced a population boom, owing to all the U-Hauls leaving California and the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Finley is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Life Science" column.\par \par \uc2 Collin Levy\u8212\'20\'146:24 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Everyone tuning in now will be poring over the early exit polls, but that's a seriously limited predictive exercise. Yes, exit polls have the advantage of recording the opinions of people who actually voted (unlike nearly all other polls). The difficulty is capturing an accurate cross-section of the overall voter pool, since some people (like retired voters, college students, or stay-at-home parents) are more likely to cast ballots during the day.\par \par \uc2 NBC reported at 5 p.m. Eastern time that its exit poll showed 35% of voters said their top concern was "democracy," and 31% said the economy. "Two in 10 women said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was most important," the network added. If that seems low, it's probably because a large slice of the female professional class, a group likely to feel strongly about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will vote after work. The exit-poll figures shift throughout the night as more information comes in, but the early numbers get the most attention and stick in people's minds.\par \par \uc2 Given the polarization of this election, I'm also curious how the actual voting results will match the scene on the ground in certain areas. In Chicago's suburban Lake County, for instance, Harris-Walz yard signs were running roughly 30 to 1 against Trump-Vance yard signs, by my unofficial tally. But that belies the actual partisan balance from the past two elections, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won a bit under 40% of the vote each time. In 2016 Hillary Clinton took 57% of the Lake County vote to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 37%, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did only a few points better in 2020. Decisive, to be sure, but not as decisive as the yard signs.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Levy is a member of the Journal's editorial board.\par \par \uc2 Kim Strassel\u8212\'20\'14 6:19 p.m.\par \par \uc2 If elections were settled on the basis of policy alone, the AP Votecast survey would suggest \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a tough lift tonight. That survey of more than 110,000 voters finds nearly 40% say the economy (inflation) is their top concern, while another 20% cite immigration.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ranked No. 1 for only 11% of voters, and climate change for only 7%. Even more concerning for President Biden's desired heir: 8 in 10 voters said they'd like to see at least "substantial change."\par \par \uc2 Luckily for Ms. Harris, it looks like personality is also on the ballot. AP reports: "Nearly 6 in 10 are very or somewhat concerned that another Trump term would bring the U.S. closer to being an authoritarian country." And when asked what most influenced their vote, about half of voters identified "the future of democracy" (though that could cut against either candidate).\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ms. Strassel is a member of the Journal's editorial board. She writes the weekly "Potomac Watch" column.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Hennessey\u8212\'20\'14 6:01 p.m.\par \par \uc2 One thing is always true of political campaigns: The line between clever and stupid is invisible. Winners come away looking like geniuses (sometimes evil geniuses). Losers look like dopes. In an election this close, the ball could bounce either way.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wins, the press will shower her strategists and staff with praise. How smart they were to keep her away from the press. What a cool idea to put her on "Call Her Daddy" and "Saturday Night Live." What foresight to know that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the key issue on which to run.\par \par \uc2 If she loses, expect an absolute savaging of that same strategy.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  win will prompt a full media meltdown, much of it a lament on the enduring power of cynicism in our politics. His campaign ads successfully divided the electorate, the analysts will say. His cabal of advisers knew just how to stoke the misogyny and racism of his mostly male and mostly white voters and get them to the polls. Sadly, negative beats positive every time.\par \par \uc2 If he loses, well, you may not be able to hear anything over the massive sighs of relief coming out of your TV screen. Oz the Great and Powerful has finally been exposed.\par \par \uc2 For the voters and the candidates, elections are binary\u8212\'20\'14all or nothing. It's the same for the campaign strategists. You're either a hero or a goat. No in-between.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Hennessey is the Journal's deputy editorial features editor.\par \par \uc2 Kyle Peterson \u8212\'20\'14 5:52 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Democrats who are anxiously awaiting the first tranche of official results might be wondering if their party made a mistake by dumping President Biden and substituting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The answer is no, at least according to a \uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll conducted over the weekend of 1,103 Americans.\par \par \uc2 The survey has Ms. Harris leading Mr. Trump nationally, 49% to 46%. But it gives Mr. Trump a solid lead in a hypothetical race against Mr. Biden, 48% to 41%. Five percent of Harris supporters said that with Mr. Biden on the ballot, they wouldn't vote at all, and another 5% weren't sure.\par \par \uc2 It's counterfactual history, so who knows, but this fits my intuition. Mr. Biden is two weeks shy of his 82nd birthday. His demeanor during his single debate against Mr. Trump reminded too many voters of their own aging relatives. He dismissed that performance as a "bad night," but Ms. Harris hasn't exactly asked him to go barnstorm the swing states. After Mr. Biden dialed into a get-out-the-vote call last week, he had to clarify that he hadn't intended to refer to Trump supporters as "garbage."\par \par \uc2 Whether Ms. Harris wins or loses today, Mr. Biden did his party and his country a disservice by trying to stay in office until age 86.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Peterson is a member of the Journal's editorial board.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Hennessey\u8212\'20\'14 5:09 p.m.\par \par \uc2 This Election Day tweet says so much about the progressive worldview. It's all climate crisis, all the time. And they say Republicans trade on fear?\par \par \uc2 Personally, I'd be more sympathetic to the view that hot days in early November call for urgent intervention if the proposed solutions didn't map so neatly over the rest of the Democratic Party's agenda. The political uses of the perpetual apocalypse are limitless.\par \par \uc2 AOC's flair for the dramatic calls to mind \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's grandiose claim in 2008 that his nomination would mark "the moment when the rise of the oceans began to slow and our planet began to heal." Get over yourselves guys.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Mr. Hennessey is the Journal's Deputy Editorial Features Editor.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020241105ekb50086h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
