\par \par \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , president of Students for Life Action. PHOTO: Desiree Rios for WSJ\par \par \uc2 Few have agonized over it more than Hawkins. At 39 years old, she has been leading her organization for nearly two decades, after skipping a grade in high school and giving up a dream of becoming an aeronautical engineer. She is brash and sometimes profane in describing the fight. When three female Republican legislators in South Carolina were contemplating voting against a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban there, Hawkins had students deliver them plastic spines to send the message she wanted them to "grow a spine" on the issue. When the three women ultimately voted against the bill, the group campaigned against them in a primary and successfully defeated them, sending them moving boxes as a final dig. "We're pretty f\u8212\'20\'14ing tough with these legislators," she said.\par \par \uc2 She knows the battle will long outlast Trump's time at the head of the party.\par \par \uc2 Hawkins plans to vote for Trump in hopes that the doors of the White House will be open to her if he wins. "Do I particularly love having to say I'm voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  publicly when he does stupid things constantly? No, it's hard to have to say that," she said. But she indicated that unlike some in the movement, she could live with it for what she thought was the greater good: "My vote isn't about me. It's not about making myself look like the most moral or righteous person, like I'm standing on principle and letting it all burn."\par \par \uc2 Lila Rose\uc2 , a 36-year-old antiabortion leader who has built one of the movement's largest social-media followings by publishing disturbing depictions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is taking a harder line. She has publicly wrestled with whether to vote for Trump, saying he and the GOP "have gutted their platform on life." She said she reluctantly decided to vote for Trump in solidarity with voters in swing states, without telling her supporters to do the same.\par \par \uc2 "I think Trump understands and the campaign understands that there's a whole group of people that helped him get elected in 2016 and were very excited. He's now throwing this movement under the bus," Rose said. The party is "increasingly hostile to its own base."\par \par \uc2 Trump spokeswoman Karoline Leavitt said, "President Trump has been consistent and clear: the issue of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/abortion-donald-trump-2024-election-fe641cc5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  should be decided by the people in their respective states}}}\uc2 , not by the federal government in D.C."\par \par \uc2 Popular resistance\par \par \uc2 Before a decade ago, the antiabortion movement never would have considered Trump a standard-bearer for its cause, but the relationship flourished in those years.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Carney, chief executive of the antiabortion group 40 Days for Life, met with Trump in 2015, at a time when his peers were deeply skeptical of the thrice-married real-estate developer who once described himself as "very pro-choice."\par \par \uc2 Carney got a call from a friend asking if he would entertain Trump for several hours before a speaking event at a high school in Houston. He didn't have high hopes for the meeting, saying he was expecting "a huge jerk."\par \par \uc2 Carney said he was pleasantly surprised when Trump's car pulled up and it was just him, \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2  and a security guard, signaling to Carney that Trump wouldn't be controlled by advisers. Trump clearly had limited knowledge of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Carney said, "I was convinced he could be influenced towards our side of the issue."\par \par \uc2 For months Carney felt alone in that view among his peers. He recalls that at a private event for national antiabortion leaders, one of them stood up and uttered an acronym that seemed to reflect the broader sentiment there: "ABD," anyone but Donald.\par \par \uc2 "I spent a lot of time convincing people in 2015 and 2016 that Trump would do what he said on the pro-life issue," Carney said.\par \par \uc2 Trump made other inroads. He hired Kellyanne Conway, a Catholic who is antiabortion, as a campaign manager. His campaign released a list of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/donald-trump-releases-names-of-11-potential-supreme-court-choices-1463598588"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 potential Supreme Court nominees}}}\uc2  that assured social conservatives his victory would create a real prospect for Roe's demise. In a debate with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in Las Vegas he accused Democrats of supporting laws that would allow babies to be ripped from the womb in the final month of pregnancy, the kind of graphic description that GOP politicians traditionally shied away from.\par \par \uc2 Trump's Supreme Court appointments were one of his signature White House achievements. A little over a year ago, he called himself "the most pro-life president" in U.S. history. Antiabortion leaders agreed.\par \par \uc2 During the first weeks after Roe was overturned in June 2022, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents were optimistic about significantly reducing the roughly one million annual abortions in the U.S. But warning signs flashed early, beginning with a vote that August in which Kansans overwhelmingly rejected a measure to eliminate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives said they were caught off guard by the depth of popular resistance to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and their opponents' use of ballot measures as a tactic to override conservative legislatures that wanted to limit it.\par \par \uc2 The ballot-initiative fights have been "sort of a mess," said \uc2 Rick Santorum\uc2 , a former GOP senator from Pennsylvania and a member of the board for Students for Life. The antiabortion movement "didn't have the resources. They didn't do their job in waging the battle."\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups, who have been reliant on a handful of large donors in these fights, are significantly outfunded by the opposing side in most of the 10 state referendum battles they are fighting this year. Many donors have come to see them as a losing cause, according to people involved in the campaigns. That view was cemented last year when Ohio voters passed a constitutional amendment protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Concord Fund, a conservative group with ties to the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 's \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , spent more than $20 million in the Ohio campaign, according to public filings. The antiabortion movement suffered a major blow when the group, disappointed with the loss, pulled back significantly from the current election cycle. The Concord Fund has spent several million dollars fighting a Florida ballot initiative but is sitting out many other state-level battles, according to a person familiar with the matter. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
