\par \par \uc2 Hawkins, right, speaks to supporters of Students for Life. PHOTO: Desiree Rios for WSJ\par \par \uc2 The antiabortion movement's troubles on ballot initiatives have reached even deep-red Missouri, which has a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with only limited exceptions for medical emergencies. The ballot measure would constitutionally protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through fetal viability, or about halfway through a typical pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14although opponents argue that the language is so broad it would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the end of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights side in Missouri has raised some $30 million, while the antiabortion campaign has raised only about $5 million after a multimillion-dollar donor got cold feet at the last minute, prompting other matching donors to pull out. Cassidy Anderson, who is helping lead the antiabortion campaign, said donors throughout much of the campaign "thought that this was not a winnable issue."\par \par \uc2 Anderson said donors have become more optimistic in the final days about the potential for success. Her political-action committee on Tuesday received a roughly $1 million donation from the Concord Fund, according to public filings.\par \par \uc2 Backing off nationally\par \par \uc2 The movement's struggles are more pronounced on the national stage, where Republicans, led by the Trump campaign, have backed off almost entirely from endorsing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America President Marjorie Dannenfelser, an antiabortion activist who has cultivated a close relationship with Trump, staked her post-Roe ambitions on a national 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limit, which she has presented as a realistic goal. But it would require Congressional legislation, and Trump has thwarted that by refusing to back the effort.\par \par \uc2 Early in the year Trump publicly toyed with the idea of endorsing such a ban, but in April said he wouldn't endorse any national limit. The issue, he said, should be decided by the states.\par \par \uc2 "We had hoped and expected for something different," Dannenfelser said. Trump blasted her by name on social media, saying she was "making it impossible" for Republicans to win elections.\par \par \uc2 Things came to a head again this summer during the drafting of the Republican platform, which many in the antiabortion movement saw as a chance to chart the GOP's post-Roe future. The final document stripped out virtually any mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, for the first time in more than four decades, didn't call for a constitutional amendment to protect unborn children.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of antiabortion groups and social conservatives, including former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , were publicly angry. "For the first time in decades the Republican Platform retreats on life. Pro-life Americans are rightly outraged and gravely concerned," the groups wrote in a letter to the convention's delegates.\par \par \uc2 Platform committee attendees said Trump allies ran a tightly controlled process in which the committee was locked in a room and their cellphones were taken and placed in bags rendering them inoperable. When committee members raised objections they were told to write them down, a process that effectively silenced debate. After about an hour, they took a vote.\par \par \uc2 "They silenced people who were opposed to their agenda," \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2  President \uc2 Tony Perkins\uc2 , who was in the room, said in a \uc2 C-Span\uc2  interview.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2 , who chaired the platform committee, said "President Trump personally reviewed and approved a platform that is of, by, and for the people\u8212\'20\'14not special interests." She added the "Republican Party proudly stands for families and life."\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion leaders remain hopeful that if Trump is elected he will be more receptive to their position, so despite the setbacks they are keeping the lines of communication open. They hope that once freed from the pressure of needing to get elected, Trump will take actions that may be less politically visible than a national ban, such as starving federal funding for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and rolling back access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills via mail.\par \par \uc2 At times, when things have gotten tense between Hawkins and the Trump campaign, she said strings of text messages go unanswered and she wonders if his team has simply stopped listening.\par \par \uc2 After one such silent period, Hawkins learned that she had a last-minute opportunity to secure an advertising spot during the vice presidential debate. She scrambled to raise more than $160,000 in four hours to run an ad accusing \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , the Democratic vice presidential candidate, of signing legislation as Minnesota governor that allowed babies who survive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be left to die. (Walz denies this.)\par \par \uc2 She and her advisers texted the ad to their Trump campaign contacts, hoping Sen. \uc2 JD Vance\uc2 , Walz's vice presidential opponent, would echo the talking points in the debate. She also said they wanted to show the campaign that "we're not enemies," and that they were pouring their own resources into a message they believed could help Trump win the election.\par \par \uc2 It prompted a response from her contacts, who broke their silence and said they had shown it to co-campaign manager Chris LaCivita.\par \par \uc2 That evening, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/vance-walz-vice-presidential-debate-election-2024/card/trump-commits-to-vetoing-national-abortion-ban-u9gIefKceH3sBSKrry4V"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump announced on social media that he would veto a national \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2 , another blow to the movement. But during the debate, Vance repeated the ad's accusations about babies being allowed to die. For Hawkins, it was a win.\par \par \uc2 Jess Bravin and Jennifer Calfas contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020241101ekb100004\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
