\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Gender Gap Is Defining Feature of Deadlocked Trump-Harris Race; Split between male and female voters cuts across racial and economic lines\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey, Aaron Zitner and Xavier Martinez \par \uc2 1,437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2024\par 18:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is struggling to win support from men. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has the same problem with women.\par \par \uc2 The gender gap has come to define a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-harris-election-win-paths-5ac8cd97"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deadlocked presidential race}}}\uc2 , with a galvanized group of women voting for Harris because of her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and Trump wooing men with uber-masculine rhetoric. The split has affected the candidates' media strategies and how they frame the issues most important to voters in the final weeks of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 While a divide between the sexes has become a fixture of modern elections, it appears to have broadened since 2020, cutting across many racial, educational and economic groups. Trump's 5-point advantage among men in the 2020 election has widened to 10 points in The Wall Street Journal's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-harris-poll-august-election-2024-e6fa024e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most recent national poll}}}\uc2 , in late August. President Biden's 12-point edge among women in 2020 has become a 13-point lead for Harris.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Longwell, executive director of Republican Voters Against Trump, called 2024 the most gendered election she had seen, particularly among younger voters. "You don't want it to become boys vs girls," she said. "You don't want to be in a political environment where your gender is the number one factor you are voting on."\par \par \uc2 Trump appeared at a town-hall event before an all-female audience on Fox News this week, and Harris on Monday rolled out an "{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/harris-trump-black-men-election-2024-3d07ca3f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opportunity agenda}}}\uc2 " for Black men, including business loans, job training and health initiatives. She did a town hall Tuesday with radio host Charlamagne tha God, who is popular with young Black men. Harris also has gone on podcasts and the Howard Stern show and will be interviewed by Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Polls show little evidence such overtures are working. In fact, with Black and Latino men moving toward Trump, the polls suggest that America is becoming a bit less divided politically by race and more divided by gender.\par \par \uc2 "I think men are very heavily economy-focused," said David Lee, lead pollster for the pro-Trump MAGA Inc. super political-action committee. "It all has to do with the economy and inflation."\par \par \uc2 While women also care about the economy, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-claims-women-will-no-longer-be-thinking-about-abortion-if-he-wins-efcf6c7c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  weighs equally heavy}}}\uc2  as an issue in their voting calculations. In a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-harris-swing-state-poll-october-2024-c3ca9414"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal poll}}}\uc2  of the seven battleground states, 27% of women\u8212\'20\'14but 8% of men\u8212\'20\'14listed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the top issue motivating their vote for president. The finding reflects the emphasis female voters have placed on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022. One third of women said they couldn't back a candidate who disagrees with them on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  compared with 18% of men who said so.\par \par \uc2 The second woman to be a major party's nominee\u8212\'20\'14and the first woman of color\u8212\'20\'14Harris mostly avoids overt references to her gender or race, in contrast with her lone female predecessor \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who donned suffragist white in big moments. Still, Harris's embrace of music and dancing is popular with the women who attend her rallies, and she has leaned into a set of issues that connect with women, emphasizing her support for the "care economy" and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Harris has excoriated Trump for appointing three of the justices who overturned Roe. "It's clear that they just don't trust women," Harris said of Trump and Republicans during remarks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights last month in Atlanta. "Well, we trust women."\par \par \uc2 Such priorities appeal to voters such as Bri Ortega, 27 years old, of Glendale, Ariz., who said she was supporting Harris in part because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. She said she appreciated the family policies Harris had laid out, because, "I really like people having open avenues to living their life how they want, either having a family or not having a family, and being able to plan out when they would like to have one." \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal7200
