\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Is Ted Cruz Blowing His Re-Election Race? Facing a too-close-for-comfort contest, Texas Republican mixes bipartisanship with red meat for his base\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Findell \par \uc2 1,677 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2024\par 15:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PLANO, Texas\u8212\'20\'14In the suburbs north of Dallas, as in most of America, everyone has an opinion on Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 "He's a man of integrity and intelligence," 86-year-old Plano resident James Guthrie said of the junior GOP senator from Texas.\par \par \uc2 Local tech worker Cory Lewis, 40, had the opposite reaction. "I would vote for anything over Ted Cruz\u8212\'20\'14a rock or a potato," Lewis said. "He's a terrible person."\par \par \uc2 This year, amid a re-election race Republicans consider too close for comfort, the fiery Cruz is trying to move past his reputation for being unlikable and convince swing voters in the state's rapidly growing and increasingly competitive suburbs that he is a substantive statesman. Democrats have poured money into the coffers of Cruz's challenger, Rep. \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 , a former \uc2 NFL\uc2  football player and civil-rights attorney.\par \par \uc2 Cruz has been a household name since running for president in 2016. In Texas, he is known to fans for his staunchly conservative bona fides and to detractors for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/sen-ted-cruz-to-return-from-mexico-trip-11613674334"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fleeing to Canc\u250\'00\'FAn, Mexico,}}}\uc2  during a 2021 freeze that crippled the state. In Washington, he has long had a mixed reputation with colleagues. Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R., S.C.) once joked at a dinner with journalists: "If you killed Ted Cruz on the floor of the Senate, and the trial was in the Senate, nobody would convict you."\par \par \uc2 The race won't just turn on Cruz's popularity, or lack of it. It is also the latest test of whether Texas is becoming a purple state\u8212\'20\'14in the vein of Georgia and North Carolina\u8212\'20\'14or still leans red. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
