\par\par \uc2 Health News\par \uc2 Health\par \b \uc2 A Young Doctor's Dilemma: Stay Home or Move Where  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is Protected? Residency programs in states where  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  is restricted are drawing fewer applicants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas | Photographs by Kaiti Sullivan for WSJ \par \uc2 1,170 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2024\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 INDIANAPOLIS\u8212\'20\'14Dr. Kyle Baugh has a choice to make.\par \par \uc2 In the penultimate year of her residency program in obstetrics and gynecology at Indiana University School of Medicine, the lifelong Hoosier is weighing where to build a career: East Coast or West Coast? Chicago, New York or San Diego?\par \par \uc2 Indiana, where Baugh seldom misses an Indy 500 and completed her undergraduate and medical-school degrees, is off her list. The Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 removal of constitutional protections for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  in 2022 and Indiana's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023 recast her life plans. Indiana is among more than a dozen states to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlaw or restrict the procedure}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "To me, this is basic healthcare," said Baugh, 33 years old.\par \par \uc2 Applications to OB-GYN residency programs in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have fallen. Having fewer doctors in these states could exacerbate shortages of caretakers in poorer, rural swathes of the country that overlap considerably with new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, doctors said.\par \par \uc2 OB-GYN residency programs in states that restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have established partnerships for their young doctors to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-abortion-law-changes-upend-medical-training-programs-11665020346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 learn the procedure in states}}}\uc2  where it remains legal. The residents spend weeks at a time on the rotations. The \uc2 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education\uc2  requires OB-GYN programs to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training or help residents get it elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Politics will shape whether those arrangements last {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/new-mexico-abortion-clinics-demand-1a888fa7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as state \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  laws shift}}}\uc2 . States including Florida and Missouri will vote in November on whether to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in their constitutions, while other states have sought to curb access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from across state lines. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-kamala-harris-election-2024-92511caa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is a top issue}}}\uc2  in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 "We can't count on stability of access or training," said Dr. Abigail Cutler, an OB-GYN at \uc2 UW Health\uc2  and professor at the University of Wisconsin School of Medicine and Public Health. Despite a court ruling that a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/wisconsin-abortion-clinics-prepare-to-resume-appointments-ed5a753e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1849 state law doesn't ban abortions}}}\uc2 , \uc2 UW Health\uc2 's residency program sends residents to an academic medical center in Illinois for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training in case Wisconsin's laws change.\par \par \uc2 Baugh applied to Indiana for her residency in part because it was one of about 100 programs that focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training. Now, Indiana is among about the fifth of OB-GYN residency programs that are in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Indiana University\uc2 's health system is considering stipends and higher pay to keep residents in the state after their four-year training, said Dr. Nicole Scott, director of the OB-GYN residency program.\par \par \uc2 "Sometimes, it's not enough for people to want to stay," Scott said.\par \par \uc2 Matters of state\par \par \uc2 When she sees pregnant patients in Indiana, Baugh said she first asks whether their pregnancy is desired. The law doesn't restrict doctors from telling patients about options to end a pregnancy outside the state.\par \par \uc2 Indiana's residency program partnered with Planned Parenthood of Illinois for residents and faculty to travel there for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training. In 2023, Baugh spent five weeks performing abortions at a clinic in central Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Residents drive there from Indianapolis, spend the night and drive back each week. Practicing there is a relief compared with the constraints in Indiana, she said.\par \par \uc2 Recently, inside the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic in Illinois where Baugh and her peers train, Dr. Emma Wittman used a tube to remove fetal tissue from her patient's uterus.\par \par \uc2 "Just take some nice, deep breaths, into your nose and out of your mouth," Wittman, a second-year resident at Indiana, told the patient.\par \par \uc2 Indiana's second-year OB-GYN residents often complete more than a dozen abortions a day during their five-week rotation in Illinois, learning from faculty including Dr. Caitlin Bernard, an Indiana OB-GYN.\par \par \uc2 The Indianapolis Star reported days after the Supreme Court's 2022 ruling that Bernard had performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a 10-year-old who had been raped. Indiana's attorney general {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/indiana-abortion-doctor-fined-for-violating-privacy-of-10-year-old-patient-94e423f5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought a case}}}\uc2  against Bernard, arguing she violated patient privacy laws by discussing the girl's case. The state medical board fined her $3,000.\par \par \uc2 Bernard had security guards protecting her and her family for weeks. Two years later, she wears scrubs when she arrives at the clinic in Illinois and tells residents to be proud of their work.\par \par \uc2 "I don't want it to be a secret," Bernard said. "I'm not going to live that way."\par \par \uc2 Places to go\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's 2022 decision and the scrutiny on Bernard galvanized Baugh and her fellow residents. Several of them got tattoos of wire coat hangers\u8212\'20\'14a symbol of the unsafe and illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  methods common decades ago.\par \par \uc2 Baugh worries about the tradeoff between working in a place where she can practice without constraints, such as California or New York, or a place like Indiana where the need is greater.\par \par \uc2 "I, at least for a period of time, need to be in a place where that's not such a fight," Baugh said.\par \par \uc2 At an Indianapolis taco spot recently, Baugh, Dr. Komal Kumar and Dr. Prakrithi Srinand vented about everything from the highs and lows of labor-and-delivery rotations to how their hospital-issued scrubs fit. They also talked about the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 After Srinand and Kumar complete two-year family-planning fellowships in New York and Washington, respectively, they hope to practice in their home states.\par \par \uc2 Srinand, a fourth-year resident, wants to return to Minnesota, where the state legislature was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/minnesota-with-new-democratic-control-enacts-state-abortion-rights-11675190873"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the first to pass new protections}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Supreme Court's 2022 decision.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think the future holds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the U.S.? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Kumar, another fourth-year resident, aims to return to Missouri, where the state's attorney general {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-courts-abortion-decision-sparks-immediate-action-from-states-11656096002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enacted its \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  minutes after the Supreme Court's decision. Voters there will soon consider whether to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the state's constitution. Either way, Kumar hopes to provide abortions in nearby Illinois or Kansas, driving there from Missouri.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of people might not be fully prepared for the realities that might happen," Kumar said. "But I do think we are."\par \par \uc2 Baugh has more than a year of her four-year residency to go. For a coming elective, she is considering shadowing a doctor focused on family planning in a state without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions to see what that life would look like.\par \par \uc2 It is difficult for Baugh to explain her quandary about her future to longtime friends who are getting married and raising kids in Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 "They can't understand why I'd want to move," Baugh said, "but they also can't understand what I'm doing and seeing." \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
