\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Inside the Critical Fight for the Working-Class Voter; Harris needs to win over non-college-educated voters, who have gravitated toward Trump. Notoriously unpredictable Nevada could go either way.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Findell and Andrew Restuccia | Photographs by Alejandra Rubio for WSJ \par \uc2 2,290 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2024\par 01:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LAS VEGAS\u8212\'20\'14In this electoral battleground, both parties {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/gop-cements-gains-as-the-working-class-party-across-racial-lines-cd7e3ba5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are fighting to win over}}}\uc2  voters like Juan Flores.\par \par \uc2 The 48-year-old stagehand, union member and father of eight has worked in Las Vegas casinos for decades. Like most Nevada voters, his biggest concern is the economy\u8212\'20\'14especially the cost of housing and availability of jobs. That's one reason the registered Democrat and strong backer of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who previously cast ballots for \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , is leaning toward voting for Republican \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as he has done twice before.\par \par \uc2 Flores said he doesn't like Trump's rhetoric and wouldn't want him anywhere near his daughters. But Flores, who struggled to find a new job in his 40s, thinks the former president will focus on bringing jobs back to the U.S. from overseas. Like several other voters in his working-class corner of Las Vegas, Flores said \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hadn't made a strong impression.\par \par \uc2 "I have not seen anything from her that would convince me to vote for her," he said in a recent interview at his home. He had just had a pleasant conversation with liberal canvassers who knocked on his door, praising their efforts and pledging to support a statewide referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, while staying mum about the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Harris has spent two months building a campaign centered on helping working-class families, yet there are signs her pitch hasn't resonated with some of those voters in Nevada. As much as she represents a departure from President Biden, she remains burdened by the increase in prices that occurred on his watch. Many voters here said Harris's views seem fuzzy, and that they didn't know what she stands for.\par \par \uc2 Trump has vulnerabilities of his own. Some undecided voters in the state worried he would have few guardrails in a second term, and they expressed dismay about his indictments in high-profile legal cases.\par \par \uc2 With just weeks to go before the election, GOP operatives privately worried that Harris's ground forces are outperforming Republicans in get-out-the-vote efforts in Nevada, a dynamic that could tip the closely divided state toward Democrats. Voters and organizers from both parties said Democratic volunteers outnumber pro-Trump campaigners in the state's battleground counties. An \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 -backed political-action committee supporting Trump recently fired a vendor overseeing its ground operations in the state, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Harris's performance in Nevada will help determine who ends up in the Oval Office next year. It also might show whether Democrats can win back the kind of working-class voters who have gravitated to the Republican Party in recent elections.\par \par \uc2 For a state that voted for the Democratic presidential candidate in the last four election cycles, Nevada looks the least like the rest of Democratic America, which tends to be more urban, more educated and with higher incomes. Nevada has the highest share of non-college-educated voters of any state that Democrats have a reasonable shot at winning.\par \par \uc2 Nevada is considered especially unpredictable, with unreliable polling and thousands of new voters moving into the state each year. While polls indicate Harris has made gains in Nevada since she replaced Biden on the ticket, the race remains close.\par \par \uc2 Since the last presidential race, both parties have lost registered voters in Nevada, although the Democrats have lost more. The proportion of unaffiliated voters, which Nevada calls nonpartisan voters, has jumped from 24% to 33%, and now represent the largest group in the state.\par \par \uc2 That has left both parties trying to convince persuadable voters that their candidate will be most effective in addressing the higher cost of groceries, housing and other necessities. Organizers described many of those Nevadans as particularly difficult to reach and cynical about both parties.\par \par \uc2 The lack of voter engagement risks hurting Harris, who doesn't have much more time to explain her policies and sell herself to the electorate.\par \par \uc2 Case in point: A proposal by Trump to eliminate taxes on tips resonated with Flores, who worked for 20 years as a bellman at Caesars Palace, sometimes making up to $300 a day in tips. Flores wasn't familiar with Harris's similar policy to eliminate taxes on tips for service and hospitality workers.\par \par \uc2 Voters say they already have a fixed view of Trump. His name is visible high over the Strip on the 64-story Trump International Hotel Las Vegas, and on many campaign billboards, several of them reading, "Endorsed by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 National polls have shown that a majority of voters views Trump as best able to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/harris-is-still-struggling-to-blunt-trumps-edge-on-the-economy-ddc71a36"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 manage the economy}}}\uc2 , though Harris has made up ground on the issue since she replaced President Biden on the ticket. While Trump's crass comments about women and his brash public persona have turned off some independent and undecided voters, others have said they were willing to set those things aside in hopes he will do something about high prices.\par \par \uc2 In television ads and campaign trail speeches, Harris and her allies have said that Trump's policies, including his proposal to lower the corporate tax rate, would favor big corporations over working Americans, and that the raft of tariffs he has said he would impose would amount to a "sales tax" on American consumers.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, an automatic voter registration measure that went into effect in 2020 added a large new pool of voters to the rolls when they obtained driver's licenses. They add to the large groups of "nonpartisans"\u8212\'20\'14{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nvsos.gov/sos/home/showpublisheddocument/14468/638616688551830000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 600,000}}}\uc2 as of Sept. 1\u8212\'20\'14that the campaigns see as potentially persuadable.\par \par \uc2 One day recently, canvassers went door to door in Flores's North Las Vegas neighborhood of modest homes. Organizers with the progressive group Make the Road Nevada knocked doors in the morning, followed by the Latino-focused right-leaning group Libre Initiative in the early evening.\par \par \uc2 They were fighting for the same voters, and facing the same frustrations: Unopened doors, locked gates and listed voters who had moved. The state might be won or lost based on who can win over such voters.\par \par \uc2 Economic worries\par \par \uc2 Las Vegas residents have faced several economic convulsions in recent years. The coronavirus pandemic closed the casinos, restaurants and theaters that line Vegas's strip, causing unemployment in the state to surge past 30% in 2020, the highest jobless rate in the nation at the time.\par \par \uc2 The city recovered from the pandemic, but was hit hard by inflation. At the end of 2023, Nevadans were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.helpadvisor.com/community-health/cost-of-groceries-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spending more on groceries than residents of any other state but California}}}\uc2 . While joblessness has dropped significantly, the unemployment rate is hovering around 5.5%, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/web/laus/laumstrk.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the highest}}}\uc2 of any state.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Woods, who runs the Center for Business and Economic Research at the \uc2 University of Nevada, Las Vegas\uc2 , said the lack of stability, which dates back to the 2008 financial crisis, has had lasting consequences in the state.\par \par \uc2 "The promise of Nevada was that you didn't necessarily have to be highly skilled, but you could get a job, buy a house, buy two cars and raise a family," he said. "To some extent, that still exists, but it has been shaken by economic crises. In order to really build a middle-class lifestyle, you need to have some certainty for not just years, but decades."\par \par \uc2 Nicole Wells, 44, an East Las Vegas voter who on a recent night was walking her terrier, Sir Elton John, through a busy park, has felt the pinch of a higher cost of living. She clips coupons now for groceries, and the space she rents as a vendor at a local antique shop has increased in price. She said she doesn't know if either party will make a difference, but believes Harris will make the best effort.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know what it will take to make the economy change, but she has the core beliefs I have," she said. Wells said she opposes Republicans' stances on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and LGBTQ rights, and fears for the country should Trump win.\par \par \uc2 Census data indicate that just 29% of Nevada residents age 25 or older have a bachelor's degree or higher, lower than every other state that Biden won in 2020 and Hillary Clinton won in 2016. Only six states, all solidly red, have a lower percentage of college-educated residents.\par \par \uc2 Harris is orienting her campaign around helping working-class families. She has proposed a $6,000 baby bonus for parents of newborns and $25,000 in down-payment assistance for first-time home buyers. She has called for lowering the cost of child care, universal paid family leave and less expensive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Harris appears to have a ground-game advantage over Trump. Democratic organizers in the state said they have opened 14 campaign offices, including in rural areas that lean heavily Republican, and they have recruited thousands of volunteers who have reached more than 800,000 Nevada voters.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have a smaller footprint in the state. They have set up five regional offices: one in Reno and four in competitive Clark County, where Las Vegas is located. Republican officials described their efforts in the state as more targeted than Democrats' operation, with a focus on Latino voters, infrequent voters and voters who are disconnected from politics. Regional GOP offices host weekly training for volunteers, who are assigned tasks like door knocking and phone banking. Republicans also are working with Trump-aligned groups such as Turning Point USA.\par \par \uc2 Door knocking\par \par \uc2 At the sprawling Las Vegas offices of Culinary Union Local 226, Secretary-Treasurer Ted Pappageorge tried to fire up a couple hundred canvassers one recent afternoon before they went door-to-door on behalf of Harris and other Democrats. The local, which represents more than 60,000 Las Vegas and Reno servers, porters, cooks, bartenders, laundry and kitchen workers, said it regularly mounts the largest outreach effort in the state, knocking more than a million doors before the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 "You knock on every single door, and you talk to every single person in the house and get their commitment to vote," Pappageorge told canvassers clustered around clipboards and posters. "That's the only way you win, and nobody is going to do that but us."\par \par \uc2 The union has tried to orient its message around kitchen-table economic issues. The colorful door hangers it is distributing say Harris and other Democrats will work for cheaper groceries and gas, lower prescription drug prices, cap rent increases, and will build affordable homes and raise minimum wages. They say Harris, not Trump, will take on what they call greedy corporations.\par \par \uc2 Pappageorge recognizes that many voters in the union have drifted away from the Democratic Party. He said unions are trying to counter that through face-to-face interactions, and by pressing party leaders to focus on issues important to members and the working class.\par \par \uc2 "That's what labor brings to the table," Pappageorge said. "If the election happens now, Trump wins," he said. "That's what we think."\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, \uc2 Americans for Prosperity\uc2  and the affiliated Libre Initiative have focused on turning out voters for GOP Senate candidate Sam Brown. While Brown has campaigned for Trump, AFP Action, a PAC funded by billionaire \uc2 Charles Koch\uc2  which supported \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  in the Republican primary, is trying to stay out of the presidential race as they knock doors in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 The America PAC, a super PAC supporting Trump and funded by Musk, shook up its ground game this month by firing the vendor that was running it.\par \par \uc2 Ronnie Najarro, a senior adviser for AFP Action in Las Vegas, said people have told him that the rising cost of living has led them to bike to work rather than drive, to pull their children out of soccer leagues and to cut back on weekend cookouts. Najarro blamed inflation on the pandemic stimulus packages supported by both Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 "The affordability that attracted people to Nevada is diminishing," he said.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans can keep the conversation focused on the economy, Najarro said, they have a good shot at winning. The wild cards, he said, are Trump's unpredictable talk about everything but the economy, and the extent to which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will galvanize voters against the party. Nevada will also vote in November on a ballot measure enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access into the state's constitution, which Harris's allies hope will drive Democratic turnout.\par \par \uc2 John Ybarra, a 24-year-old worker at a local amusement park, said he is skeptical of both candidates. He expressed concern about what he had heard about Project 2025, a conservative blueprint for a second Trump administration led by \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . But he doesn't know much about Harris and doesn't trust her, he said.\par \par \uc2 Ybarra supported \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  in 2020 before ultimately voting for Trump. He said he is leaning toward voting for Trump again, after Robert F. Kennedy Jr.endorsed him, but still needs to do more research. Ybarra, who has a 6-year-old son, still hopes to go to college and buy a home someday, but both goals feel far out of reach.\par \par \uc2 Nevada is struggling, he said, which he thinks will determine whether the state swings red or blue.\par \par \uc2 "I've had friends flip on a dime," he said. "They could change their votes tomorrow based on the gas price."\par \par \uc2 Write to Elizabeth Findell at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:elizabeth.findell@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 elizabeth.findell@wsj.com}}}\uc2  and Andrew Restuccia at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:andrew.restuccia@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 andrew.restuccia@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240929ek9t00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
