\par \par \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  last week visited Leisure World in Silver Spring, Md. PHOTO: Shuran Huang for WSJ\par \par \uc2 Hogan is getting deep-pocketed help: Maryland's Future, an independent political-action committee backed by \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2 , chief executive of the \uc2 Citadel\uc2  hedge fund, and others placed an $18.3 million ad buy on radio and television, starting this week. That sum raises ad spending by Hogan and allies from the primary through Election Day to $33.5 million, according to ad tracker AdImpact. In the same time, Alsobrooks and allies have booked $14.7 million in ads.\par \par \uc2 Hogan's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  veto\par \par \uc2 Democrats' TV ads cast him as a rubber stamp for party heavies such Sens. Ted Cruz and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  in a prospective GOP majority. For many Democrats, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are the best example of the race's stakes.\par \par \uc2 Hogan has said he would oppose a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and would support legislation codifying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Democrats, however, point to state legislation he vetoed in 2022 that would have allowed nurse practitioners, physician assistants and some other licensed individuals to perform abortions, not just physicians.\par \par \uc2 "There was a bill to lower the standards of care," Hogan said. "You can disagree, but you can't say that's restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Democrats, who overrode Hogan's veto, dispute that the legislation would have altered the level of care. And Alsobrooks said Hogan's stance that he would vote to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would be moot in a GOP-controlled Senate.\par \par \uc2 Hogan's supporters say he would use his leverage on behalf of Maryland. Centrist senators such as Sen. Joe Manchin (I., W.Va.) and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  (R., Maine) have used their clout as a deciding vote in recent years.\par \par \uc2 "He will make Maryland's vote more important than it's ever been," said Rona Kramer, a former Democratic state senator and co-chair of Democrats for Hogan, who praised his approach to the economy and fiscal responsibility. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
