\par \par \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  publicly broke from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , something some voters say they like. PHOTO: Shuran Huang for WSJ\par \par \uc2 'A cold day in hell'\par \par \uc2 Hogan shocked the political establishment in 2014 when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/BL-WB-50442"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he won his first term as governor}}}\uc2 after campaigning to lower the state's taxes and work across the aisle. On his inauguration day, a light snow turned heavier when Hogan's speech began. "They said it was going to be a cold day in hell before we elected a Republican governor," he joked.\par \par \uc2 In his first year in office, Hogan canceled a multibillion-dollar subway project and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/national-guard-deployed-in-baltimore-amid-riots-after-freddie-grays-funeral-1430218096"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 activated the Maryland National Guard}}}\uc2  in response to protests and riots in Baltimore. Hogan underwent chemotherapy for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/maryland-gov-larry-hogan-says-he-has-cancer-of-lymph-nodes-1435006234"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 non-Hodgkin lymphoma}}}\uc2  and was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/maryland-gov-larry-hogan-says-he-is-100-cancer-free-1447706517"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 officially in remission}}}\uc2  by November 2015.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, Hogan said he wouldn't vote for Trump, instead writing in his father, who had been the first Republican congressman to come out for impeaching former President Nixon. Hogan was re-elected governor in 2018 by about 12 points.\par \par \uc2 "He was very moderate, he kept taxes low," said Republican Jason Dengenis, 47, of Odenton, who planned to vote for Hogan.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you think \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  will be successful in pulling together voters from across the political spectrum in the Maryland Senate race? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Marylanders are less familiar with Alsobrooks, 53, with a late August poll by Gonzales Research & Media Services showing that roughly a third didn't yet recognize her name. She would be the first Black person to represent Maryland in the Senate if elected.\par \par \uc2 The daughter of a newspaper distributor and a receptionist, she grew up in Prince George's County. Her father, who had wanted a son, called her "little fella," she said. Alsobrooks worked as a domestic-violence prosecutor and later as state's attorney of Prince George's County, before running for county executive in 2018. She cites Harris as a longtime mentor and noted that since Harris became the party's nominee for president, the coordinated Maryland efforts to elect Harris and Alsobrooks have picked up 50,000 more volunteers.\par \par \uc2 "A, I think she'll do a good job, and B, she's a Democrat," said Renee Conway, 70, a writer and retired nurse who plans to vote for Alsobrooks. "I didn't think he was a terrible governor," Conway said of Hogan. But, she said, "The main thing is, I want a majority in the Senate."\par \par \uc2 Write to Kristina Peterson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:kristina.peterson@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 kristina.peterson@wsj.com}}}\uc2  and Anthony DeBarros at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:anthony.debarros@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anthony.debarros@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240926ek9q001e1\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
