\par \par \uc2 Sen. Tester at a campaign event with \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2  in Bozeman, Mont., earlier this month. PHOTO: Louise Johns for WSJ\par \par \uc2 That is why this race is attracting tens of millions of dollars in outside spending and campaign ads. But for voters here, there is something more personal gnawing at them. The country is changing. Montana is changing. Has Tester changed too?\par \par \uc2 "Look, I have never had a wide margin of victory," Tester reminded a crowd of nervous supporters one recent evening in downtown Bozeman. "And I ain't gonna have one this time, either."\par \par \uc2 'The last Democrat left in Montana'\par \par \uc2 Tester insists he is the same centrist he has always been and votes to advance Montanans' interests, in line with their values. Many voters said they have had a personal interaction with him, bumping into the Democrat at a grocery store, the airport or a park. Some have heard him play his trumpet at Memorial Day events. In part, he is running on personal ties and familiarity.\par \par \uc2 But public polls show Tester, 68, trailing his Republican opponent, \uc2 Tim Sheehy\uc2 , a wealthy businessman and combat veteran who is running as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/trump-biden-election-2024-rnc/card/senate-candidate-tim-sheehy-tells-convention-his-pronouns-5nxeFK9aGNgXot0H6TrR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump-endorsed conservative}}}\uc2 . National election forecasters recently shifted the race from "tossup" to "lean" or "tilt" Republican.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are increasingly confident that they can finally oust Tester. They say his breed of "prairie populist" Democrat, once common in places such as Iowa, South Dakota, North Dakota and Montana, is headed for extinction, as rural, working-class and union voters shift toward the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Trump won Montana by 16 points in 2020, the same year the state's Democratic then-Gov. Steve Bullock lost by 10 in his Senate bid. Republicans have swept all other statewide offices and secured a supermajority in the legislature. Even Tester, the thinking goes, can only outrun his party's fate for so long.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Steve Daines, the state's other senator and chair of Senate Republicans' campaign arm, said Tester's voting record is "out of step with where most Montanans are," and internal GOP polls show him losing. Ultimately, the voters will decide if Tester is done for, Daines said, "but he's the last Democrat left in Montana."\par \par \uc2 Sheehy's campaign declined to talk to The Wall Street Journal for this article and didn't respond to multiple requests for a reporter to attend his campaign events or speak with the candidate. On Fox News, Sheehy called the Montana race "the tipping point" for the Senate and said he is running like he is behind.\par \par \uc2 Tester courts Trump voters\par \par \uc2 To pull off a fourth term, Tester will need a significant number of Trump voters to split their tickets, voting Democrat for Senate even as they vote Republican for president. In 2012, the last time Tester ran in a presidential-election year, he outperformed then-President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who lost in the state, by almost 7 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Tester's campaign is running ads featuring Trump voters who plan to support him. He skipped his party's Chicago convention and is the only Democratic senator who has declined to endorse Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , saying he wants his race "to be about Montana."\par \par \uc2 Tester also is leaning into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, framing the issue as a matter of personal freedom in line with Montana's libertarian ethos. An initiative that would codify the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to viability in Montana's constitution is on the ballot this fall, and Tester allies hope enthusiasm for the measure will drive turnout for him.\par \par \uc2 At the Bozeman rally, Tester was introduced by \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , the president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 . Waving his two-fingered left hand, Tester strolled on stage to chants of "Dirt! Farmer! Dirt! Farmer!"\par \par \uc2 Tester asked: "You don't want a politician or a bureaucrat or a judge telling you, especially if you're a woman, what healthcare decisions you're going to make, do you?"\par \par \uc2 "No!" the crowd roared.\par \par \uc2 Tester called the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2022 Supreme Court decision}}}\uc2  that eliminated the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "bull baloney," drawing out the words in a country drawl.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to make sure that initiative passes. Anything else we're going to do?"\par \par \uc2 "Elect you!" his supporters shouted back.\par \par \uc2 Down-home dirt farmer or Washington insider?\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you think Sen. \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  will win re-election in Montana? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Interviews with dozens of voters across the state found Montanans divided over Tester's authenticity and his politics.\par \par \uc2 "It's always been a tight race for him in Montana. He just managed to get past 'em," said Susan Walker, 75, a retired music teacher who attended the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights rally. "I'm a little worried this year."\par \par \uc2 Walker lived in Tester's hometown of Big Sandy, population about 600, before moving to Bozeman. She said she taught his kids and isn't swayed by Republican ads that accuse Tester of becoming a Washington insider who is too liberal for Montana.\par \par \uc2 "He genuinely wants what's best for Montana and he's done a darn good job," said Walker. "He's probably got a little bit of the Washington in him now, but basically, he's still a down-home dirt farmer."\par \par \uc2 For other Montanans, Tester's dirt-farmer image has worn thin.\par \par \uc2 Shannon Bice, a 53-year-old electrical engineer in Belgrade, said he has voted for Tester in the past, but he isn't going to support him this time. Tester has backed President Biden too often, Bice said.\par \par \uc2 "He's changed. He used to look at the issues and vote," Bice said of Tester. "But now it's just straight with the Democratic side." Bice plans to vote Republican up and down the ballot because he is unhappy with Biden and Harris.\par \par \uc2 "It's the whole world, the border, everything's in chaos," he said.\par \par \uc2 Tester voted with Biden 71.4% of the time on non-nomination votes last year, according to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/538/member-congress-voted-biden-2023/story?id=106718543"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis by political website 538}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14the second-most conservative record of any senator in the Democratic caucus, after Manchin.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to major Biden administration priorities, Tester has been a reliable vote. He backed the president's $1.9 trillion coronavirus relief package and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/climate-legislation-inflation-reduction-act-small-towns-eb0ce798"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Inflation Reduction Act}}}\uc2  funding climate and healthcare programs, both of which passed along party lines. Unlike Manchin or another centrist, Sen. \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  (I., Ariz.), Tester didn't make national headlines for picking public fights with the White House to shape bills.\par \par \uc2 Bernadetta WanglerBailey, a 63-year-old retired X-ray technician, said she appreciates Tester's work to help veterans, including the 2022 PACT Act, which expanded healthcare and benefits for millions of veterans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/senate-broadens-benefits-for-veterans-exposed-to-noxious-chemicals-11655402455"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exposed to toxic chemicals}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WanglerBailey plans to vote for Trump. But she found herself seething last month when he mocked Tester at a Sheehy rally in Bozeman. Trump called Tester a slob and said he had the "biggest stomach I have ever seen."\par \par \uc2 "Oh, I wanted to slap Trump," WanglerBailey said. She remains undecided in the Senate race but said she is leaning toward voting for Tester.\par \par \uc2 "He's a good old boy. That's what Montana likes," said WanglerBailey, who, like Tester, was born and raised in Montana. The younger, photogenic Sheehy seems "too much of a pretty boy," she said.\par \par \uc2 Bellwether county in a shifting state\par \par \uc2 Tester will need the support of Trump voters such as WanglerBailey, who will cast her ballot in Cascade County, a onetime Democratic stronghold that is trending red. In Senate races since 2000, no Democrat has won without taking Cascade County, but Tester's margin of 5.5 percentage points there in 2018 was roughly half of what it was in 2012. Democrats say they can win even if they lose Cascade.\par \par \uc2 Republican Steve Fitzpatrick, who is the majority leader of Montana's state senate and represents part of the county, said the Democratic Party has moved too far to the left for the typical Montanan.\par \par \uc2 "They're talking about race and gay rights and climate change, and that's not helping a guy who's worried about paying the bills," said Fitzpatrick.\par \par \uc2 Republicans now hold all of Cascade's seats in the state legislature and control the county commission. But Fitzpatrick still gave Tester 50-50 odds in Cascade, pointing to his aggressive field operation to register and turn out voters.\par \par \uc2 "He's going to be competitive here," Fitzpatrick said. "I go out and I knock doors and talk to people. And there's a lot of people that say, you know, 'I'm voting for you, [and] I'm voting for Tester.'"\par \par \uc2 Tester's camp has tried to paint Sheehy, who moved to Montana in 2014, as one of the wealthy outsiders who are flocking to the state, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/montana-jon-tester-senate-race-3a41243b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 driving home prices and property taxes higher}}}\uc2 . Montana is the fifth fastest-growing state this decade, according to Census Bureau estimates. Since Tester's last election, about 54% of voters who moved to the state are Republican, according to estimates from voter-file vendor L2.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say his background as a Navy SEAL resonates in a state with a large number of veterans, but the first-time candidate has recently run into controversy. Tribal leaders this month called on Sheehy to apologize for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.charkoosta.com/news/senate-candidate-tim-sheehy-makes-questionable-racially-tinged-remarks-about-crow-reservation/article_afc75344-6725-11ef-9065-afb44493d9ff.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comments he made about Native Americans}}}\uc2 . In published recordings, Sheehy describes bonding "with all the Indians out there while they're drunk at 8 a.m." and beer cans thrown at his head during a parade. Sheehy told Fox News the recordings had been edited to "make me sound like somebody I'm not."\par \par \uc2 Still, Tester's deep local roots may not be enough. At the grocery store in Big Sandy, Jessica Sietsema, a 47-year-old retirement consultant, said she knows Tester personally and believes he is a decent person who has worked hard for veterans. But she doesn't agree with Tester on issues that are more important to her: border security, inflation and government spending.\par \par \uc2 He has been a "'yes man' for Biden," Sietsema said. She won't be voting for him in November. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich427\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6405
