\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 How Trump and Harris Compare on Key Policy Issues; A guide to where the candidates stand on taxes, healthcare, immigration,  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access, foreign affairs and other topics\b0\par\par\uc2 By WSJ Staff \par \uc2 4,812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par 19:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/2024-presidential-senate-and-house-election-forecasts-5c4b54b8?mod=election2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election on Nov. 5  }}}\uc2  offers American voters a choice between two presidential candidates with differing views on a range of policy issues. Here is how former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  stand on issues ranging from climate change to healthcare and jobs. This package of stories is a guide to help voters understand {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/harris-and-trump-avoid-detailed-policies-in-favor-of-big-ideas-b8871aa8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 where the candidates agree and disagree  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Federal Reserve\par \par \uc2 * Personal taxes\par \par \uc2 * Corporate taxes\par \par \uc2 * Trade and tariffs\par \par \uc2 * Healthcare\par \par \uc2 * Immigration\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Education\par \par \uc2 * Energy and the environment\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 NATO\par \par \uc2 * Iran\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine and Russia\par \par \uc2 * Middle East\par \par \uc2 * China\par \par \uc2 * North Korea\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris said she respects central-bank independence, continuing a precedent enforced by President Biden. "As president, I would never interfere in the decisions that the Fed makes," she told reporters in August. As a senator in 2018, she voted against \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  's confirmation as chair. Powell was reappointed in 2022 by Biden.\par \par \uc2 Harris hasn't said what kind of people she would pick to lead the central bank. She has argued that corporate greed has contributed to the high inflation of the past three years, and she has proposed rules against price gouging that could become price controls. Price controls were used with little success to address high inflation in the early 1970s.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 The president shapes the Federal Reserve by appointing members to its board. As president, Trump frequently bashed Powell, his pick to lead the central bank, and demanded lower interest rates. He has said he wouldn't keep Powell after his current four-year term as chair expires in 2026. Trump said in August that, as president, he should have a greater say in how the Fed sets interest rates, and he has said there are other ways to bring down inflation without raising rates.\par \par \uc2 Republican members of Congress have voiced concern about eroding central-bank independence by pressuring the Fed to keep rates low. White House pressure to stimulate the economy by keeping interest rates low contributed to the high inflation of the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 Nick Timiraos\par \par \uc2 Personal taxes\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/personal-finance/taxes/trump-tax-cuts-expire-2025-486894dc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bulk of Trump's 2017 tax cuts  }}}\uc2  expire at the end of next year, and he wants to extend them indefinitely, preserving the lower tax rates, larger standard deduction and bigger child tax credit that Republicans put in place seven years ago. The tax cuts also include a higher estate-tax exemption and a tax break for closely held businesses. Extending all of that would save taxpayers about $4 trillion over a decade\u8212\'20\'14and Trump hasn't clearly said how he would avoid increasing budget deficits.\par \par \uc2 With scant details, Trump has floated a steady stream of new tax breaks on top of that, including a tax exemption for tips, an end to income taxes on Social Security benefits and a new deduction for expenses associated with newborns. He has also criticized Biden's expansion of \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  enforcement, and he could curtail those efforts as president.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris would keep President Biden's pledge to prevent tax increases on households making under $400,000. That includes extending expiring tax cuts for about 97% of Americans. Beyond that, she backs the no-tax-on-tips idea and an expanded refundable child tax credit that would provide $3,000 per child to most parents and up to $6,000 for parents of newborns.\par \par \uc2 Beyond letting some Trump tax cuts expire, Harris supports about $5 trillion worth of tax increases from Biden's most recent budget. That plan would raise top marginal tax rates on some income to as high as 44.6%, though she would set a top capital-gains rate of 33%, below Biden's proposed level.\par \par \uc2 She didn't signal any distance from Biden's other plans to transform taxation of capital gains. Under that approach, unrealized gains above $5 million, with some exceptions, would be taxed at death instead of being exempt; capital gains above $1 million would be taxed at ordinary income rates instead of today's lower rates. Households worth at least $100 million would face a 25% minimum tax, including annual taxes on unrealized gains.\par \par \uc2 Richard Rubin\par \par \uc2 Corporate taxes\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Like Biden, Harris would raise {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/companies-weigh-effects-of-potential-corporate-tax-rate-changeon-growth-investment-plans-12223c09"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the corporate-tax rate  }}}\uc2  to 28% from 21% and, at more than $1.3 trillion over a decade, that is one of the single largest revenue-raising items in her plan.\par \par \uc2 She would also raise taxes on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/harris-puts-government-intervention-at-heart-of-economic-policy-f6bdc459"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 U.S. companies' foreign income  }}}\uc2 , as part of implementing the global minimum-tax agreement that Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  helped negotiate.\par \par \uc2 Harris would also take the new corporate alternative minimum tax\u8212\'20\'14set at 15% of financial-statement income for large profitable companies\u8212\'20\'14and bump it up to 21%. She would quadruple the new 1% tax on stock buybacks, and she would also impose new limits on companies' ability to deduct executive compensation.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Unlike most of the 2017 tax law, the corporate-tax-rate cut that Republicans put in place doesn't expire after 2025. Trump, having gotten the tax rate down to 21% from 35%, wants to push it even lower. At times, he has talked about a 20% rate, while other times he has set 15% as a target. As with his individual tax-cut plans, he hasn't specified how he would prevent the budget deficit from increasing.\par \par \uc2 A full extension of the tax cuts might also bring back other corporate-tax provisions, including immediate write-offs for capital investment and research expenses and lighter limits on interest deductions.\par \par \uc2 Richard Rubin\par \par \uc2 Trade and tariffs\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 In his first term, Trump broke with decades of bipartisan support for liberalizing trade by imposing steep tariffs on China, and more targeted tariffs on steel, aluminum and products from other countries, including allies. In a second term, Trump has said he would go further, imposing a tariff of at least 60% on China and 10% to 20% on the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 Exactly how much tariffs rise, though, is unclear. Trump has also called for reciprocity, that is, setting tariffs at the same level other countries impose on the U.S.\u8212\'20\'14mostly under 10%. It would also depend on Congress. Trump wants to end "permanent normal trade relations" with China, which extends to China the benefits of membership in the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  . That, however, would require an act of Congress, as would permanent changes to tariff rates. Trump could raise tariffs by himself using executive authority granted by several laws, although he may face opposition in court.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris would for the most part follow the trade policy of President Biden, who has maintained most of Trump's tariffs on China while raising some, such as on electric vehicles, and negotiated resolutions to disputes over steel and commercial aircraft with key allies. He has maintained Trump's blocking of new appointments to the \uc2 WTO\uc2  's dispute-settlement body, effectively neutering its enforcement role, while negotiating changes to how it operates.\par \par \uc2 Harris was one of the few senators to vote against ratification of the U.S. Mexico Canada Agreement (USMCA) in 2020, which Trump negotiated to replace the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  , because, she said, it didn't do enough to address climate change. That suggests she is even less fond of traditional trade deals than Biden, who has prioritized workers over consumers in trade matters. His Indo-Pacific Economic Framework with 14 mostly Pacific nations enhances cooperation on tax, digital trade and supply chains without reducing tariffs. A spokesperson said Harris would "employ targeted and strategic tariffs to support American workers."\par \par \uc2 Greg Ip\par \par \uc2 Healthcare\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris cast the tiebreaking vote to pass the massive package known as the Inflation Reduction Act, which gave the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  for the first time the power to negotiate the price of prescription drugs it buys for Medicare patients.\par \par \uc2 She has promised that she would speed up the pace of those negotiations, cap everyone's out-of-pocket drug spending at $2,000 a year overall and $35 a month for insulin, and crack down on pharmaceutical companies with noncompetitive business practices. "For years Big Pharma has often inflated the price of lifesaving medications," she said Aug. 15 at an appearance with Biden.\par \par \uc2 In her previous run for president, Harris supported a version of Medicare for All that would give everyone health insurance and allow private insurers to offer plans in a tightly regulated system. In this campaign, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/kamala-harris-policy-position-changes-80ea3f7f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she has backed off that proposal  }}}\uc2 . She has also proposed working with states to cancel patients' medical debts.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Trump has pledged to bring down prescription drug costs but hasn't said how exactly he would do that. Near the end of his first term in office, his administration rolled out a "most-favored nation" proposal to ensure that the U.S. didn't pay more than other similar countries for certain Medicare drugs, but the Biden administration canceled the pilot study. Another Trump rule on Medicare drug rebates was also never implemented.\par \par \uc2 Trump has vowed to protect Medicare and bring down healthcare costs overall. Despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/why-republicans-abandoned-their-fight-to-repeal-obamacare-a28d4872"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his previous ambition to repeal and replace  }}}\uc2  the Affordable Care Act, he said in April that he now plans to keep it and improve it. During the Sept. 10 debate, Trump said, "If we can come up with a plan that's going to cost our people, our population less money and be better healthcare than Obamacare, then I would absolutely do it." Asked if he has a plan for improving Obamacare, Trump said, "I have concepts of a plan. I'm not president right now."\par \par \uc2 Policy groups with ties to Trump, including \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  's Project 2025, have called for restructuring the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  , characterizing the agency as too heavy-handed with its pandemic advice.\par \par \uc2 Liz Essley Whyte\par \par \uc2 Immigration\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Trump has made his primary objective clear: to deport as many immigrants in the country illegally as he can, a measure he says is necessary after illegal crossings hit a record high under Biden. He hasn't made clear how, as president, he could carry out his mass-deportation plan. Without Congress, the president wouldn't have enough money or detention space, and he would have trouble finding most of the immigrants in question, who live in blue-leaning states that have declined to work with federal immigration authorities.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, Trump has promised to return to an array of first-term immigration policies, including Remain in Mexico and reinstating and expanding the travel ban on several Muslim-majority nations. He has also vowed to halt all refugee admissions, reduce legal immigration to the U.S. and end the automatic granting of citizenship to anyone born in the U.S., a right enshrined in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris has staked out a decidedly more moderate position on immigration than her recent Democratic predecessors, reflecting the changing politics of the issue following a record surge of illegal border crossings under the Biden administration. Harris, for example, says she supports allocating more money for immigration enforcement, and she even featured in a recent campaign ad images of the border wall built by Trump.\par \par \uc2 Harris has promised she would sign a bipartisan Senate border deal negotiated earlier this year that was blocked by Republicans. That bill would have given the president the authority to stop processing asylum seekers if crossings rose too high. At the same time, that measure would have increased legal immigration to the U.S., a principle Harris still supports. She has endorsed providing a path to citizenship for longtime immigrants in the country illegally, and would continue controversial Biden administration programs allowing migrants to enter the country legally to seek asylum.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Hackman\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris has been a more vocal supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights than Biden, but in practice her policies would be largely a continuation of the current administration's. Harris has said she supports codifying the same protections found in Roe v. Wade into law nationwide. That would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal for any reason through fetal viability\u8212\'20\'14about halfway through a typical pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14or after that if a woman's life or health is in danger.\par \par \uc2 A Harris administration could also continue fighting for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the courts. The Supreme Court declined this term to fully resolve cases involving {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-abortion-pill-mifepristone-f78f3320"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills  }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-allows-emergency-abortions-in-idaho-but-leaves-big-questions-unresolved-7fae6427"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to the procedure in medical emergencies  }}}\uc2 , and a future administration will have a role in deciding how to proceed with those cases.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Trump appointed three \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices who helped overturn Roe v. Wade, eliminating federal constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and has said the issue should now be settled state-by-state. The former president has said he won't sign a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if it were to come across his desk. During the September debate, Trump wouldn't say whether he would veto a national ban. "I won't have to," Trump claimed, suggesting it would never get enough support to get through Congress. Under the current patchwork of state laws, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is banned or heavily restricted  }}}\uc2  in about 18 states and largely legal in the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 Some antiabortion leaders want a future Republican administration {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/a-19th-century-law-could-help-influence-the-abortion-pills-future-639dddb6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to enforce the Comstock Act  }}}\uc2 , a 19th-century law that forbids shipping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related material across state lines. Trump has indicated he wouldn't block access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Laura Kusisto\par \par \uc2 Education\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.donaldjtrump.com/agenda47/agenda47-president-trumps-ten-principles-for-great-schools-leading-to-great-jobs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has pledged to push  }}}\uc2  for "universal school choice," to cut funding to "any school pushing Critical Race Theory" or transgender ideology, and to "impose real standards" on universities.\par \par \uc2 Many promises would be hard to pull off absent large Republican majorities in Congress. As president, Trump made little headway expanding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/trump-pledges-school-choice-grants-1473385331"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 private-school subsidies  }}}\uc2  and failed to close {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/white-house-to-propose-merging-education-labor-departments-1529533148"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Education Department  }}}\uc2 , which he has said he would try to do again.\par \par \uc2 Trump could have the biggest impact through enforcement of existing laws. He has promised to undo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/education/federal-lgbtq-rule-for-schools-under-siege-c60024e1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Biden administration rule  }}}\uc2  extending Title IX protections to LGBTQ students. He has suggested that he would open civil-rights investigations into diversity efforts that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/diversity-equity-dei-companies-blum-2040b173"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservatives say are discriminatory  }}}\uc2  against white and Asian students. Trump has also vowed to use the accreditation system to force universities to "defend the American tradition and Western civilization" and eliminate "Marxist diversity, equity, and inclusion bureaucrats."\par \par \uc2 Matt Barnum\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 In her initial run for president in 2019, Harris called for universal pre-K, higher teacher pay and more affordable higher education. Though her camp hasn't offered a formal education plan this election cycle, Harris has mentioned all those issues again in recent public appearances.\par \par \uc2 "God knows we don't pay you enough," she told attendees at an \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2  meeting in July. She has also stressed the need to ease the student-loan burden for public servants\u8212\'20\'14including teachers\u8212\'20\'14and for students who attended predatory for-profit colleges.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Party platform calls for free, universal preschool for 4-year-olds and better funding to support students with disabilities, and opposes private-school vouchers. Harris has also pushed to expand civil-rights protections for LGBTQ students and staff, and criticized efforts to eliminate diversity programs from schools. As many Republican-led states have adopted restrictions on classroom material, Harris has blasted book bans and talked about the importance of teaching America's "true and full history."\par \par \uc2 Melissa Korn\par \par \uc2 Energy and the environment\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act, for which Harris cast the tiebreaking vote in 2022, provided {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/the-cost-of-bidens-climate-tax-credits-is-soaring-bde5d704"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several hundred billion dollars  }}}\uc2  in grants, loans and tax incentives to promote renewable energy, reduce the use of fossil fuels and help communities prepare for the worst effects of climate change.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, while campaigning for the presidential nomination, Harris said she would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/fracking-boom-pennsylvania-trump-biden-voters-a360fd1b?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban fracking  }}}\uc2 , a form of oil and gas drilling prevalent in the swing state of Pennsylvania. She later reversed that position. During the September debate, she stressed she has no plans to ban the practice. "My position is that we have got to invest in diverse sources of energy so we reduce our reliance on foreign oil," she said.\par \par \uc2 During her acceptance speech at the Democratic National Convention, Harris said that many freedoms are at stake in the coming election, including "the freedom to breathe clean air and drink clean water and live free from the pollution that fuels the climate crisis."\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Trump has called climate change "a hoax" and said he would eliminate a $7,500 tax credit for electric vehicles that has spurred the industry. Trump has also vowed to repeal the Inflation Reduction Act and its subsidies for green technologies such as wind-turbine manufacturing, solar components and battery plants.\par \par \uc2 The Republican platform posted on Trump's campaign website pledges to lift "restrictions on American Energy Production and terminating the Socialist Green New Deal." That would mean the attempted repeal of Biden administration rules on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/climate-environment/new-epa-emissions-rules-squeeze-coal-plants-69f7dfc4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 coal-burning power plants  }}}\uc2 , vehicle tailpipe emissions and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/climate-environment/environmental-protection-agency-methane-rules-oil-gas-industry-ff05fa1b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 methane leaks  }}}\uc2  from oil-and-gas operations.\par \par \uc2 A separate effort known as Project 2025 and written by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/project-2025-donald-trump-election-2024-b89ed4dd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 former Trump advisers  }}}\uc2  suggests replacing the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2  and the National Weather Service with private companies and limiting federal climate-change research. Trump has distanced himself from Project 2025 and its recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Eric Niiler\par \par \uc2 NATO\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  , Trump's third national security adviser, is confident Trump will seek to withdraw the U.S. from the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  . Bolton describes Trump in 2018 coming "within an inch" of calling for the U.S. to leave the alliance\u8212\'20\'14during a NATO Summit, no less. "He really wants out," he said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is still in \uc2 NATO\uc2  after four years of Trump, and Biden signed legislation in 2023 blocking presidents from unilaterally withdrawing from the alliance. But Trump continuously blasts allies that don't spend the required 2% of GDP on defense, wrongly asserting they are delinquent on paying dues to the alliance. In February, Trump said he would let Russia "do whatever the hell they want" to those countries.\par \par \uc2 At a minimum, confidants say Trump will aim to downshift America's European commitments, shifting much of the security responsibilities over to allies on the continent.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris, just like Biden, would work to strengthen \uc2 NATO\uc2  and keep allies united in the defense of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "Our sacred commitment to \uc2 NATO\uc2  remains ironclad," Harris told an audience at the Munich Security Conference in February. "And I do believe, as I have said before, \uc2 NATO\uc2  is the greatest military alliance the world has ever known."\par \par \uc2 Harris is expected to rely on \uc2 NATO\uc2  as a whole and individual allies to defend Ukraine and deter further Russian aggression. She will also demand that \uc2 NATO\uc2  members spend more on their own defense, taking on more of the burden for the continent's security. But Harris is unlikely to break any relationship with an ally regardless of their military budget.\par \par \uc2 Alexander Ward\par \par \uc2 Iran\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris, like others in the Biden administration, hoped the U.S. might re-enter the Iran nuclear deal upon entering office in 2021. That effort failed, but Harris hasn't given up hope of one day putting the U.S. back into the agreement, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-06-19/kamala-harris-says-she-d-rejoin-iran-agreement-if-she-s-elected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 potentially constraining Iran's ballistic missile program  }}}\uc2  as part of a renewed pact. Harris hasn't said much more about her Iran plans. But she is advised by top aides with expertise on the country: Phil Gordon, her national security adviser, and Ilan Goldenberg, who is now on the campaign.\par \par \uc2 As vice president, Harris has mainly confronted Iran with mixed results. In January, Tehran-backed proxies killed three American service members in Jordan. Three months later, Iran launched the first direct attack on Israel since the regime came to power in 1979, prompting the U.S. and partners to successfully defend Israel.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 In his first term, Trump exacted a "maximum pressure" campaign on Iran, an attempt to starve the regime of funds for its proxies and military programs. He claims it was so successful that, had he won in 2020, Tehran would have had no choice but to make a grander nuclear deal with him. "I would have made a great deal with them\u8212\'20\'14no nuclear weapons," Trump told Bloomberg in July.\par \par \uc2 Pressure will be on Trump to once again place sanctions and other pressures on Iran, giving the regime no choice but to change its foreign policy wholesale. It isn't clear that Tehran would make any deals with Trump, as Iran's leaders don't trust him to abide by agreements and, most notably, the former president ordered a top Iranian general killed. In response, Iran has plotted to kill Trump and other top officials from his administration.\par \par \uc2 Alexander Ward\par \par \uc2 Ukraine and Russia\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Trump has repeatedly promised to strike a peace deal for Ukraine in 24 hours by threatening Kyiv and Moscow with dire consequences if they don't cooperate. While such a quick solution appears unlikely, Trump's promise signals he will quickly try to re-establish ties with Moscow to see what kind of leverage might work in pushing the Kremlin toward a settlement in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Trump's previous dealings with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  led to few tangible agreements, however, and so Trump's direction on the Russia-Ukraine war will likely be determined by his appointees. Some senior Republicans who could have top positions in a second Trump administration, such as former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  , have called for further tightening sanctions on Moscow and dramatically ramping up military support for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Pressed during September's debate on whether he thinks it is in the best interest of the U.S. for Ukraine to win its war against Russia, Trump refused to answer. "I want the war to stop," he said.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris has vowed to continue the Biden administration strategy of firmly supporting Ukraine. But she will be seeking aid for Kyiv at a time of rising congressional opposition to big aid packages such as the $60 billion passed earlier this year that buttressed Ukrainian defenses. To compensate, Harris will be looking to traditional allies and \uc2 NATO\uc2  to take on more of the financial burden so that Ukraine can fight Russia to a negotiated settlement.\par \par \uc2 Her administration is likely to say little about the delicate question of when to open negotiations with Russia. While U.S. officials had hoped to avoid a protracted war of attrition in Ukraine, they note that it could take years for the West's unprecedented sanctions on Russia to be fully felt there. U.S. officials hope the Kremlin could become more amenable to concessions if it sees signs that living standards are beginning to erode in the next year or two.\par \par \uc2 Alan Cullison\par \par \uc2 Middle East\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Since Oct. 7, Harris has been one of the administration's most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/national-security/harriss-support-for-gaza-cease-fire-hints-at-foreign-policy-shift-bbe8dc2a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outspoken advocates  }}}\uc2  of a cease-fire in Gaza and a two-state solution, signaling that a Harris administration might be tougher on Israel than Biden has been. Early on she called attention to the dire humanitarian situation in Gaza and has pressed Israeli leaders hard in private to do more to protect civilians. She has also publicly laid out an American vision for postconflict Gaza.\par \par \uc2 But Harris has also shown herself to be a strong proponent of Israel, condemning \uc2 Hamas\uc2  's brutal terrorist attack and meeting with the families of Americans held in Gaza. So while she may be willing to press Israel harder to end the war, she may not go so far as to threaten to halt weapons sales. "President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I are unwavering in our commitment to Israel's security," Harris said in a March 3 speech in Selma, Ala.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 One of Trump's most touted accomplishments on Middle East policy was the Abraham Accords, a breakthrough that normalized relations between Israel and the U.A.E. and Israel and Bahrain. While both the Trump and Biden administrations have pushed to normalize ties between Israel and Saudi Arabia, that is likely a long shot after the Oct. 7 terrorist attacks and Israel's war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Trump in a second term would likely continue to support Israel's defense, but the candidate has also said publicly that he would push Israel {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/trump-israel-gaza-netanyahu-biden-ba17bedaf2f1b5f2ea220828d0fba73b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to "finish up" its war  }}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14although he hasn't specified how he would do so.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the region, Trump would likely continue trying to bring American troops home. While Biden has kept small contingents of U.S. service members in Syria and Iraq, where \uc2 Islamic State\uc2  is still a threat, Trump would likely strive to withdraw American forces.\par \par \uc2 Lara Seligman\par \par \uc2 China\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Trump's tough approach to China, both from an economic and military perspective, would likely continue in a second term. He has promised to impose a tariff of 60% or more on Chinese goods, building on the tariffs he imposed in his first term as a way to reduce the trade deficit with China. Meanwhile, \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  , Trump's U.S. trade representative and a likely candidate for a senior post in a second Trump administration, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://foreignpolicy.com/2023/07/16/trump-trade-war-robert-lighthizer-china-economic-policy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposed in a new book  }}}\uc2  to raise tariffs on China, cut off investment between the two countries, block Chinese social-media companies, and other measures until China's trade surplus disappears.\par \par \uc2 "President Trump looks at China as an economic adversary," retired Lt. Gen. Keith Kellogg said in an interview, stressing that he doesn't speak for Trump.\par \par \uc2 In his first term, Trump made countering China's rapidly expanding military and aggressive moves in the South China Sea a top priority. In a second term, Trump would likely continue to push back on China's military action and rally partners such as Japan and South Korea to keep Beijing in check in the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 Harris is poised to continue Biden's approach of countering China by strengthening partnerships in the Pacific. Like Biden, Harris has made clear in public remarks that she sees alliances as critical to prevent wars and maintain stability. That is especially true in the Pacific, where she believes these alliances are a bulwark against China.\par \par \uc2 Harris has visited the region four times as vice president, including a meeting with President Xi Jinping in November 2022 in Bangkok, according to an aide. She is particularly invested in continuing to boost the U.S.-Philippine relationship: she was the highest-ranking official to ever visit the island of Palawan, on the South China Sea.\par \par \uc2 She has also signaled that she opposes China's pressure campaign against Taiwan. "We will continue to support Taiwan's self-defense, consistent with our longstanding policy," Harris said in a Sept. 28 visit to the USS Howard at Yokosuka Naval Base, Japan.\par \par \uc2 Lara Seligman\par \par \uc2 North Korea\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 In her Democratic convention speech, Harris said as president she would "not cozy up to tyrants and dictators like \uc2 Kim Jong Un\uc2  , who are rooting for Trump."\par \par \uc2 Harris isn't expected to substantially deviate from Biden's approach to North Korea, which has included an open invitation for talks without preconditions\u8212\'20\'14an offer Pyongyang has rejected.\par \par \uc2 She would likely build upon the administration's work to tie South Korea and Japan closer together to contain North Korea. Sanctions pressure on Pyongyang is likely to persist, though it has had minimal impact on its nuclear and missile programs, as are attempts to squeeze its methods of earning cash through illicit cyber means.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic platform made no explicit mention of denuclearization on the Korean Peninsula, but a Harris campaign official later said it would remain a key objective. In 2022, following a visit to the demilitarized zone, Harris said the U.S. commitment to South Korea's defense was "ironclad."\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 As president, Trump exchanged fiery, threatening rhetoric with North Korean leader \uc2 Kim Jong Un\uc2  before pivoting sharply toward a policy of intense and historically unprecedented engagement. The pair met in Singapore in 2018 and again in Hanoi in 2019 in an attempt to broker a nuclear deal in exchange for eased sanctions. Trump's outreach went against the recommendations of many of his own national-security advisers and ultimately amounted to nothing.\par \par \uc2 The 2024 GOP platform contains no mention of North Korea, but a deal-loving Trump could seek to again negotiate with Kim if he returns to office. Trump has spoken admiringly of Kim\u8212\'20\'14and other authoritarian leaders\u8212\'20\'14since leaving office, and he kept in his personal files friendly letters the two exchanged when he was president. During his speech accepting the Republican nomination, Trump, speaking of Kim, said: "I think he misses me."\par \par \uc2 Dustin Volz\par \par \uc2 This explanatory article might be updated periodically.\par \par \uc2 Design and illustrations by James Benedict and Adrienne Tong\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240909ek99001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
