\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Why Trump Is the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Moderate; Harris wants to impose a national law without late-term restrictions.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 578 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par 21:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Here's a fact check that ABC's eager debate moderators missed Tuesday night: Of the two candidates on stage, Donald Trump is the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  moderate. This is easy not to notice, amid the rapid-fire competing claims, but it's true.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump bragged about his role in ending Roe v. Wade, which protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access through fetal viability, or about six months into pregnancy (23 or 24 weeks). Yet he also has repeatedly criticized Florida's law restricting abortions at six weeks. "Six weeks is too short," he recently said. "I've told them that I want more weeks."\par \par \uc2 Broadly speaking, this is in step with public opinion. Look at Gallup's 2023 numbers: 69% of Americans want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "generally legal" in the first three months. In the second three months, however, only 37% agree; 55% say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be "generally illegal," and that includes 52% of women.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's view is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now a question for the voters of each state to decide. Ms. Harris claimed Tuesday that he's raring to sign a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. "It's a lie," Mr. Trump replied. "I'm not signing a ban." Asked if he'd veto one, he said he wouldn't have to, suggesting it could never pass Congress.\par \par \uc2 So why does Mr. Trump plan to vote against Florida's Amendment 4, which would overturn the six-week law? Because he thinks it goes too far. It would restore the viability line, protecting abortions again until about six months, as well as when necessary "to protect the patient's health," however a doctor defines that.\par \par \uc2 Does Ms. Harris believe in any restrictions at all? She refused to say. Her response Tuesday was to call for reimposing Roe v. Wade nationwide, as if that's all voters need to know. But she has backed the Women's Health Protection Act, which mirrors the Florida ballot measure by reinstating the viability line with the same undefined "health" loophole late in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The bill is more radical than laws in many European countries that have reached a middle ground on this difficult moral issue. Ms. Harris is staking out the extreme position favored by the left that no restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are tolerable, not even on late-term abortions long after a fetus is viable in the womb.\par \par \uc2 Pressed by Mr. Trump on whether she'd permit abortions in the seventh month or even later, Ms. Harris acted incredulous, as if such a thing were impossible to imagine. She should read "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Absolutist," an interview last year in the Atlantic magazine with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in Colorado named Warren Hern.\par \par \uc2 "He specializes in abortions late in pregnancy," the article {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2023/05/dr-warren-hern-abortion-post-roe/674000/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 says}}}\uc2 , usually "until around 32 weeks." That's eight calendar months, long after viability. Some of his patients have gotten devastating news about fetal brain anomalies or the like. "But Hern estimates that at least half, and sometimes more, of the women who come to the clinic do not have these diagnoses." Twice he has done abortions simply because the parents wanted a baby of the other sex. "The reason doesn't really matter to Hern."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump is moderating the GOP's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  position and taking some heat from the right for doing so. Ms. Harris is the real \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  absolutist.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240911ek9b004s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
