\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 These Republicans Are Pushing for  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights; Longtime members of the GOP are campaigning for ballot measures in Florida, Arizona and other states\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto | Photographs by Tina Russell for WSJ \par \uc2 1,177 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2024\par 09:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BRADENTON, Fla.\u8212\'20\'14The progressive campaign for a Florida ballot initiative to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is heating up, but one recent gathering on the Gulf Coast had a different feel. It was organized by\u8212\'20\'14and for\u8212\'20\'14Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Several dozen people attended the event at a local oyster bar, where they were visibly uncomfortable talking explicitly about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, instead using terms like freedom and limited government. Carol Whitmore told the story of her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  anyway.\par \par \uc2 Whitmore, who has served more than three decades in elected office, was approaching high-school graduation and living by herself when she learned she was pregnant. She got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, the year the Supreme Court decided Roe v. Wade, granting women legal access nationwide. That year, she also registered as a Republican.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not going to leave my party," Whitmore said. "How are you going to change things if you give up?"\par \par \uc2 Whitmore is one of a small but growing number of Republicans who believe the GOP has veered too far right on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by supporting policies that allow the procedure only in very limited time frames or circumstances. The tensions have been particularly visible in the Florida ballot fight, highlighting the party's difficulty in managing an issue that has hurt them politically in the two years {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since the Supreme Court delivered}}}\uc2  on the long-sought Republican goal of overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 Last week, GOP presidential nominee \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a Florida resident, said the state's current ban on the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/donald-trump-gives-mixed-signals-on-florida-abortion-referendum-2782c55c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was too short}}}\uc2 , and suggested he would vote for the initiative, which would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, or about halfway through a typical pregnancy. His campaign walked back the comments, and Trump a day later {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/trump-says-he-will-vote-against-florida-abortion-referendum-c0648486"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said he would vote no}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A group Whitmore helped form, Conservative Women for Freedom, hosted the event, part of an effort to reach voters who oppose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/florida-abortion-ballot-measure-supreme-court-a3340feb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the state's six-week ban}}}\uc2  but who can't imagine aligning themselves with progressive groups that are driving the ballot measure campaign.\par \par \uc2 "You have a right to do what you want with your body and nobody in Tallahassee is going to tell me and many of us what we're going to do," Whitmore, a 69-year-old registered nurse, told the crowd. She previously served as a city commissioner and mayor of Holmes Beach, near Bradenton, and commissioner for Manatee County, which went 57% for Trump in 2020. She is running for a return to the city commission this year, after losing her county re-election bid in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Another group leader is Jaymie Carter, a local real-estate agent who recently resigned her seat on a state college board, to which she had been appointed by GOP Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who signed the six-week ban into law this past year. Carter said she loved her position on the board but decided she should step down because she is advocating a position in opposition to the governor.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Whitmore's group, there is a statewide bipartisan effort called Florida Women's Freedom Coalition that has aimed to reach people who are "a little bit twitchy about giving money directly to something they felt was a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  or \uc2 ACLU\uc2  project," said the coalition's executive director, Anna Hochkammer.\par \par \uc2 That group has raised $1.6 million, including five-figure donations from Republicans. Having Republicans publicly advocate for the measure helps reassure conservative voters who need "validators who also wear a big 'R' on their political label," Hochkammer said.\par \par \uc2 One donor is Carlos Lacasa, a former corporate lawyer who served as a Republican state legislator for eight years. In the legislature, he voted for a ban on so-called partial birth abortions, a nonmedical term often used to refer to some abortions performed in the second trimester, and for a law requiring parental notification and consent for many abortions given to minors.\par \par \uc2 Lacasa said he views Florida's six-week ban as at odds with Republican principles about limiting government interference in people's private lives. "As your rights as a woman go, so go my rights as a man and a citizen of this country," he said.\par \par \uc2 Florida's Republican Party officially opposes the measure, which would amend the state constitution. DeSantis has called it "radical" and "very, very extreme." Antiabortion groups say the ballot initiative is vague enough that it would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until birth and could be used {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/abortion-rights-advocates-deploy-a-new-red-state-playbook-8f13a644"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to wipe out parental consent laws as well as health and safety regulations}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 In all, there are 10 measures to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights headed for the ballot in November, including in states with a large share of Republicans and independents such as Florida, Arizona, Missouri and South Dakota. The issue could help boost Democrats in other races, including for the White House and Congress.\par \par \uc2 Florida has the most organized effort thus far to court GOP support. The state requires 60% support for ballot measures to pass, higher than in other states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been on the ballot. That means that by some estimates, roughly 40% of Republicans and two-thirds of independents will need to join with Democrats to vote in favor.\par \par \uc2 History suggests the Florida vote will come down to the wire. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters didn't clear the 60% mark in previous ballot referendums their side won in Kansas, Michigan and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 When Roe was overturned, about half of the states were expected to ban or heavily restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Today that number stands at around 18}}}\uc2 , in part because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups have won seven straight ballot referendums.\par \par \uc2 Polls show a significant shift in how Republican women view \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe was overturned. A survey taken by public-opinion researcher Tresa Undem, before the Supreme Court's decision, showed just over 20% of female Republican voters felt their party's views on the issue were too extreme. Today, that number is closer to 40%.\par \par \uc2 The ballot impact could be important in Arizona, a key swing state where some Republicans are publicly urging residents to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights measure there.\par \par \uc2 Longtime Republican Rebecca Gau stood with Democratic Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  at a press conference in support of adding the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Gau worked for a Republican governor, is Christian and once identified as "very pro-life" but said when a family member in an abusive relationship decided to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  it helped change her views. She also participated in a commercial focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  and was recently placed on a list of Republican "traitors" sent out by a local GOP party committee.\par \par \uc2 "This issue is so big for our daughters' and our granddaughters' future that it's worth taking a stand right now," she said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240903ek93001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
