\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Vance Flips the 'Fact Check' Script; His slip of the tongue leads \uc2 CBS\uc2 's Margaret Brennan to reveal \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's radical view on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sierra Dawn McClain and Nicholas Tomaino \par \uc2 571 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2024\par 17:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even a blind squirrel finds a nut once in a while, and so it goes with journalistic "fact checkers." Sen. J.D. Vance made a slip of the tongue last week, which prompted CBS News to reveal the truth unwittingly.\par \par \uc2 In an Aug. 11 "{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pK1V2q05Zi8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Face the Nation}}}\uc2 " interview with Margaret Brennan, Mr. Vance said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is "trying to find some common ground" on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile, "you have Democrats who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right up to the moment\u8212\'20\'14and sometimes even beyond the moment\u8212\'20\'14of birth, which is just sick stuff."\par \par \uc2 "That's not accurate," Ms. Brennan admonished him.\par \par \uc2 "It is accurate," he replied. "In fact, the Born Alive Act, multiple members of the current Democratic administration, including our vice president, supported that legislation\u8212\'20\'14they have supported taxpayer-funded abortions up to the moment of birth."\par \par \uc2 The screen, moments later, cuts to the studio, where Ms. Brennan reads from a script: "We want to clarify what Sen. Vance said about the Born Alive [\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] Survivors Protection Act and his claim that Vice President Harris supported the legislation. A CBS News fact-check finds that Harris voted against advancing the bill twice when she was a senator, and has previously called it extreme and a setback to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America. We found no evidence that anyone who currently serves in the Biden administration voted for it either." Then the interview continues.\par \par \uc2 It's true: Ms. Harris opposed the bill, which would have required medical professionals to "exercise the same degree of professional skill, care, and diligence to preserve the life and health" of a child who survived an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure as a "reasonably diligent and conscientious health care practitioner would render to any other child born alive at the same gestational age." In 2020 she and almost all Senate Democrats killed the bill on a procedural vote. When Rep. \uc2 Ann Wagner\uc2  (R., Mo.) introduced it last year in the House, it passed 220-210, with only one Democrat, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , in favor.\par \par \uc2 Democrats argue that the bill is unnecessary because Congress passed the Born-Alive Infants Protection Act in 2002, and every state forbids murder. But the 2002 law establishes no penalties for failing to provide care and merely states that children who survive attempted abortions are, legally speaking, persons. As of January 2023, according to the Charlotte Lozier Institute, "only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20230206180920/https:/www.frc.org/prolifemaps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 18 states}}}\uc2  have laws offering robust protections to babies who survive abortions." That number declined by one when Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  signed a law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.revisor.mn.gov/laws/2023/0/70/laws.4.56.0#laws.4.56.0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 repealing}}}\uc2  Minnesota's requirement that medical personnel "preserve the life and health of the born alive infant."\par \par \uc2 The encounter raises an interesting question: What's more tempting for the press, the urge to catch a Republican in a falsehood or the desire to conceal a Democrat's radical positions?\par \par \uc2 Perhaps Mr. Vance could test it by intentionally misspeaking about Ms. Harris's views. "The vice president voted to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/115th-congress/senate-bill/2311/all-actions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infants}}}\uc2  can feel pain," he could say. No she didn't! "Ms. Harris doesn't believe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/KamalaHarris/status/1136320875102429184"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taxpayers}}}\uc2  should fund elective abortions." Yes she does! Tactical denial might be an effective way for Mr. Vance to get out truths the media finds inconvenient.\par \par \uc2 Ms. McClain and Mr. Tomaino are assistant editorial features editors at the Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240818ek8i0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
