\par\par \uc2 Cross Country\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 New Jersey Harasses Pregnancy Resource Centers; Attorney General Matt Platkin's effort is part of a nationwide campaign that claims to be 'pro-choice' but seeks to limit options for women.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sierra Dawn McClain \par \uc2 864 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2024\par 18:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Montclair, N.J.\par \par \uc2 Politicians and attorneys general in states run by Democrats have been on a crusade to make life miserable for pregnancy resource centers, and the campaign has picked up since the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade in 2022. This has included harassing them with legal action and trying to discredit their work.\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy resource centers provide women facing unplanned pregnancies with free and low-cost support such as counseling on alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , parenting education, medical referrals and material goods. These centers in 2022 provided clients across the U.S. with services valued at more than $367 million, including more than 500,000 free ultrasounds, 3.5 million packs of diapers and 43,000 car seats.\par \par \uc2 Crystal Umanzor is one of the many grateful beneficiaries. When Ms. Umanzor learned she was pregnant at age 22, she was financially insecure and already a single mother. She thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was her only option and took a mifepristone pill\u8212\'20\'14the first in a two-part regimen. Then she changed her mind and sought help from First Choice Women's Resource Centers, a faith-based New Jersey nonprofit that she found through a web search. First Choice, Ms. Umanzor said in an interview, connected her with a pharmacy that gave her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aaplog.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/PG-6-Reversal-of-the-Effects-of-Mifepristone-by-Progesterone.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 progesterone}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831017/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reverse}}}\uc2  the pill's effects and provided her with nonjudgmental support.\par \par \uc2 "If it weren't for them, I would not\u8212\'20\'14I don't even know what I would've done," she said. "Honestly, they've felt like family to me, even more than my family." Ms. Umanzor gave birth to a healthy daughter, Avianna, in August 2023.\par \par \uc2 First Choice has supported more than 36,000 women facing unplanned pregnancies since 1985 and doesn't charge for its services. Yet rather than praise the nonprofit, New Jersey officials are harassing it because of its pro-life stance. Democratic Attorney General Matt Platkin last November issued a subpoena demanding that it turn over a broad range of documents. He did so under the pretense of conducting a civil investigation into possible violations of state laws and regulations, but attorneys representing First Choice wrote in a court filing challenging the subpoena that Mr. Platkin "has never cited any complaint or other substantive evidence of wrongdoing to justify his demands."\par \par \uc2 The attorney general's order requires First Choice to dig up and hand over documents going back 10 years, including personal information about employees, volunteers, affiliates and donations. "Collecting that information would be completely overwhelming," Aimee Huber, First Choice's executive director, said in an interview. "It would take multiple hours per day and take us away from our mission of serving women." Ms. Huber is also concerned that the information could be used to badger and intimidate the nonprofit's pro-life supporters.\par \par \uc2 A core plank of Mr. Platkin's investigation is the assertion that First Choice may be misleading the public and thus violating consumer-fraud law. This isn't the first time he has made such a claim about pregnancy resource centers. Mr. Platkin and the attorneys general of 14 other mostly blue states and the District of Columbia laid out this theory in an open letter in October 2023. They claim that pregnancy centers set up shop near health clinics, use deceptive tactics to lure in women seeking abortions, then give them misleading information about the procedure to trick them into carrying their babies to term.\par \par \uc2 It's no secret that pregnancy resource centers are pro-life and that they inform their clients about the potential negative physical and mental health effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's also well known that these nonprofits consider it a win\u8212\'20\'14for the mother and her child\u8212\'20\'14when she chooses not to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . First Choice states on its website and in conversations with clients that it doesn't provide or refer for abortions.\par \par \uc2 Yet Mr. Platkin's consumer-fraud argument is one piece of a broad smear campaign. In December 2022 his office issued a consumer alert warning the public to be wary of the centers, which "seek to prevent people from accessing comprehensive \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care."\par \par \uc2 Emails obtained through a public-records request show that the attorney general's office asked \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , the nation's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, to preview and edit the draft consumer alert before it was issued\u8212\'20\'14a clear conflict of interest. In addition to slamming pregnancy centers, the consumer alert urged women seeking abortions to check out \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's website. The attorney general's office and \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  declined to comment on their collaboration.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Platkin's campaign does nothing to help women. Instead, it makes it more difficult for pregnancy centers to do their work, which in turn limits their ability to support women like Ms. Umanzor seeking alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the attorney general is pro-choice, why is he working so hard to limit options for women?\par \par \uc2 Ms. McClain is an assistant editorial features editor at the Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240802ek82004xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
