\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 America's New Political War Pits Young Men Against Young Women; A majority of men under 30 support former President Trump and Republican control of Congress, a sharp reversal from the 2020 race; young women strongly favor Democrats\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aaron Zitner and Andrew Restuccia \par \uc2 2,237 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2024\par 01:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Collin Mertz, a 23-year-old farmer in North Dakota, believes American men like himself have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/donald-trump-second-term-plans-d4c15912"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 targeted}}}\uc2  by liberals in the push for diversity.\par \par \uc2 "It would seem the white male is the enemy of the left," said Mertz, who voted for former President {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-republican-party-complete-control-25f4100c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  in 2020 and plans to do so again in November.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Starrett, a 28-year-old engineer in Cincinnati, feels a personal threat from conservatives seeking to scale back access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other rights.\par \par \uc2 "It's kind of terrifying, really," said Starrett, who backs Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The forces of American culture and politics are pushing men and women under age 30 into opposing camps, creating a new fault line in the electorate and adding an unexpected {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-allies-launch-20-million-effort-to-reach-young-men-e65aeb31"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wild card into the 2024 presidential election}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Voters under 30 have been a pillar of the Democratic coalition since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  left office in 1989. That pillar is showing cracks, with young men defecting from the party.\par \par \uc2 Young men now favor Republican control of Congress and Trump for president after backing President Biden and Democratic lawmakers in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Women under 30 remain strongly behind Democrats for Congress and the White House. They are also far more likely to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/609914/women-become-liberal-men-mostly-stable.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 call themselves liberal}}}\uc2  than two decades ago.\par \par \uc2 Before Biden ended his candidacy this month, Trump was winning support from a majority of men under age 30, according to a merger of Wall Street Journal polls in February and July. If that holds until Election Day, Republicans would win young men for the first time in more than two decades, voter exit polls show.\par \par \uc2 Young men backed Trump over Biden by 14 points in the merged Journal polls this year, a substantial swing from 2020. In that election, they supported Biden by 15 points, according to AP VoteCast, a voter survey. Young women in the Journal surveys backed Biden by 30 points and Democratic control of Congress by 34 points, essentially unchanged from 2020.\par \par \uc2 The gender gap extends to opposing views of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , student-loan forgiveness and other issues affecting the lives of young adults.\par \par \uc2 The question now is whether Harris, the expected Democratic nominee, will recapture the support of young men or push more of them away. Harris's candidacy removes Biden's age, 81, as a voter concern and instead spotlights social, economic and policy issues driving young men and women apart.\par \par \uc2 Trump, 78, now facing a 59-year-old female opponent, has long tailored much of his campaign to appeal to younger men with shows of virility.\par \par \uc2 Tough talk\par \par \uc2 Days after an attempted assassination, the former president headlined the Republican National Convention, which featured an onstage appearance by celebrity wrestler Hulk Hogan.\par \par \uc2 "I've been in the ring with some of the biggest, some of the baddest dudes on the planet," Hogan told the cheering crowd. "Trump is the toughest of them all."\par \par \uc2 Ultimate Fighting Championship Chief Executive Dana White introduced Trump ahead of his nomination acceptance speech. Kid Rock performed his song "American Bad Ass."\par \par \uc2 The testosterone-fueled lineup, coming on the heels of Trump's fist-pumping response to bullets flying past him, "positions Trump as an anti-hero that millions of young men\u8212\'20\'14specifically, young, first-time voters\u8212\'20\'14connect with and even aspire to,'' said John Della Volpe, director of the Harvard Youth Poll. "It's something they see everywhere they consume information, whether it's in the gamer community, TikTok, \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Mertz, the North Dakota farmer, said he was more excited to vote for Trump than even a few weeks ago, in part because of the way the former president handled the assassination attempt, captured in photos of Trump after the shooting. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal7200
