\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and Trump's Choice for Vice President; The veep finalist who's closest to Trump is North Dakota Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2024\par 22:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is loving the public suspense over his pick for Vice President, as he teases Sen. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  at a Florida rally and muses on a radio show about Sen. J.D. Vance's beard. (Mr. Trump's verdict: "He looks like a young Abraham Lincoln.") A notable difference on substance is where the three reported VP finalists stand on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump was asked Wednesday whether it's a negative for North Dakota Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  that he signed a state law prohibiting most abortions, with exceptions up to six weeks. "It's a little bit of an issue. It's a pretty strong ban," Mr. Trump replied. "He's taken a very strong stance\u8212\'20\'14or the state has. I don't know if it's Doug, but the state has." What's fascinating here is that Mr. Burgum's position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably the closest to Mr. Trump's.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Burgum ran for office in 2016 as an outsider, he was perceived as a social moderate from the C-Suite. He suggested "working comprehensively" to reduce unwanted pregnancies. When does life begin? "That's a theological question, not a Governor question." He told a voter that outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  merely pushes it into the shadows: "America was an unsafe place for women before Roe v. Wade."\par \par \uc2 Confronted with that last quote on NBC recently, Mr. Burgum said "certainly I've evolved in that position." What he believes in his heart is impossible to say, but the imperatives of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics in Bismarck, N.D., are obvious. The bill Mr. Burgum signed in 2023 passed by a veto-proof margin, 76-14 in the state House and 42-5 in the Senate. North Dakota already had strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on the books, passed in protest of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Burgum has been consistent in arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is a question for the states, not the federal government. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/from-fargo-to-microsoft-to-the-white-house-doug-burgum-debate-great-plains-trump-83c95ae6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 During a visit}}}\uc2  to the Journal last year, when he was running in the GOP primaries, he said a President Burgum wouldn't sign a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law: "What works for North Dakota is not going to work for California and New York. It doesn't even work for Minnesota."\par \par \uc2 This wasn't the popular answer in the primaries, yet it's what Mr. Trump is advocating today. "Now that we have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  where everyone wanted it from a legal standpoint, the states will determine by vote or legislation," Mr. Trump said on Truth Social. "Whatever they decide must be the law of the land." There's no tension with Mr. Burgum. As Mr. Trump's running mate, he could go to swing states and credibly deliver that message.\par \par \uc2 The contrast is with Messrs. Vance and Rubio. Running for his Ohio seat, Mr. Vance called for "minimum national standards" and said he'd support a federal 15-week limit. In one interview he defended making no exceptions for rape. "I think two wrongs don't make a right," he said. "It's not whether a woman should be forced to bring a child to term; it's whether a child should be allowed to live even though the circumstances of that child's birth are somehow inconvenient or a problem to the society."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rubio co-sponsored a Senate bill to restrict abortions at 15 weeks, and he has also argued against exceptions for rape. "I do not believe that the dignity and the worth of human life is tied to the circumstances of their conception, but I recognize that that's not a majority position," Mr. Rubio said in 2022. "This is not an easy issue." No question, and it's worth saying that the position he articulated there is a philosophically coherent pro-life view.\par \par \uc2 It isn't, however, Mr. Trump's view. "I believe in the exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother," he said in his recent debate against President Biden. "Some people don't. Follow your heart. But you have to get elected also."\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240711ek7b006mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
