\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court's Notable Cases of the 2023-24 Term; Justices address issues on Trump,  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 , guns, homelessness and federal regulatory power\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall \par \uc2 962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2024\par 18:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-courts-strains-intensify-as-terms-end-approaches-1d2b3d77"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has wrapped up a term  }}}\uc2  that included blockbuster cases about former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , hot-button social issues, questions of free speech and matters of importance to the business community. Here are some highlights.\par \par \uc2 Ballot eligibility\par \par \uc2 Trump v. Anderson\par \par \uc2 Decision: 9-0\par \par \uc2 The court restored presumptive Republican nominee Trump's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-ruling-trump-ballot-colorado-092852fe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot eligibility in Colorado  }}}\uc2 , ruling in March that states lack the power to reject presidential candidates on the grounds they engaged in rebellion or insurrection against the U.S. All nine justices agreed on the bottom-line outcome, but four said the court had gone too far in limiting ways to enforce a constitutional clause that bars former officials who engaged in insurrection or rebellion from holding office again.\par \par \uc2 Presidential immunity\par \par \uc2 Trump v. U.S.\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 The court dealt a major blow to Trump's prosecution on charges he sought to subvert the 2020 election, ruling that former presidents {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/donald-trump-immunity-supreme-court-ruling-7ce6415b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enjoy sweeping immunity for their acts  }}}\uc2  while in office. The decision threw out some of special counsel Jack Smith 's allegations and erected new hurdles for the ones that remain, casting doubts on whether the case can ever go to trial.\par \par \uc2 Jan. 6 attack\par \par \uc2 Fischer v. U.S.\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-narrows-law-used-against-some-jan-6-rioters-f77b681d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled federal prosecutors overstepped  }}}\uc2  by charging some Jan. 6 rioters under an obstruction statute passed as part of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act, the 2002 corporate-governance law enacted after the Enron financial scandal. The court's majority sided with an alleged rioter who argued it only applies to evidence in congressional investigations, not live proceedings such as the Capitol session to certify President Biden's election. Two of the four charges the special counsel brought against Trump for election interference rely on portions of the same statute. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
