\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court examined whether federal authorities improperly loosened restrictions on an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. PHOTO: Adria Malcolm for WSJ\par \par \uc2 Abortion\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2  v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine\par \par \uc2 Decision: 9-0\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-abortion-pill-mifepristone-f78f3320"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected an effort  }}}\uc2  seeking to restrict access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone, preserving for now the widespread availability of the most common method American women use to end their pregnancies. The justices ruled \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents didn't have legal standing to challenge the Food and Drug Administration 's moves to expand access to the drug.\par \par \uc2 Moyle v. U.S.\par \par \uc2 The court, in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-allows-emergency-abortions-in-idaho-but-leaves-big-questions-unresolved-7fae6427"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 case about Idaho's \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban  }}}\uc2 , chose not to rule on the issue it had previously agreed to decide: whether federal law permits emergency-room doctors to perform abortions to preserve a woman's health even in states that outlaw the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Homelessness\par \par \uc2 City of Grants Pass v. Johnson\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 The court ruled that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-homeless-camps-f3c68a9a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it isn't cruel and unusual punishment  }}}\uc2  for cities and towns to enforce laws that prevent the homeless from camping on public property when they have nowhere else to go. The decision allowed an Oregon city's ban on sleeping in cars parked on the street or in public parks with a blanket. The city, backed by a range of municipalities in the West, had argued that an appeals court governing its region has made it too difficult to implement policies to address homeless encampments.\par \par \uc2 Regulation\par \par \uc2 Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo, Relentless v. Department of Commerce\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-pares-back-federal-regulatory-power-954a101c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overruled its Chevron decision  }}}\uc2  from 40 years ago that required judges to give considerable deference to government regulators, a precedent that had become part of the federal regulatory framework for decades. The idea behind it was that when legislation is ambiguous, judges shouldn't second-guess expert agencies interpreting the laws Congress assigned them to apply, so long as their actions weren't unreasonable. Industry groups and other conservatives argued that executive-branch agencies have used that deference to implement policies that Congress never approved.\par \par \uc2 CFPB\uc2  v. Community Financial Services Association of America\par \par \uc2 Decision: 7-2\par \par \uc2 The court, in an opinion by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  , upheld the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/finance/regulation/federal-consumer-financial-watchdog-survives-supreme-court-scrutiny-e90485bd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 structure Congress created  }}}\uc2  for funding the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  , ruling that lawmakers had authority to insulate the agency's budget from political interference. The decision rejected a conservative-backed challenge that could have crippled the \uc2 CFPB\uc2  , an Obama-era creation intended to protect consumers from predatory and discriminatory practices.\par \par \uc2 First Amendment\par \par \uc2 Murthy v. Missouri\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 The court ruled two Republican-led states and several individual social-media users {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-rules-for-biden-administration-in-covid-era-speech-case-2e34ad25"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 didn't have standing  }}}\uc2  to press a lawsuit alleging the Biden administration unlawfully coerced online platforms into suppressing antivaccine posts and other disfavored speech during the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Moody\uc2  v. NetChoice, NetChoice v. Paxton\par \par \uc2 Decision: 9-0\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-avoids-final-decision-on-state-regulation-of-social-media-a6da746c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sidestepped a ruling  }}}\uc2  on the validity of laws in Texas and Florida that sought to restrict \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and other internet giants from suppressing user speech.\par \par \uc2 Bankruptcy\par \par \uc2 Harrington v. \uc2 Purdue Pharma\par \par \uc2 Decision: 5-4\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-rejects-purdue-pharma-deal-to-shield-sackler-family-7870205a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected a bankruptcy plan  }}}\uc2 for OxyContin maker \uc2 Purdue Pharma\uc2  that would have allocated billions of dollars from members of the wealthy Sackler family to combat opioid addiction in exchange for shielding them from civil lawsuits over their alleged role in fueling the drug epidemic.\par \par \uc2 Guns\par \par \uc2 Garland v. Cargill\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-strikes-down-ban-on-bump-stock-firearm-devices-a5067238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threw out a Trump-era ban on bump stocks  }}}\uc2 , which can convert semiautomatic weapons to function like automatics. The conservative majority found that the Justice Department couldn't rely upon a 1934 law to prohibit the devices.\par \par \uc2 U.S. v. Rahimi\par \par \uc2 Decision: 8-1\par \par \uc2 The justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-says-domestic-abusers-can-be-denied-guns-39b648c0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 upheld a federal law  }}}\uc2  that forbids domestic abusers from possessing guns, ruling that the Second Amendment permits temporarily disarming people found to pose a threat to others.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Brent.Kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brent.Kendall@wsj.com  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240701ek71003ux\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
