\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 These Men Are on the Front Lines of the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 -Rights Movement; 'We need men.' Young males emerge as unexpected allies as issue of access to turns to states.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Scott Calvert | Photographs by Michelle Bruzzese for WSJ \par \uc2 1,280 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2024\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ORLANDO, Fla.\u8212\'20\'14A push to boost the ranks of men fighting for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights picked up a fresh recruit in a driveway here the other day, some four months before Florida voters will decide whether to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to their constitution and effectively scrap the state's six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 The pitch came from Ethan Temple, an earnest 20-year-old promoting the amendment in shirt-soaking humidity. More guys need to get "off the sidelines," such as by knocking on doors and rallying in support of bodily autonomy, he told Jordan Gulbronson, a web designer returning from a bagel run.\par \par \uc2 Gulbronson, 29, said he was keen to step up. The self-described pro-choice Democrat has wanted to become involved in local politics since moving to town with his wife last year. With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on the line, he said, "the stakes are incredibly high."\par \par \uc2 Nationwide there are signs that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , long seen primarily as a women's issue, has growing importance in how men vote since the Supreme Court, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 , ended a federal right to the procedure in 2022, prompting strict limits in over a dozen states.\par \par \uc2 'We need men'\par \par \uc2 Temple's group, Men4Choice, seeks to parlay that support into activism in Florida and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 A February Wall Street Journal poll found 55% of male U.S. voters think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all cases or with some restrictions, up from 51% in March 2022. Women's support rose from 58% to 66%.\par \par \uc2 Though the change in male voter attitudes is within the margin of error at a 95% confidence level, Democratic pollster Michael Bocian said men regularly cite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a defining issue.\par \par \uc2 "I see this in focus groups all the time," said Bocian, who conducts Journal surveys with Republican David Lee. "You'll have a couple men saying, 'I don't believe government should be telling women what to do with their bodies.' That never came up before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/supreme-court-turns-to-emergency-abortions-as-dobbs-fallout-continues-9bfe8c9d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs }}}\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The trend has implications for the presidential election and ballot measures in several states. Florida's proposed amendment would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access until fetal viability\u8212\'20\'14around 23 weeks\u8212\'20\'14or when medically necessary. Before the six-week ban took effect in May, Florida law barred most abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Bocian said while voters typically list \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a midtier concern, a March Journal survey of voters in seven battleground states found 14% of men said they wouldn't vote for a candidate who disagreed with them on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Nate Maier of Waukesha, Wis., is one such voter. The libertarian-minded centrist said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is his top concern due to his wife's elevated risk of ectopic pregnancy, which could require surgery to end the pregnancy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is accessible in Wisconsin, but has been in legal flux in recent years. Maier fears his wife having to leave the state for care.\par \par \uc2 "That is not a place I'd ever want to be in," said Maier, 31, a father of two who works in finance. Before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/democrats-privately-discuss-replacing-biden-on-presidential-ticket-73622773"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thursday's debate }}}\uc2 , he said he would vote for President Biden because Trump's Supreme Court picks helped overturn Roe, though he saw both men as "bad options." Now, he says, he hopes to see Biden replaced on the ticket, but would still vote Democratic if the election were tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, changing the state constitution requires a 60% supermajority. "We need men, plain and simple," said Natasha Sutherland of Floridians Protecting Freedom, the coalition leading "Yes on 4."\par \par \uc2 Critics: 'mansplaining'\par \par \uc2 The group works alongside Men4Choice, which calls Florida it is no. 1 priority this year and collected petitions before a \uc2 University of Miami\uc2  football game to help put the measure on the ballot. Temple is among 100 young men in Florida who have completed a 10-week training that involves engaging male peers in "deep conversation," says Men4Choice, founded in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 "The whole point of our organization is to get guys who are passively pro-choice but would not speak up, show up, donate\u8212\'20\'14to get them to do those things," said Florida-based Deputy Executive Director Aaron Bos-Lun. "It is the role of men to be additive, it isn't the role of men to be dictating when it comes to reproductive freedom."\par \par \uc2 Referendum opponent Jim Radloff, a lawyer and board member of the Women's Help Center in Jacksonville, said Men4Choice is "mansplaining" to women about "what's best" for them.\par \par \uc2 "Our campaign is focused on informing ALL Floridians that Amendment 4 is deceptive and extreme," said Taryn Fenske of Florida Voters Against Extremism, a key opponent to the measure. She cited wording she said would enable non-physician clinical staff to approve later abortions, and eliminate a parental-consent requirement for minors.\par \par \uc2 Sutherland rejects that, saying Amendment 4 would empower patient-doctor decisions. She pointed to amendment language she said preserved parental rights.\par \par \uc2 One recent Saturday, Yes on 4 campaigners held events statewide. In Orlando, about 30 volunteers gathered at a church east of downtown before door-knocking likely supporters. A key aim: ensure voters knew the proposed amendment is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 In the upbeat church meeting hall, people noshed on bagels and sipped coffee. A table showcased buttons saying "End the 6-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban!" Facilitators advised wedging fliers in front doors, not dropping them in mailboxes.\par \par \uc2 Men present included Temple and another college student in blue Men4Choice shirts, along with Gabriel Gomez, youth organizer for the pro-amendment campaign. Gomez, 25, traces his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to being the son of Mexican immigrants and seeing disparate treatment of certain groups in America.\par \par \uc2 "We're giving men the information and the language they need to help advocate to other men," Gomez said. "Because we know that as men, we're most likely to listen to our peers."\par \par \uc2 'Add my voice'\par \par \uc2 Canvassers dispersed into the sticky morning after a group photo that drew giddy shouts of "Limit government interference in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 !" and "Doctors not politicians!" instead of "Cheese!" Temple hopped in Gomez's Toyota for the drive to Lawsona Fern Creek, a neighborhood of bungalows among oaks draped in Spanish moss.\par \par \uc2 The men's message resonated with some not on their voter list, including Azia Parker, 43, a Republican who was outside her home.\par \par \uc2 A commercial-property manager, she shared personal stories to illustrate why she backs the amendment. One concerned her decision to have a child after getting pregnant at 17 and wrestling with what to do. Another centered on her choice to terminate a later pregnancy because of an abusive relationship.\par \par \uc2 Parker said the canvassers reminded her of her 25-year-old son, adding, "For this activism to happen is fantastic."\par \par \uc2 At another house, Gomez encountered a man who said he is a Republican but isn't against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and thinks it is necessary in some cases. The man liked Trump but disagreed with him on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Temple, a rookie door-knocker, met Gulbronson in his driveway and said he seemed a good fit for Men4Choice. "We're trying to grow our influence out across Central Florida in order to help out the community here and get more young men out and engaged," Temple said.\par \par \uc2 Gulbronson took Temple's phone number, and called two days later: he was in. He said he plans to volunteer at events and maybe call voters. "Just trying to add my voice."\par \par \uc2 Write to Scott Calvert at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:scott.calvert@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scott.calvert@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240630ek6u000ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
