\par \par \uc2 A patient from Texas sits in the waiting room at a women's health center in New Mexico. PHOTO: Adria Malcolm for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The nearly four-decade-old Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act requires emergency rooms to stabilize patients with critical medical conditions, regardless of their ability to pay, as a condition of accepting Medicare funding.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hospitals-must-provide-abortions-in-emergency-situations-biden-administration-says-11657569637"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden administration issued guidance }}}\uc2  saying the law requires doctors and hospitals to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if a woman needs one to be medically stabilized, after the Supreme Court overturned constitutional protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  two years ago in its decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization }}}\uc2 . Since the decision, the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2  has investigated hospitals that didn't provide abortions to stabilize pregnant patients under the federal law.\par \par \uc2 In a partial dissent from the Supreme Court decision, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  said doctors and patients need more clarity.\par \par \uc2 "While this court dawdles and the country waits, pregnant people experiencing emergency medical conditions remain in a precarious position, as their doctors are kept in the dark about what the law requires," Jackson wrote.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights and antiabortion groups both expressed disappointment that the decision didn't resolve questions about the high court's reading of the law.\par \par \uc2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/idaho-must-allow-abortions-in-certain-emergencies-judge-rules-11661386919"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2022 injunction }}}\uc2  on Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban allowed doctors to treat pregnant patients for emergency complications. The Supreme Court put the injunction on hold in January. The decision on Thursday allows doctors in Idaho to provide emergency abortions under federal law again.\par \par \uc2 For months, doctors in Idaho have struggled to care for pregnant patients with serious complications without violating the law. St. Luke's Health System in Idaho transferred six pregnant patients in the first three months of this year to other states for emergency care, compared with one in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Stacy Seyb, a maternal-fetal medicine physician at St. Luke's, cared for at least two such patients and has advised on at least two others. One patient's water broke at 18 weeks, which can lead to an infection endangering the mother and fetus. Terminating the pregnancy can be necessary in such cases. An 18-week-old fetus isn't considered viable outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 Seyb sued Idaho state and county officials in May, arguing that the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is unjust. "I don't want to wait until someone is on death's doorstep," he said. "That's just not good medicine."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/issue-brief/a-review-of-exceptions-in-state-abortions-bans-implications-for-the-provision-of-abortion-services/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have varying exceptions }}}\uc2 . Some require doctors to use their best medical judgment to determine whether an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is needed to prevent death or physical impairment.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should hospitals be allowed to provide abortions in dire circumstances even in states that now ban the procedure? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers and antiabortion groups say these laws allow doctors to make a decision without waiting until patients are on the verge of death. They say that hospitals have narrowly interpreted medical exceptions and that doctors are politicizing tragic cases.\par \par \uc2 Pregnant women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/the-tragic-pregnancy-stories-filling-the-abortion-campaign-airwaves-74c4fa41"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have described being made to wait }}}\uc2  until they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/fall-of-roe-v-wade-upends-abortion-landscape-for-american-women-11656081225"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 developed sepsis }}}\uc2  or hemorrhaging before being deemed eligible for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some states. Doctors in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have had patients airlifted or driven elsewhere to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Transferring pregnant patients with life-threatening conditions to another state for care the hospital could provide is risky and expensive, said Dr. David Hackney, a maternal-fetal medicine physician in Cleveland and associate professor at \uc2 Case Western Reserve University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's just a bad idea on multiple levels," he said.\par \par \uc2 Katy Dulong didn't know she had a severe infection until her second hospital visit in November 2022.\par \par \uc2 Just over a week earlier, bleeding and cramping, Dulong was diagnosed with cervical insufficiency and told to go to the hospital. There, doctors said her child wouldn't survive. They told her they couldn't perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the risk she would develop an infection because of Tennessee's restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 About 10 days later, a nurse at another hospital smelled an odor indicating an infection during her exam. Her doctor said Dulong's life was at risk, consulted legal and hospital staff, and performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I would've been dead soon if I had not received this," said Dulong, who is among patients who have joined lawsuits challenging state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws after such experiences.\par \par \uc2 Doctors in the U.S. are trained to consider \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address life-threatening conditions such as early severe preeclampsia, an infection that occurs when the water breaks prematurely, and existing heart or kidney disease that can worsen during pregnancy, maternal-fetal medicine physicians said.\par \par \uc2 "Because of all these state laws that try to get in between that patient-physician relationship, it makes the practice of medicine that much harder," said Dr. Bobby Mukkamala, president-elect of the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last summer, a patient in her second trimester arrived bleeding through her clothes at Dr. Jennifer Smith's practice in St. Louis. Under Missouri's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, Smith said the only option was to see whether her condition worsened. The patient asked her husband to drive her to Illinois.\par \par \uc2 "She felt a lack of confidence that we could care for her like we could've before Dobbs," Smith said.\par \par \uc2 Some states have tried to clarify these laws through legislation or guidance from health officials. Healthcare systems have created their own guidelines. The result is policies that vary among states, cities and institutions.\par \par \uc2 Texas' medical board on June 21 approved guidance on how doctors can interpret exemptions to its state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. That followed a state Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/texas-supreme-court-declines-to-expand-access-to-emergency-abortions-37ada1af"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling last month }}}\uc2  that said doctors would be misinterpreting the law by delaying an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a pregnant woman at risk of death or long-term physical impairment.\par \par \uc2 The board's president, Dr. Sherif Zaafran, said prosecutors should defer to the board on whether an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  qualifies under the state's medical exemptions.\par \par \uc2 "There is no right for anyone other than a physician to make that judgment," Zaafran said.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Laura Kusisto contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Betsy McKay at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:betsy.mckay@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 betsy.mckay@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240627ek6r001jo\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
