\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Vindication of Dobbs After Two Years; Letting voters decide on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  has energized the democratic process.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 699 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2024\par 21:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats are loudly bemoaning the second anniversary of the Supreme Court's ruling overturning Roe v. Wade this week, and that's because they think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will help them politically. Yet that political frenzy is actually a vindication of the Court's 6-3 Dobbs decision and Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's opinion.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision, which returned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to the states and the voters, was correct as a matter of constitutional law. It overturned what even liberal jurists in 1973 and since recognized was one of the High Court's worst decisions.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito wrote that he had no idea how the voters would sort out the issue, but two years later it's clear that Dobbs is letting democracy work. Despite the left's predictions, the fall of Roe hasn't ushered in a "Handmaid's Tale" dystopia. Some conservative states have restricted many or most abortions, while some liberal states have moved to become sanctuaries.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  said this year "that an estimated 1,037,000 abortions occurred in the formal health care system in 2023." Even after Dobbs, that was up 11% from 2020, and is the highest figure in a decade. More women are traveling across state lines, sometimes with financial help from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds. This activity is likely protected under the constitutional right to travel, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/can-a-state-stop-abortion-travel-alabama-5fd4de51"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a federal court recently ruled}}}\uc2 , and as Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  suggested in his Dobbs concurrence.\par \par \uc2 Another factor, according to Guttmacher, is "broader availability of telehealth for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." This month the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-fda-v-alliance-for-hippocratic-medicine-mifepristone-starbucks-v-mckinney-nlrb-aa4b8e9b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unanimously rejected}}}\uc2  a challenge to the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's regulations on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone. So much for the Supreme Court serving as a rubber stamp for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians.\par \par \uc2 The other piece of the story is that the Dobbs ruling energized the democratic process, with results that have sometimes been surprising. Residents of Kansas, Ohio and Michigan voted in referendums to keep or add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in their constitutions. Those outcomes were driven in part by highly motivated Democrats, but the Ohio initiative won counties that President Trump carried in 2020 with 60% or more, so there were obviously swing voters.\par \par \uc2 Is Florida next? Florida's Republican Legislature last year banned most abortions after six weeks. But Democrats have responded by making that vote a centerpiece of their campaign in November.\par \par \uc2 That includes putting on the ballot a constitutional amendment that goes further than Roe in some ways. "No law," the text says, "shall prohibit, penalize, delay, or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability or when necessary to protect the patient's health, as determined by the patient's healthcare provider."\par \par \uc2 Amending Florida's constitution takes 60%, a high bar. A recent Fox News poll said the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment had 69% support.\par \par \uc2 Public sentiment on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more complicated than either party likes to admit. To cite the Gallup results from the first Dobbs anniversary, 69% of Americans said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be generally legal during the first three months of pregnancy. But in the second three months, 55% said illegal, including 52% of women.\par \par \uc2 That's the reality of American opinion, but for decades after Roe, politicians were free to ignore it. The Supreme Court had made the law on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so elected officials treated it as a political totem.\par \par \uc2 Now politicians have to take their positions seriously, because they're actually empowered to make policy. President Biden and Democrats in Congress want to pass a federal law to impose a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/chuck-schumers-radical-abortion-bill-senate-democrats-roe-v-wade-womens-health-protection-act-11652133702"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 would go beyond Roe}}}\uc2 , but it could prove unpopular, and it can't get past a Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are adjusting, albeit slowly, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is probably right about the politics. "You don't need a federal ban," he told Time magazine recently. "I'm leaving everything up to the states. The states are going to be different. Some will say yes. Some will say no. Texas is different than Ohio."\par \par \uc2 Two years after Dobbs, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is being settled by the electorate, and it isn't always predictable, but that's democracy.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240624ek6o004xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
