\par\par \uc2 Health Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Antiabortion Lawsuits Leaned on Discredited, Disputed Research; Peer-reviewed studies were later challenged, disavowed or retracted\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nidhi Subbaraman \par \uc2 1,306 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2024\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Recent efforts to roll back {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-abortion-pill-mifepristone-f78f3320"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  have relied in part on certain scientific studies that have been disputed, disavowed or subsequently retracted.\par \par \uc2 These studies, which raise questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  safety, were published in peer-reviewed journals but were later faulted for having irreproducible results, methodological flaws or misleading presentations of data, among other issues.\par \par \uc2 Three studies that have been severely criticized have turned up in more than two dozen lawsuits over the past decade, most frequently in friend-of-the-court briefs, including Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the case that led the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade, and \uc2 FDA\uc2  v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, the Texas case that\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/health/healthcare/abortion-pill-case-puts-supreme-court-back-in-the-hot-seat-dec8edad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought litigation over the \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pill}}}\uc2  to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The authors of the studies contend that efforts to discredit their work are politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Professional associations and medical groups including the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  and the \uc2 American Psychological Association\uc2 , say the overwhelming evidence indicates abortions are safe.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at the research.\par \par \uc2 Emergency room visits\par \par \uc2 One study asserting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill was associated with a surge in emergency-room visits by women who had recently had abortions was cited in more than a dozen filings in the Texas mifepristone case, and by U.S. District Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk in his since-overturned decision in that case invalidating the pill's approval.\par \par \uc2 The paper was published by the peer-reviewed journal Health Services Research and Managerial Epidemiology in 2021. It was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/23333928211053965"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 retracted}}}\uc2  in February.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How should scientific misinformation be addressed? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 According to the retraction notice, the study contains misleading figures, and the authors didn't disclose conflicts of interest. Seven of the study's eight co-authors were affiliated with antiabortion groups but the lead author answered "no" to the question: "Do you have any conflicts of interest?" when the article was submitted for publication, according to a spokesperson for \uc2 Sage\uc2 , the publisher of the journal.\par \par \uc2 "We have become visible, people are quoting us, and for that reason we are dangerous, and for that reason they want to cancel our work," James Studnicki, lead author of the study, said in a video responding to the retraction.\par \par \uc2 Studnicki is vice president and director of data analytics for the Charlotte Lozier Institute, which opposes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/abortion-rights-advocates-deploy-a-new-red-state-playbook-8f13a644"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 . He was lead author on two other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  studies retracted by the same journal for errors and misleading presentations of the data, according to the retraction notices.\par \par \uc2 The study said that rates of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ER visits from women 30 days after the procedure rose between 1999 and 2015, with visit rates for women who had taken mifepristone rising more steeply, signaling the harm caused by the pill.\par \par \uc2 One flaw was that the paper used emergency-room visits as an indicator of safety, whereas women are known to visit the ER as a first stop for information or reassurance that side effects such as bleeding are normal, according to Ushma Upadhyay, a public-health scientist at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.contraceptionjournal.org/article/S0010-7824(24)00070-2/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published}}}\uc2  a critique of Studnicki's paper in March in the journal Contraception.\par \par \uc2 Studnicki said he stands by the work.\par \par \uc2 Anxiety, depression\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  study that has attracted the most criticism\u8212\'20\'14and was cited by Kacsmaryk in his opinion in the Texas case\u8212\'20\'14concluded that women who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were 81% more likely to experience mental-health problems.\par \par \uc2 When the study, a meta analysis of 22 papers, was published in the British Journal of Psychiatry in 2011, it provoked a volley of comments to the journal criticizing the methods.\par \par \uc2 Some months later, in a{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3646711/pdf/nihms457998.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 }}}\uc2 peer-reviewed study {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.contraceptionjournal.org/article/S0010-7824(12)00150-3/abstract"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published}}}\uc2  in the journal Contraception, a group of scientists pointed to "fatal flaws" in the methodology, noting that more than half the studies were "less than poor quality" and that half had been written by the author analyzing the body of work.\par \par \uc2 Some of that group of scientists asked the journal to retract the paper as recently as June 2022.\par \par \uc2 In response, the journal tapped a group of editorial board members to examine the evidence. They recommended a retraction, according to Alexander Tsai, associate professor of psychiatry at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 , and Aileen O'Brien, a research psychiatrist at St. George's, University of London, who were members of the panel.\par \par \uc2 The journal closed the matter without retracting the study, prompting Tsai, O'Brien and some other members of the panel to resign from the journal's board.\par \par \uc2 "We did an investigation and came up with a recommendation," O'Brien said. "That recommendation was overruled. That struck me as not respecting editorial independence, which is a slippery slope."\par \par \uc2 Priscilla Coleman, a research psychologist who conducted the study, said the call for retraction was "politically motivated and the criticisms were based on the signatories' biases." She has served as an expert witness in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases.\par \par \uc2 The paper has been cited in more than 20 U.S. court cases, including in petitions submitted to the Supreme Court, such as a petition {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/BL-LB-46422"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 from Arizona in 2013}}}\uc2  to reinstate a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 20 weeks of pregnancy, which the Supreme Court didn't take up, and a petition from the state of Mississippi to consider the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , resulting in the Dobbs decision in June 2022.\par \par \uc2 The American Psychological Association and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists in friend-of-the-court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.apa.org/about/offices/ogc/amicus/alliance-hippocratic-medicine.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 briefs}}}\uc2  submitted in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases including \uc2 FDA\uc2  v. Alliance cited several studies that find \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have a negative impact on women's mental health.\par \par \uc2 Severe mental disorders\par \par \uc2 In an earlier study, Coleman found that women who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were at higher risk of developing severe mental-health disorders such as PTSD, bipolar disorder and substance-abuse disorders, compared with women who hadn't had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This study, published in the Journal of Psychiatric Research in 2008, has been cited in at least six federal court cases, including in a friend-of-the-court brief submitted to Kacsmaryk in the Texas case.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S027795361000729X"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2010 paper}}}\uc2  published in the peer-reviewed journal Social Science and Medicine, two scientists said they couldn't replicate the results.\par \par \uc2 One of the authors, Lawrence Finer, was at the time associated with the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/health/abortion-pill-increase-roe-wade-68472ff8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 group that supports \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 . The other, \uc2 Julia Steinberg\uc2 , a social psychologist who studies psychology and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the University of Maryland, has appeared as an expert witness in court cases for groups that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, including \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In response to their critique, Coleman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022395611001269"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued}}}\uc2  a correction, which the authors said pointed to further flaws in her work.\par \par \uc2 Two independent reviewers of this exchange\u8212\'20\'14Alan Schatzberg, the co-editor of the journal that published the study, and Ronald Kessler, a professor of healthcare policy at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2  who had collected the survey data used in the study\u8212\'20\'14{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022395612000325"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote a commentary}}}\uc2  that said Coleman's "analysis does not support their assertions that abortions led to psychopathology."\par \par \uc2 Schatzberg, a professor of behavioral science and psychiatry at \uc2 Stanford University School of Medicine\uc2 , said the commentary was sufficient to correct the record.\par \par \uc2 "The issues were resolved in a manner acceptable to the journal," Coleman said, "and no further concerns were raised for 12 years."\par \par \uc2 In 2023, a group of researchers once again pointed to flaws in the study and asked for a retraction. Coleman said she sent a rebuttal to the editor last year.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Elsevier\uc2 , the publisher of the journal, said an investigation is ongoing and declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Gershman contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Nidhi Subbaraman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:nidhi.subbaraman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nidhi.subbaraman@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240618ek6i001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
