\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 How Telehealth Widens Access to the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill; Without telehealth, one in seven women would have to drive over 200 miles to the nearest provider\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kara Dapena \par \uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par 14:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court rejected an effort seeking to restrict access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone, ruling that a group of antiabortion doctors who filed suit had no legal standing to challenge Food and Drug Administration regulations for prescribing the drug.\par \par \uc2 Telehealth for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became available in 2020 when the \uc2 FDA\uc2  stopped enforcing its in-person dispensing requirement and allowed certified mail-order pharmacies to ship pills to patients during the pandemic. These changes became permanent in 2023, when the agency also allowed certified bricks-and-mortar pharmacies to dispense the pills for the first time.\par \par \uc2 In September 2023, some 16% of all abortions within the formal U.S. healthcare system were performed via telehealth, according to WeCount, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -data project sponsored by the Society of Family Planning, which supports {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/abortion-rights-advocates-deploy-a-new-red-state-playbook-8f13a644"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights  }}}\uc2 . Twenty-six states and D.C. allow telehealth for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The remaining states have restrictions that supersede federal guidance: 14 ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy, and the remaining 10 have various combinations of in-person requirements, such as mandatory ultrasounds and visits to doctors and counselors.\par \par \uc2 Patients in states that don't permit abortions can currently turn to telehealth providers in many other states. Providers in some states are protected by so-called shield laws designed to protect them from liability or prosecution if they provide pills to patients from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned. Some telehealth shield laws only protect the provider if the patient travels to the provider's state to terminate the pregnancy, and others protect the provider regardless of what state the patient is in. Seventeen states plus D.C. have shield laws that pertain to telemedicine, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Initiative for Telehealth Equity & Solutions, an organization that advocates for telehealth in reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen states currently ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy. This has put further distance between providers and many patients compared with before the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization case that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminated a federal right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . The court could reinstate protocols that required patients to see a doctor in person three times to receive the pill regimen, effectively blocking telehealth nationwide. If pills could no longer be mailed, women would be forced to travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the states that ban nearly all abortions, 59% of reproductive-age women must drive at least 200 miles to the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, according to January figures from Caitlin Myers, an economist at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  who studies the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and maintains a database of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities. That is up from 3% in the months before the Dobbs decision. Nationally, about 14% of reproductive-age women live at least 200 miles from the closest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, now.\par \par \uc2 Despite greater driving distances for many patients compared with pre-Dobbs, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate has ticked up since the Dobbs ruling, and an increased share of abortions are via medication, growing from 53% of U.S. abortions in 2020 to 63% in 2023, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. A Supreme Court ruling is expected in June.\par \par \uc2 Max Rust contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kara Dapena at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:kara.dapena@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 kara.dapena@wsj.com  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240613ek6d003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
