\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Rejects  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill Challenge, Preserving Wide Access to Drug; Justices hand loss to antiabortion groups who sought to restrict most common method for ending a pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Jess Bravin \par \uc2 1,310 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par 21:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Supreme Court on Thursday unanimously rejected an effort seeking to restrict {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-arguments-abortion-pill-mifepristone-c31de31a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to the \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pill mifepristone }}}\uc2 , preserving for now the widespread availability of the most common method American women use to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The case was the first major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue to come before the court since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its 2022 decision overturning Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  and rescinding the federal constitutional right to the procedure that it had recognized since 1973. That ruling bitterly divided the court over constitutional views on individual rights and set off a series of state-level battles that have expanded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the Northeast and Pacific Coast while largely outlawing the procedure in most of the South and parts of the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 But Thursday's case, filed by the Christian conservative group \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  on behalf of doctors and groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than anyone who had been harmed by the drug, turned on procedural questions involving the right to sue. In that area, the most ardent opponents of Roe and its most determined defenders found common ground.\par \par \uc2 "We recognize that many citizens, including the plaintiff doctors here, have sincere concerns about and objections to others using mifepristone and obtaining abortions," Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  wrote for the court. But "a plaintiff 's desire to make a drug less available for others does not establish standing to sue," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Philosophical or political objections to mifepristone were better addressed to legislators and regulators who set policy, rather than to courts which hear claims over concrete injuries, Kavanaugh wrote.\par \par \uc2 Because the case lacked a proper plaintiff, the court left for another day {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-ivf-republicans-election-2024-f044dc5b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 potentially high-stakes legal questions }}}\uc2 about how the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  decided to make mifepristone easier to obtain. It is possible those issues, as well as other questions about sending the pills in the mail, could return soon because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are continuing to press their arguments in court with other challengers.\par \par \uc2 The decision overturned lower federal courts that had accepted the theory that someday a woman suffering from complications from mifepristone could present herself for treatment to one of the plaintiff physicians, thereby giving the doctor a stake in the drug's availability.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court rejected that theory as too speculative, noting that no such instance had been documented in the decades since the drug first was approved.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, Kavanaugh wrote, even if such a patient someday should arrive at their doorstep, no doctors could be compelled to end her pregnancy. "Federal law fully protects doctors against being required to provide abortions or other medical treatment against their consciences," he wrote, even if no other physician is available to assist the patient.\par \par \uc2 The decision marks a win for the Biden administration, which defended \uc2 FDA\uc2  protocols on prescribing the drug, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/abortion-rights-advocates-deploy-a-new-red-state-playbook-8f13a644"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -rights groups }}}\uc2  that have seen access to the procedure dwindle in states conservatives control.\par \par \uc2 But the court isn't done yet with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this term; still pending is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/supreme-court-turns-to-emergency-abortions-as-dobbs-fallout-continues-9bfe8c9d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a separate case out of Idaho }}}\uc2  on whether a federal law that requires hospitals to provide stabilizing care for patients at risk of serious injury allows doctors to perform emergency abortions in such circumstances, even when state law permits the procedure only to save a woman's life.\par \par \uc2 The pill lawsuit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/health/healthcare/abortion-pill-case-puts-supreme-court-back-in-the-hot-seat-dec8edad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had threatened to restrict access }}}\uc2  to the drug even in most states, including New York, Illinois and California, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal. It also could have cut off a pathway women in states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have used to terminate pregnancies: obtaining the pills by mail from out-of-state providers.\par \par \uc2 The ease of obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail has bedeviled conservative lawmakers and lawyers since the 2022 case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, overruled Roe. Women can obtain these pills in some cases for $5 or less and they can be sent in unmarked envelopes across state and international borders with little prospect of detection. Antiabortion groups said they would continue to pursue litigation in an effort to get judges to go beyond the technical question of standing that the Supreme Court addressed Thursday to whether the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has adequately studied the safety of the drug.\par \par \uc2 "We are very hopeful that the federal courts will have a chance to hold the \uc2 FDA\uc2  accountable," said Erin Hawley, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/erin-hawley-abortion-pill-supreme-court-03c14274"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued the case for the Alliance Defending Freedom }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Biden in a statement said the ruling "does not change the fact that the right for a woman to get the treatment she needs is imperiled if not impossible in many states."\par \par \uc2 "Let's be clear: attacks on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are part of Republican elected officials' extreme and dangerous agenda to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide," Biden said.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/lawsuit-filed-to-reverse-approval-access-to-abortion-pill-11668796816"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought the challenge }}}\uc2  in November 2022, arguing the \uc2 FDA\uc2  had improperly approved the drug two decades ago under a pathway for medications used to treat serious and life-threatening illness. They also said the agency had failed to properly study the safety of more recent changes, including allowing the drug to be administered without an in-person visit to a medical provider.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Danco, which manufactures the brand-name version of the drug, said the decision "safeguards access to a drug that has decades of safe and effective use."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has said the pill has proven safe over years of use by millions of women and that exhaustive scientific research supported more recent changes loosening restrictions on the drug. The agency said in a legal brief that as of December 2022, 32 deaths had been reported among nearly six million women who have taken mifepristone and some of those had obvious alternative causes, such as drug overdose.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit took a dramatic ride through the courts. A federal district judge last year suspended approval of mifepristone altogether, though that ruling never went into effect.\par \par \uc2 An appeals panel later {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/health/pharma/appeals-panel-rules-fda-improperly-expanded-access-to-abortion-pill-4ffbd3b8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rolled back much of the ruling }}}\uc2 , saying it was too late to challenge the drug's original approval. But the appeals court did find that the plaintiffs had standing to sue, and it ruled the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's efforts beginning in 2016 to make the pill more available were unlawful. The Supreme Court had previously put that ruling on hold, preserving the status quo of widespread mifepristone access while it considered the case.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -pill litigation had been a key part of the antiabortion movement's strategy since Roe was overturned. Even after Thursday's decision, efforts by conservatives to limit access to the pill are likely to continue. Three Republican state attorneys general have intervened in the litigation, arguing they have standing to sue because their states have incurred additional healthcare costs to treat women who have suffered physical and mental side effects from taking the pill.\par \par \uc2 Some antiabortion groups also hope that if former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins a second presidential term, his administration will enforce the Comstock Act, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/a-19th-century-law-could-help-influence-the-abortion-pills-future-639dddb6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 19th-century law }}}\uc2  that outlawed shipping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs across state lines.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana this year became the first state in the nation to classify mifepristone, and another commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug known as misoprostol, as Schedule IV controlled substances, putting more guardrails around the drugs. Antiabortion groups are urging other states to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 On the other side, the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  has called for the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to add miscarriage as an official use of mifepristone\u8212\'20\'14a move that could make it more widely available in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is outlawed, as well as make women more comfortable filling a prescription for it.\par \par \uc2 Liz Essley Whyte contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jess.Bravin@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jess.Bravin@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240613ek6d0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
