\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2 's Strains Intensify as Term's End Approaches; Ideological differences, ethical questions test justices wrestling with decisions on Trump,  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  and guns\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 1,283 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2024\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court justices are completing decisions at the second slowest rate since the 1946 term. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said it was the slowest rate. (Corrected on June 9)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14\uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  justices take pride in a collegiality that transcends their philosophical differences. As the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-cases-2024-7e779f6e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court's term heads to its close}}}\uc2 , with pending cases that could remake the presidential election and ethical questions dogging its members, that politesse might be all the nine have left in common.\par \par \uc2 Over the past month, internal fissures and external pressure have intensified at the court. Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , speaking at the Harvard Radcliffe Institute, said she sometimes weeps in her chambers after the conservative majority issues one of its polarizing rulings.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who might have written some of the opinions Sotomayor had in mind, told a judicial conference in Alabama that he and his wife, Ginni, a conservative activist who encouraged Republican officials to overturn the\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-courts-justice-clarence-thomas-faces-scrutiny-over-wifes-texts-11648231403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2020 election}}}\uc2 , have faced nastiness and lies. Thomas, who has drawn criticism for accepting gifts from wealthy friends, on Friday disclosed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/justice-clarence-thomas-discloses-2019-trips-gifted-by-gop-donor-5f3f69c1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two additional vacations}}}\uc2  he accepted in 2019 from his longtime benefactor, the Dallas developer and Republican megadonor \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the midst of scrutiny and sagging approval ratings, the court is behind on its work. With less than a month remaining in the current term, the justices have yet to issue rulings in nearly half the cases on their docket. The court's next decisions are expected Thursday.\par \par \uc2 People familiar with the court's workings said that when things are going well, decisions are produced at a regular clip, with the majority and dissenters keeping to internal deadlines for filing opinions, responding to each other's positions, and moving on to the next case. By that measure, the augurs for this term are poor.\par \par \uc2 The court heard only 61 cases in the 2023-24 term, close to a historic low. The 29 cases it still needs to decide include the most contentious disputes, involving not only federal prosecution of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/supreme-court-turns-to-emergency-abortions-as-dobbs-fallout-continues-9bfe8c9d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access}}}\uc2 , gun rights, online speech and the powers of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-to-reconsider-precedent-giving-federal-agencies-benefit-of-doubt-443facf1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 regulatory agencies}}}\uc2 . According to Adam Feldman, creator of the Empirical \uc2 Scotus\uc2  blog, the justices are completing decisions at the second slowest rate since the 1946 term.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What is your view of the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2 ? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The pace suggests that justices, those in the majority and dissenters alike, are going into overtime as they repeatedly exchange opposing opinions, fighting over who gets the last word on issues of major national importance, according to court observers.\par \par \uc2 The justices "are hammer and tongs against each other at this point," said Daniel Ortiz, a \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  law professor who for years directed the school's \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  litigation clinic. "There's a lot of ill will and anger that's been building up, and now that they are in the crucible, it's just going to get worse."\par \par \uc2 Leading the list of pending cases are two stemming from the Jan. 6, 2021, riot at the Capitol, one over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/donald-trump-immunity-supreme-court-case-b89f1519"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump's claim of immunity}}}\uc2  for alleged crimes committed in office, and the other involving the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-takes-up-jan-6-case-that-could-affect-hundreds-of-prosecutions-d56be243"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 validity of obstruction charges}}}\uc2  filed against Trump as well as Jan. 6 rioters.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers called on Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  to recuse himself from those cases after reports that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/alito-rejects-recusal-calls-over-flying-of-maga-associated-flags-at-his-homes-1e8dcd9a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MAGA-associated flags}}}\uc2  flew from his homes in Virginia and New Jersey. Alito said no, declaring that his impartiality in the cases couldn't reasonably be questioned\u8212\'20\'14the legal standard\u8212\'20\'14because it was his wife, Martha-Ann Alito, who raised the flags, at times over his objection.\par \par \uc2 Senators including Judiciary Committee Chairman \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  (D., Ill.) requested a sit-down with Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  over the court's ethics practices. Roberts declined. "Such a meeting would be inadvisable," he wrote, citing "separation of powers concerns and the importance of preserving judicial independence."\par \par \uc2 Public perceptions of that independence, particularly among Democrats and unaffiliated voters, have been ebbing since 2020, when Trump appointed \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  to succeed the late Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , expanding the court's narrow conservative tilt to a supermajority ready to reconsider long-established precedents. A May poll by Marquette University Law School found that 61% of registered voters disapproved of the court; 54% said the justices were primarily motivated by politics rather than the law.\par \par \uc2 High-court tensions already are evident in some cases that justices have decided. In a 6-3 opinion in May involving a South Carolina congressional seat, Alito scaled back minority voters' ability to challenge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/supreme-court-restores-white-dominated-congressional-district-in-south-carolina-5bb5106c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 political districts as racial gerrymanders}}}\uc2 , laying out a doctrine requiring federal courts to presume the good faith of state legislatures that draw the maps.\par \par \uc2 In dissent, Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  accused Alito of turning a 2017 precedent she wrote "upside down"\u8212\'20\'14by treating the dissent he wrote to her opinion seven years ago on the standard for assessing racial-gerrymandering appeals as the binding rule today. "The majority ignores it\u8212\'20\'14no, worse, does the opposite of what the standard commands," Kagan wrote.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps it was a coincidence, but Kagan's phrasing followed a New York Times report on the upside-down American flag flown at Alito's Alexandria, Va., home in January 2021, like some flags carried by the pro-Trump mob that attacked the Capitol days earlier.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/supreme-court-ruling-trump-ballot-colorado-092852fe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decided a major Trump case}}}\uc2  in March, restoring the presumptive Republican presidential nominee's ballot eligibility after the Colorado Supreme Court deemed him ineligible. The state court cited a constitutional bar on electing former officials who engaged in insurrection.\par \par \uc2 All nine \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices agreed that states couldn't enforce the provision against presidential candidates, but Sotomayor, Kagan and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  filed a separate opinion complaining that the majority went out of its way to protect Trump from other potential challenges. Barrett agreed that the majority went too far, but in her own separate opinion, chastised the liberals for rocking the boat.\par \par \uc2 "This isn't the time to amplify disagreement with stridency," Barrett said. "Writings on the Court should turn the national temperature down, not up."\par \par \uc2 People close to the court say that internal rifts go beyond philosophical differences between the six-member conservative majority, all appointed by Republican presidents, and the three liberals chosen by Democratic presidents. Bad blood remains from the 2022 decision overruling Roe v. Wade, which was leaked nearly two months before the majority and dissenting opinions were finished. The leak fueled distrust between chambers where confidentiality once was assured.\par \par \uc2 The rift over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued into the current term, where two cases involving the procedure remain to be decided. In one, the Biden administration is asking the justices to overrule a federal appeals court in New Orleans that reimposed restrictions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone that, beginning in 2016, the Food and Drug Administration found unnecessary. If the appeals court decision is upheld, women once again would need to obtain the medication in person from a medical practitioner rather than through a mail order or local pharmacy.\par \par \uc2 The other involves a conflict between an Idaho state law banning nearly all abortions and a federal statute requiring emergency rooms to provide necessary medical treatment, which the Biden administration argues in some health crises could include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also pending is the court's first major test of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/a-skeptical-supreme-court-considers-whether-domestic-abusers-have-gun-rights-51e52b40"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expansive gun-rights doctrine}}}\uc2  it announced in 2022, which holds weapons regulations valid only if they are analogous to those typical of the founding era more than 200 years ago. At issue is the validity of a federal law banning people who are under domestic-abuse restraining orders from possessing firearms.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jess.Bravin@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jess.Bravin@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240609ek690008f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
