\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Senate Republicans Block Bill Protecting Access to Contraception; Democratic leader Schumer sees fallout from 2022  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling galvanizing voters again this fall\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsay Wise and Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,285 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2024\par 21:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14Senate Republicans blocked a Democratic measure that would protect access to contraception nationwide, in a vote designed to show the contrast between the two parties on women's reproductive choices headed into the fall election.\par \par \uc2 The bill, which included protections for emergency contraception pills such as Plan B, was backed by 51 senators, with 39 opposed, short of the 60 votes needed to advance under chamber rules.\par \par \uc2 The measure was the first in a series of votes planned by Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.) to paint Republicans as not only against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but opposed to contraception and in vitro fertilization as well. It is part of an election-year strategy that Schumer and other top Democrats think will galvanize suburban women, a crucial group of swing voters.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, most of whom support restrictions on abortions, accuse Democrats of fear mongering and say there is no real threat to contraception or IVF in the courts or in Congress. They said that the Democrats' contraception legislation would create sweeping protections that trample on religious liberties and parental rights.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Schumer said he is eager to continue needling Republicans on the issue ahead of November's elections. Nearly two years after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court ended federal constitutional protections for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2 , he said the debate is more politically potent than ever.\par \par \uc2 "It's an even bigger issue in the minds of the American people" than it was in 2022, he said.\par \par \uc2 Schumer says Republicans are outside the mainstream on reproductive issues, and that forcing them on the record makes their positions clear to the public. He argues that many people thought Roe v. Wade's protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would never be repealed, until they were.\par \par \uc2 "Look what happened," he said. "We have to be on guard."\par \par \uc2 Sen. Steve Daines (R., Mont.) said the votes show Schumer's desperation. Democrats control the Senate 51-49 but face an uphill battle in the fall to keep the majority.\par \par \uc2 "It's a political stunt," said Daines, who chairs Senate Republicans' campaign arm. "And they're just doing this to try to create an issue because they can't argue on any other issues right now, because they're losing on inflation, they're losing on the border, they're losing on national security, they're losing on energy."\par \par \uc2 Two Republicans\u8212\'20\'14centrist Sens. \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  of Alaska and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine\u8212\'20\'14crossed party lines to support advancing the contraception bill. "I sent a message that says I support women's access to contraception. If this is a messaging vote, that's my message," Murkowski said. "This is contraception! We thought we resolved this 50, 60 years ago!"\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What role do you think issues around \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  will play in the 2024 elections? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 She said her party could do a better job talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision that ended the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think it's been less than clear," Murkowski said.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats filed a petition this week to try to force a vote on their own contraception bill, and the Biden campaign said Wednesday it would be organizing events in swing states around the Senate vote and the Friday anniversary marking the 1965 Supreme Court decision in Griswold v. Connecticut, in which the justices ruled that the constitution protects the right of married couples to use contraception.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, Schumer said, the next step will be another vote on a package of bills that would establish a statutory right to access IVF services, allow servicemembers to freeze their eggs before deployment to a combat zone or other hazardous assignment, and require employer-sponsored and public insurance plans to cover fertility treatments.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have proven popular with voters in a politically diverse mix of states, including Michigan, Ohio and Kansas. The issue could be on the ballot in at least a half-dozen states in November, including in Senate battlegrounds such as Arizona, Montana and Florida. Bringing contraception and IVF into the conversation is more novel and it remains to be seen whether voters will be persuaded those rights are under threat.\par \par \uc2 Democrats point to the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2 's ruling in February that embryos were considered children under the law. The decision briefly upended IVF access in the state before the state's Republican legislature passed a bill shielding IVF providers from civil and criminal liability.\par \par \uc2 An Arizona Supreme Court decision in April, which revived {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-ban-821501cb?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Civil War-era law banning \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  throughout pregnancy }}}\uc2  also helped Democrats make the argument to voters that their rights are under constant threat. A small number of Republicans then joined with Democrats in the state {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/arizona-abortion-ban-repeal-f3f0a530?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to repeal the law.  }}}\par \par \uc2 Former President {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/topics/person/donald-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump }}}\uc2 , the presumptive Republican nominee, stirred up new interest in contraception last month when he was asked in an interview whether he would support restrictions and responded that his campaign was "looking at that." He added that the issue has "a lot to do with the states."\par \par \uc2 State lawmakers have passed about a dozen laws protecting access to contraception this year\u8212\'20\'14often with bipartisan support\u8212\'20\'14according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national and state statistics. Republicans in several states, including Virginia, Nevada and Arizona, blocked similar measures.\par \par \uc2 A handful of states considered bills this year that would require parental notification or consent when minors obtain contraception. Tennessee passed a bill allowing parents access to children's medical records regarding contraception, according to Guttmacher.\par \par \uc2 Some antiabortion groups and Republicans oppose the use of some popular forms of contraceptives, including intrauterine devices, or IUDs, and the morning-after pill Plan B, a hormonal medication that can prevent pregnancy after unprotected sex. They say these are abortifacients that end unborn life.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  (R., Iowa) said that Plan B was a "red line" for her. She has introduced an alternative bill that could ease access to certain contraceptives without a prescription but excludes emergency contraceptives. "My goal is to prevent pregnancy, not end pregnancy," she said.\par \par \uc2 None of the Food and Drug Administration-approved contraceptive methods, including the IUD or morning-after pill, are abortifacients because they don't interfere with a pregnancy and aren't effective after a fertilized egg has implanted successfully in the uterus, according to the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a recent Wall Street Journal poll of seven battleground states, 39% of suburban women cited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the most important issue for their vote. Nearly three-quarters of them said the procedure should be legal all or most of the time, and a majority said Trump's policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were too restrictive.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  (R., Fla.) said voters aren't asking him about contraception. "They're asking me about inflation. They're asking me about the border. They're asking me about foreign policy," Scott said.\par \par \uc2 But Sen. Tim Kaine (D., Va.) said Republicans discount the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at their own peril. "I'm not going to deny the economy is important," Kaine said. "But to say to voters, 'You don't care about something'\u8212\'20\'14 they're gonna tell you what they care about. And in election after election after election since Dobbs, they've been telling us what they care about."\par \par \uc2 Anthony DeBarros and Catherine Lucey contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lindsay Wise at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:lindsay.wise@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lindsay.wise@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240605ek65004ph\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
