\par \par \uc2 Amanda Zurawski, the lead plaintiff in the case. PHOTO: Caleb Alvarado for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The Texas Supreme Court on Friday rejected a challenge from women who argued that medical exceptions to the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were inadequate, ruling that state law already provides cover for physicians to provide the procedure before a patient is on the brink of death.\par \par \uc2 The unanimous ruling said doctors are misinterpreting the law if they delay treatment when patients are at risk of death or a long-term impairment to their physical health.\par \par \uc2 A physician who tells a patient they are suffering a life-threatening pregnancy complication "and in the same breath states 'but the law won't allow me to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in these circumstances' is simply wrong," Justice Jane Bland {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prod-i.a.dj.com/public/resources/documents/TexasSupremeCourt.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote for the court }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision is a blow to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups, which have staked their legal strategy in deep-red states on arguments that medical exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are insufficient and endangering women's lives. The Texas case was a novel legal challenge filed by women who had themselves suffered complications, along with physicians. It drew widespread national attention because the plaintiffs told harrowing stories about their experiences, including one whose child was born gasping for breath and died within hours.\par \par \uc2 "This ruling does not provide any real clarity to doctors, and it is deeply offensive to the women we represent," said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "As women are finding out across the country, exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are illusory\u8212\'20\'14they're an empty promise."\par \par \uc2 Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  said on X, "I will continue to defend the laws enacted by the Legislature and uphold the values of the people of Texas by doing everything in my power to protect mothers and babies."\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-women-denied-abortions-testify-about-impact-of-state-bans-999b8cff"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 At trial last year }}}\uc2 , the lead plaintiff, Amanda Zurawski, testified that she suffered a premature rupture of membranes that meant her unborn daughter wouldn't survive but that doctors delayed providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until she ultimately went into septic shock and spent three days in intensive care. She has since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/the-tragic-pregnancy-stories-filling-the-abortion-campaign-airwaves-74c4fa41"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appeared in an ad for President Biden's re-election campaign.  }}}\par \par \uc2 An Austin trial judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-judge-blocks-state-abortion-ban-against-women-with-health-risks-d139a7c0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quickly ruled for the women }}}\uc2 , saying physicians unsure of how to interpret the law had been withholding essential medical care. The state immediately appealed the ruling, which blocked it from remaining in effect.\par \par \uc2 In December, the separate case of Kate Cox drew even more attention to the issue when she sought permission while still pregnant to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of her fetus with a fatal abnormality. The Texas Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/texas-woman-who-sought-exception-to-abortion-ban-decides-to-leave-state-e99f16f5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 denied that request }}}\uc2  just hours after Cox said she would travel out of state for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Texas has three abortions bans on the books: A criminal law that was last revised in 1925 and two laws passed several years ago that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy and allow private plaintiffs to bring lawsuits against providers for abortions performed after about six weeks of pregnancy. All of those laws provide some type of exception for medical emergencies.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights litigants have had success in other states, including Oklahoma and North Dakota, in persuading courts to expand or clarify medical exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Texas' all-Republican high court said the legislature and the medical profession were better placed to clarify the scope of the medical exception than the courts.\par \par \uc2 "The Texas Legislature has enacted one of the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country. And whatever my personal views may be about how restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes in Texas 'should' be, it is the Legislature's prerogative to make that determination," Justice Debra Lehrmann wrote in a concurring opinion.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240531ek5v00565\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
